{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00053", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The presence of an unusual email address format (`106921878569@bolellilab.com`) combined with a reference to the \"IRS TAX RE-FUND\" is a classic tactic used in phishing attempts. The message immediately presents a document link (`PDF Document`) related to a sensitive topic (tax refund), which is designed to create urgency and prompt the recipient to click and potentially download malware or enter personal information. Legitimate government agencies like the IRS typically do not initiate contact via unsolicited SMS messages containing direct links to tax documents.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00055", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses a brand impersonation (\"navyfederal-EAlert\") to create a sense of legitimacy. It employs a high level of urgency (\"temporary locked Your DEBIT CARD\") to prompt immediate action. Most critically, it directs the user to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`https://nfcu01b-sign00.gipsitv.org`) to \"Confirm your details,\" which is a classic tactic for credential harvesting.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
