{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00155", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The content uses a sensational headline (\"Breaking: USA Says Goodbye to Dr Oz\") to grab attention, which is a common tactic in phishing. More critically, it contains a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`holdadorn.com/OK2ROKY`). This URL is likely designed to mimic a legitimate news or health site but is instead a malicious link intended to harvest personal information or install malware. There is no context to suggest this is a legitimate news alert from a trusted source.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00156", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message appears to be a casual, informal communication between two people who likely know each other, discussing a meeting or arrival time. There are no indicators of phishing, such as urgent demands, suspicious links, requests for personal information, or impersonation of a known entity. The language is conversational and relaxed.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00157", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency/Problem Creation:** It creates a sense of urgency by claiming a package delivery has failed due to incomplete address information, prompting immediate action.\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The URL provided, `https://usps.intertrck.com`, is highly suspicious. While it attempts to mimic USPS, the inclusion of \"intertrck\" suggests a third-party or potentially malicious domain rather than the official USPS tracking site.\n3. **Request for Action/Information:** It directs the user to click a link to \"confirm your address,\" which is a common tactic used by phishers to steal personal information.\n4. **Unusual Instructions:** The complex instructions (\"Please reply to 1, then exit theSMS, open the SMS activationlink again, or copy the link toSafari browser and open it\") are designed to confuse the user and ensure they interact with the malicious link.\n\nThe combination of impersonation (USPS), a fabricated problem, and a suspicious link requesting personal data strongly classifies this as smishing.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00158", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The message uses a high-profile brand name (\"Oprah's Free Summer Giveaway\") to lure the recipient, which is a form of brand impersonation. More critically, it contains a suspicious, shortened, and non-official-looking URL (`http://scienceb.associates/QN70Lb`). Legitimate giveaways from major figures like Oprah would typically direct users to official, well-known websites, not obscure domains. The promise of a \"Free Giveaway\" creates a sense of opportunity/urgency, a common tactic in phishing.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00159", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It uses brand impersonation (USPS) to create a sense of legitimacy. It introduces a problem (undelivered shipment due to missing information) and creates a subtle sense of urgency/potential penalty (\"A redelivery fee may apply\"). Most critically, it directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`uspspostal-eb.info`), which is a common tactic in phishing to harvest personal information or payment details.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00161", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It impersonates a well-known brand (UPS) to create a sense of urgency or concern regarding a package delivery (\"On Hold\"). Crucially, it requests the recipient to confirm personal information (zip code) via a shortened, suspicious link (`https://is.gd/Ybkgm2`). Legitimate companies rarely ask for sensitive information like a zip code through an unsolicited link in an SMS notification.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00162", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message contains a suspicious link (`meF92.COM/xMvLZnnJvP`) presented as a \"tiny gift.\" While the initial part mentions an invoice (which could be legitimate), the unsolicited and unusual nature of the link, especially when tied to a vague \"gift,\" is a common tactic used in smishing to trick recipients into clicking malicious URLs. The domain name itself appears random and not associated with a known, legitimate service.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00164", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It uses brand impersonation (\"BofA-EDD ALERT\" and \"BOA EDD Prepaid Card\") to appear legitimate. It creates a sense of urgency (\"reactivate your card now\") by claiming the card has been disabled for security reasons. Most critically, it directs the user to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`https://user-bo2faedd.com/edd`), which is a common tactic in phishing attacks to steal credentials or personal information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00165", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several characteristics of smishing. It uses persuasive language (\"powerful natural relief,\" \"Proven to reduce pain, aches, anxiety and stress\") to promote a product, which is a common tactic in unsolicited marketing or scams. Most critically, it contains a suspicious, shortened URL (`naturalcbdcoffee.xyz/1X0g3jAK5n`). The domain name structure and the use of a URL shortener are red flags, as they obscure the true destination of the link, which is often where phishing attempts lead to steal personal or financial information. While it includes an opt-out mechanism (a benign feature), the primary driver is the unsolicited sales pitch combined with the suspicious link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00166", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message contains highly personal and emotional content, suggesting a private communication between individuals, likely related to a breakup or moving situation. There are no indicators of typical smishing tactics such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The language is conversational and context-specific to a personal relationship.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00167", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It impersonates a legitimate entity (USPS) and creates a sense of urgency/problem (\"could not be delivered due to incomplete address information\"). Crucially, it directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-official-looking link (`usps.track-pkg.com`) to resolve the issue, which is a classic phishing tactic. The unusual instructions regarding replying 'Y' and then re-opening the message further suggest an attempt to trick the user into interacting with a malicious link or process.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00168", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It uses a classic \"prize/sweepstakes\" lure, which is a common social engineering tactic. The message creates a sense of urgency (\"Hurry up to pick up the prize within a day!\"). Most critically, it contains a suspicious, shortened URL (`groupm.è.to/wfELH?id=2f57943`) that is designed to trick the recipient into clicking, likely leading to a phishing site to steal personal information or payment details. The promise of a high-value item (Apple iPad Pro) is a strong incentive used in scams.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00169", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses a generic notification format (\"You have a new notification\") which is often used by phishing attempts to lure the recipient. Crucially, it contains a suspicious, shortened, and non-official-looking URL (`https://iaqwqqit.com/6ycyEm`). Legitimate companies, especially major brands like BMW, typically use their official domains for notifications, not random, obfuscated links. The inclusion of \"Reply STOP to optout\" is a common tactic in unsolicited marketing or phishing messages to make the communication appear more legitimate or to gather user data.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00170", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It uses brand impersonation by referencing \"Wells Fargo\" and an \"ALRT\" (alert). It creates a sense of urgency and alarm (\"Account locked due to unusual activity\"). Most critically, it directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`https://wfveri-info.com`) to resolve the supposed issue, which is a classic tactic used in phishing attacks to steal credentials or personal information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00171", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses an urgent and sensational tone (\"Huge Company is liable,\" \"9 more cases added,\" \"up to 1.4 millions\"). It attempts to create a sense of immediate importance or potential gain/loss, which is a common social engineering tactic. Most critically, it contains a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`http://checkthis1.info/g5L6DJmM2F`) that directs the recipient to an external site, likely to harvest credentials or personal information. The vague reference to a \"Huge Company\" without proper identification further suggests impersonation or a scam attempt.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00172", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several characteristics commonly associated with smishing, although it is framed as a confirmation/follow-up. The key suspicious cue is the inclusion of a shortened, non-standard tracking link (`https://vons.onelink.me/MuHG/s37kcrr2`) that directs the user to complete a sign-up process. While it impersonates a known brand (Vons), the immediate call to action via an external link to \"Finish signing up\" is a common tactic used in phishing to harvest credentials or install malware. The inclusion of a clear opt-out mechanism (\"Txt STOP 2 end\") is a legitimate feature, but it doesn't negate the suspicious nature of the preceding link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00173", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham. The language used is informal and conversational, using abbreviations and colloquialisms (\"ü\", \"wan 2\", \"lor\"). There are no indicators of phishing, such as urgent demands, suspicious links, requests for personal information, or brand impersonation. It appears to be a casual arrangement between known parties.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00174", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message contains informal language (\"thk,\" \"lor,\" \"dunno,\" \"Wat u\") and appears to be a casual conversation between acquaintances regarding a potential event or meeting (\"tickets\"). There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. Therefore, it is classified as legitimate communication.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00175", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message contains a suspicious, shortened URL (`gravitygeeks.com/?t9bPrQ2j4o`) and a direct call to action (\"Please click the link to join\") without any context, greeting, or explanation of what the user is supposed to be joining. This lack of context combined with the unsolicited link is a strong indicator of a phishing attempt designed to trick the recipient into clicking a malicious link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00176", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The primary suspicious cues are the unsolicited offer (\"Get a Free $100\") which creates an incentive, and the presence of a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`http://intentweav.shopping/nrFIB6`). Legitimate promotions from major retailers like Home Depot typically use their official domain names, not obscure, third-party links. The combination of an enticing offer and a suspicious link is a classic smishing tactic designed to trick users into clicking and potentially entering personal or financial information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00177", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message appears to be a personal update or confirmation regarding a meeting or legal proceeding, as it references an \"affidavit,\" a specific address, a division, a courtroom, and a time. There are no immediate indicators of phishing, such as urgent demands, suspicious links, requests for personal financial information, or impersonation of a known service/brand. The language is conversational and context-specific to a prior arrangement.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00178", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It uses high-pressure tactics (\"get $100 for free,\" \"in seconds,\" \"Permanent income is guaranteed and risk free!\") to create a sense of urgency and excitement, which is a common phishing technique. Furthermore, it directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`https://usdt777.vip`) to register, and it promises unrealistic, guaranteed high returns (\"$300-800 per day,\" \"Permanent income is guaranteed\"), which is characteristic of investment scams often delivered via smishing.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00179", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several characteristics of smishing. The use of a branded-sounding alert (\"Flex Alert!\") suggests impersonation of a utility or service provider. The message contains a link (`https://flexalert.link/fourtonine?id=66ZA`) which is a shortened or non-standard domain, a common tactic in phishing to hide the true destination. While the content itself is about energy conservation (a benign topic), the delivery mechanism—an unsolicited alert with a suspicious link—is highly indicative of a phishing attempt designed to trick the recipient into clicking.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00180", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message is classified as ham because it is a casual, conversational update about a personal encounter. There are no indicators of phishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00183", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message consists only of a random string of characters followed by a domain name (`juytrplmwsaqz.us`). There is no accompanying text, greeting, or context. The presence of a completely random, non-descriptive URL is a significant red flag. This structure is characteristic of phishing attempts, where the goal is to trick the recipient into clicking a suspicious link, often disguised as a legitimate service. The lack of any legitimate context or recognizable sender information strongly suggests malicious intent.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00184", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message contains a short, cryptic identifier (\"1-1/ CID: CO4125\") followed by a suspicious, non-standard URL (`https:http://3260518391/co.php`). The URL structure is highly irregular, uses a numerical string as the domain, and lacks any recognizable branding or context. This combination of an unsolicited, vague message and a suspicious link is a strong indicator of a phishing attempt designed to trick the recipient into clicking.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00185", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It impersonates a legitimate entity (\"USPostal\") and creates a sense of urgency/problem (\"Your package delivery has been paused\"). Most critically, it includes a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`uspkgos.homes/D4d5t6`) which is designed to trick the recipient into clicking and potentially entering personal information or payment details.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00186", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Unsolicited Offer/Urgency:** It presents an unexpected, high-value financial opportunity (\"$2400 every week\") tied to a government program, which is a common lure in phishing attempts.\n2. **Vague/Impersonal Language:** The language is overly promotional and uses vague terms like \"Act Plan\" and \"Taxes Refund Benefit Programe.\"\n3. **Suspicious Link:** The link provided (`https://forms.office.com/r/Oc71GAVPIJ`) directs to a Microsoft Forms survey, which is frequently used by scammers to collect personal information under the guise of official applications.\n4. **Government Impersonation:** It falsely claims to be from the \"federal Government,\" a classic tactic to gain trust and prompt action.\n\nThere are no benign cues present; the entire message is designed to trick the recipient into clicking the link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00187", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is a very short, casual question (\"You still at grand prix?\"). It lacks any typical indicators of smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, requests for personal information, or brand impersonation. It reads like a message between friends or acquaintances discussing an event.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00188", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several classic indicators of smishing. It impersonates a legitimate entity, the U.S. Postal Service, which is a common tactic in phishing. The message creates a sense of urgency or problem (\"your package could not be delivered\") to prompt immediate action. Most critically, it directs the recipient to a shortened, suspicious link (`https://bit.ly/3NR5x9f`) rather than an official USPS domain, which is designed to trick users into clicking and potentially entering personal or payment information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00189", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message contains several strong indicators of smishing. It starts with a seemingly innocuous, personal-sounding query (\"Hi, is this Dr. Janet's number?\"), which is a common tactic to establish a false sense of familiarity or to test if the recipient is engaged before delivering the malicious payload. The second part of the message abruptly switches to a promotional offer from \"Distinguished coinbase wallet users.\" This sudden shift, combined with the promise of large, unsolicited financial rewards (\"more than 500-5000$ subsidy liquidity DFI mining income\"), is a classic lure used in phishing scams. Most critically, it includes a suspicious, shortened link (`https://go.cb-w.com/gz3J1Hsfjpb`) that directs the user to an external site, likely designed to steal credentials or install malware. The combination of unsolicited financial incentives and a suspicious link points directly to a phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00190", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several cues that suggest it is not a typical smishing attempt, but rather a personal or social outreach. The tone is casual (\"hi there, it's friday!\"), and the content is a social invitation (\"wondering if we could meet sometime\"). While it includes a shortened link, the context is conversational rather than demanding immediate action, payment, or sensitive personal information, which are hallmarks of smishing. The link itself is generic and doesn't immediately suggest a known brand impersonation.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00191", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing.\n1. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates the Internal Revenue Service (IRS), a highly trusted government entity, to gain the recipient's confidence.\n2. **Urgency/Incentive:** It uses the lure of a \"tax refund\" to prompt immediate action.\n3. **Request for Personal Information:** It demands the recipient \"confirm your information\" via a link, which is a classic tactic to steal sensitive data.\n4. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL, `https://irs.gov.safe-paying.com`, is highly suspicious. While it contains \"irs.gov,\" the addition of `.safe-paying.com` makes it a deceptive domain designed to look legitimate but is not the official IRS website.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
