{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00201", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. Specifically, it uses a generic, official-sounding notification (\"POST-OFFICE No one was in to sign for...\") to create a sense of urgency or necessity. More critically, it directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-standard URL (\"Rebook.Collection-35.com\") to resolve the issue. Legitimate postal services typically use their official, well-known domain names for such actions, not a seemingly random or newly created subdomain/domain combination. The request is to click a link to \"rebook,\" which is a common tactic used in phishing to harvest credentials or install malware.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
