{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00245", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It creates a sense of urgency (\"needs to be updated\") regarding a package delivery, which is a common tactic in phishing. More critically, it contains a suspicious, shortened URL (`l.ead.me/Vg8TrwQG`) that is designed to trick the recipient into clicking and potentially entering personal or financial information. The context of a package delivery is a frequent lure used by scammers.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00246", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. It is an unsolicited advertisement for a rental property, which is a common tactic used in phishing attempts to lure victims. The link provided, `state.goo.gl/tap-`, is a shortened URL, which obscures the true destination and is a frequent indicator of malicious links. Furthermore, the message is vague (\"House available nearby\") and lacks any verifiable sender information or context, creating an element of urgency/opportunity that prompts the recipient to click the link immediately.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00247", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message contains informal language, slang (\"high,\" \":V\"), and references a shared, potentially illicit or highly casual past experience (\"got somebody high for the first time with diesel\"). There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The content suggests a private, casual conversation between acquaintances.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00248", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is very short and lacks any clear context, sender identification, or specific request. It appears to be a fragment of a casual conversation (\"Which is weird because I know I had it at one point\"). There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. Therefore, it is classified as legitimate conversation.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00250", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message \"Give one miss from that number please\" is very brief and lacks any clear context, urgency, or request for personal information or payment. It is highly ambiguous. However, the phrasing is informal and sounds like a casual request between people who know each other, possibly related to a shared activity or a misunderstanding of slang. There are no immediate red flags associated with typical smishing tactics (like urgent threats, suspicious URLs, or requests for login credentials). Therefore, based on the available text, it leans towards being a benign, albeit cryptic, message.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00251", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The sender impersonates the Internal Revenue Service (IRS), a common tactic in phishing. It creates a sense of urgency and incentive (\"You will receive a tax refund of $573.00\"). Crucially, it directs the recipient to a suspicious link (`https://irs.gov.direct-paying.com`). While the link attempts to mimic the official IRS domain, the addition of \"direct-paying.com\" makes it highly suspicious and not the official IRS website. Furthermore, it requests the user to \"confirm your information,\" which is a request for sensitive personal data.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00252", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency/Problem Creation:** It creates a sense of urgency by claiming a package delivery failure due to incomplete address information.\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The URL provided, `https://usps.trckingstamp.com`, is not the official USPS domain (which would be usps.com). The misspelling (\"trckingstamp\" instead of tracking) is a common tactic in phishing.\n3. **Request for Action/Information:** It directs the user to click a link to \"confirm your address,\" which is a common method for harvesting personal data.\n4. **Unusual Instructions:** The complex instructions (\"Please reply to 1, then exit theSMS, open the SMS activationlink again, or copy the link toSafari browser and open it\") are designed to confuse the user and ensure they interact with the malicious link.\n\nThese elements strongly suggest an attempt to trick the recipient into providing personal information or clicking a malicious site.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00253", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses an unsolicited, high-pressure sales pitch (\"Alert: 372-Month 4br/2bth home available near you no credit check\"), which is a common tactic in phishing to entice clicks. Crucially, it contains a suspicious, shortened URL (`http://5ot.short.gy/yQLk4sUm2dKc`). Shortened links are frequently used in phishing attempts to obscure the true destination of the link, making it difficult for the recipient to judge its legitimacy before clicking. Furthermore, the offer itself—a seemingly too-good-to-be-true housing deal—is a classic lure used in scams.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00255", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message contains no suspicious cues typically associated with smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. Instead, it is a sentimental, conversational message expressing a philosophical thought and ending with a casual greeting (\"Gud Ni8;-)\"). This aligns with the characteristics of a legitimate, non-malicious message.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00256", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The primary red flag is the URL: `https://irs.gov.direct-paying.com`. While it attempts to mimic the official IRS domain (`irs.gov`), the addition of `.direct-paying.com` makes it a clear phishing attempt designed to look legitimate but redirect to a malicious site. Furthermore, the message implies an action is required (\"Please reply 'y', then exit the SMS, open the SMS activation link again, or copy the link and open it in Safari browser\"), which is a common tactic used in phishing to trick users into clicking or engaging with the malicious link. The inclusion of the IRS mission statement is a form of brand impersonation intended to build false trust.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00257", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham because it is a very casual, conversational message between what appears to be acquaintances or classmates. It asks a simple, non-urgent question about scheduling (\"when you and derek done with class?\"). There are no suspicious cues such as urgency, links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00258", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several suspicious cues indicative of smishing. The message uses a generic, non-personalized greeting (\"Dear 02055666420friend\"), which is a common tactic in phishing to cast a wide net. The content promotes a \"Bitcoin Trading Study Group,\" which is a common lure in financial scams. Most critically, it includes a shortened, suspicious link (`https://rebrand.ly/v7qj08h`). Shortened URLs are frequently used by malicious actors to hide the true destination of the link, often leading to credential harvesting or malware downloads. There is no context to suggest this is a legitimate communication from a known entity.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00260", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing.\n1. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates the Internal Revenue Service (IRS), a highly trusted government entity, to gain the recipient's confidence.\n2. **Urgency/Incentive:** It uses the lure of a \"tax refund\" to prompt immediate action.\n3. **Request for Personal Information/Action:** It demands the recipient \"confirm your information\" via a link, which is a classic phishing tactic.\n4. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL, `https://irs.gov.safe-paying.com`, is highly suspicious. While it contains \"irs.gov,\" the addition of `.safe-paying.com` makes it a deceptive domain designed to look legitimate but is not the official IRS website.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00262", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses brand impersonation (\"OZOW PAYMENT\" and \"SARS Annual Return\") to create a sense of legitimacy. It creates a false sense of urgency or necessity by stating a payment was sent and requires immediate action. Most critically, it contains a suspicious, shortened URL (`https://hper.in/OzowSarsPay`) which is designed to trick the recipient into clicking and entering sensitive financial information (linking a Credit/Cheque account). Requests for linking financial accounts are a classic tactic in phishing attacks.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00263", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message is classified as ham because it appears to be a casual, personal communication between acquaintances (\"chikku\") explaining a delay in replying due to a technical issue with the sender's phone. There are no suspicious cues such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The language is informal, consistent with a personal text exchange.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00264", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses an urgent tone (\"Hurry up!\"), promises a high-value reward (\"$$90 value\"), and directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`storag e.googleapis.com/jzoxubc/1fhg3lf`). Furthermore, it impersonates a known financial institution, Bank of America, which is a common tactic in phishing attacks to trick users into clicking malicious links or providing sensitive information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00265", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is a simple, informal greeting between two individuals (\"Jenny\" and \"Flynika\"). There are no suspicious cues such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The tone is conversational and benign.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00266", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message is classified as ham because it is a casual, conversational greeting and inquiry. It contains no elements of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment, which are typical indicators of smishing.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00267", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates the Internal Revenue Service (IRS), a common tactic used by scammers.\n2. **Urgency and Threat:** It creates a sense of urgency (\"immediately,\" \"avoid potential delays or penalties\") to pressure the recipient into acting without thinking.\n3. **Request for Personal Information:** It explicitly asks the recipient to provide \"accurate personal information.\"\n4. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL, `https://irs.gov.direct-capitals.com`, is highly suspicious. While it contains \"irs.gov,\" the domain structure is clearly not the official IRS website and is designed to look legitimate (a technique called typosquatting or domain spoofing).\n\nThese elements strongly suggest a phishing attempt designed to steal personal data.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00268", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. Specifically, it uses an unsolicited, vague advertisement (\"2069-Ft² House Just Listed\") which is a common tactic in phishing to lure recipients. More critically, it contains a shortened, suspicious URL (`Xeo6.me/8ek26N`). Legitimate real estate listings typically direct users to established, recognizable real estate websites, not obscure, shortened domains. The message is designed to create interest and prompt an immediate click to view the listing, which is the primary goal of smishing attacks.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00270", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several classic signs of smishing. It creates a sense of urgency (\"within the next 24 hours,\" \"permanently limit your account\") to pressure the recipient into acting quickly without thinking. It impersonates a known brand, PayPal, which is a common tactic. Most critically, it directs the user to a suspicious, non-official-looking link (`https://fix-account.ffm.to/limited`) under the guise of needing to \"provide your information.\" Legitimate companies rarely demand sensitive information via unsolicited links in SMS messages.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00271", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency:** The message imposes a strict 12-hour deadline (\"within 12 hours\").\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL (`https://matop.xyz/nxa1TK`) is not a legitimate USPS domain and appears to be a generic, suspicious shortener/phishing domain.\n3. **Request for Action/Information:** It pressures the recipient to click a link to \"confirm your address,\" which is a common tactic to steal personal information.\n4. **Unusual Instructions:** The complex instructions (\"Please reply to Y, then exit the SMS, open the SMS activation link again, or copy the link to Safari browser and open it\") are designed to confuse the user and ensure they interact with the malicious link.\n\nThese elements strongly suggest an attempt at phishing rather than a legitimate communication from USPS.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00273", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message contains several suspicious cues. The language is fragmented and unprofessional (\"aid, button chairman click to ajoin\"), which is often characteristic of phishing attempts. More significantly, it includes a direct link formatted as a WhatsApp chat invitation (`whatsapp://chat/?code=...`). While WhatsApp links themselves aren't inherently malicious, when combined with vague, urgent, or unusual instructions (like \"aid, button chairman click to ajoin\"), they are frequently used in smishing campaigns to direct users to a compromised or malicious chat environment. The lack of any clear context or legitimate sender identification strongly suggests a deceptive attempt.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00274", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several characteristics that suggest it is not a typical smishing attempt, but rather a personal, informal conversation. There are no suspicious links, urgent demands for personal information, requests for payment, or clear brand impersonation. The language is informal (\"r,\" \"waxsto,\" \"til\"), which is common in personal texting. The content revolves around a personal situation (arranging medical insurance for someone) and a timeline for follow-up, which is consistent with a private conversation.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00275", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several characteristics often associated with social engineering, although it is framed as a personal emergency. The key suspicious cues are:\n1. **Urgency:** The use of \"its urgent\" is a common tactic to pressure the recipient into acting quickly without critical thought.\n2. **Change of Contact Method/Number:** The sender claims their primary phone is broken and directs the recipient to a new, unverified number/platform (WhatsApp). This is a classic setup for account takeover or further scams.\n3. **Vagueness:** While it appears personal, the sudden, urgent need to switch communication channels is a red flag.\n\nHowever, the message is directed to a known contact (\"Hey mum\") and relates to a plausible, albeit stressful, personal situation (broken phone). Unlike typical smishing, which often involves unsolicited links or financial demands from unknown entities, this is a form of *impersonation* or *social engineering* targeting a known relationship. Because it lacks the typical hallmarks of a mass-market phishing attempt (like suspicious links or requests for bank details), it leans more towards a personal scam attempt rather than a standard, automated smishing attack. Given the context of a personal emergency, it is difficult to definitively label it as malicious smishing without further context, but the urgency and sudden change of contact are highly suspicious.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00276", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The message uses an urgent tone (\"TODAY!\") regarding a package delivery, which is a common tactic in phishing attempts. More critically, it contains a suspicious, shortened URL (`http://www.webpage.ren/b/lc/? bfX.08&08z-mkq`). The domain structure appears non-standard and is designed to obscure the true destination, which is a major red flag for phishing. Furthermore, while it doesn't explicitly ask for payment or personal info in the visible text, the link itself is designed to trick the recipient into clicking and potentially entering such information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00277", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The language is highly alarming (\"Your Phone has been compromised!\"), creating a sense of urgency designed to panic the recipient into acting without thinking. Furthermore, it contains a suspicious, shortened URL (`pbizzz.com/J44rCi`), which is a common tactic used in phishing attacks to direct users to malicious websites. The message demands immediate action (clicking the link) to resolve a fabricated security issue.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00278", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several characteristics of smishing. It is an unsolicited advertisement promoting a product (\"Triple power moisturizer and firming gel\") and includes a suspicious, shortened URL (`soliditycheek.com/QCq620`). This type of unsolicited commercial message, especially when directing users to an external, potentially unknown link to view a product or claim a deal, is a common tactic used in phishing attempts to trick users into clicking malicious links. The lack of any recognizable brand name or context further suggests it is a mass marketing or deceptive message rather than a legitimate communication.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00280", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message contains highly informal language, emotional appeals (\"The thought alone mite break me\"), and vague, personal content. There are no indicators of smishing, such as urgent requests for personal information, suspicious links, or brand impersonation. The message reads like a personal, albeit somewhat dramatic, text exchange.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00281", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The message demands an immediate action (\"within the next 30 mins\"), creating a sense of urgency. Furthermore, it includes a seemingly random, non-standard string of characters (\"JFOQLtyexga\") which likely functions as a shortened or obfuscated link, a common tactic in phishing attempts. The request to enter a specific code into a messaging app (LINE) is a common social engineering technique used to trick users into revealing sensitive information or authorizing actions. There is no clear, legitimate context provided for this request.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
