{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00283", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency/Problem Creation:** It creates a sense of urgency by claiming a package delivery failure due to incomplete address information.\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL, `https://uspspgcm.com`, is not the official USPS domain (which is typically usps.com). The domain name is designed to look similar but is clearly fraudulent.\n3. **Request for Action/Information:** It directs the recipient to click a link to \"confirm your address,\" which is a common tactic to steal personal information.\n4. **Unusual Instructions:** The complex instructions (\"Please reply to 1, then exit the SMS, open the SMS activation link again, or copy the link to Safari browser and open it\") are designed to confuse the user and guide them toward the malicious link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00284", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency and Threat:** The message creates a sense of immediate crisis (\"Your Amazon Account has been locked,\" \"Please take action on your account within 48 hours to avoid permanent suspension\").\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL (`http://xn--80aaahorunoimsf3p.xn-p1ai/authcus/`) is heavily obfuscated using Punycode, which is a common tactic used by phishers to disguise malicious domains. It does not look like a legitimate Amazon domain.\n3. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates a well-known brand (Amazon) to trick the recipient into clicking the link.\n4. **Request for Action/Credentials:** The message demands immediate action via a link, which is typically used to steal login credentials or personal information.\n\nThese elements strongly suggest a phishing attempt rather than a legitimate communication from Amazon.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00285", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is very brief and lacks any typical indicators of smishing, such as urgent demands, suspicious links, requests for personal information, or brand impersonation. It reads like a casual, personal communication between two people arranging a meeting. The context is entirely absent, but based solely on the content, it does not exhibit the characteristics of a phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00286", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several classic indicators of smishing. The use of an urgent tone (\"expedited courier document that could not be delivered\") is a common tactic to prompt immediate action without critical thought. Furthermore, the inclusion of a shortened, non-descript link (`https://rebrand.ly/tw0ajjr`) is highly suspicious, as legitimate courier services usually direct users to their official, recognizable domains. The message implies a problem requiring the recipient to click the link to resolve, which is a typical phishing lure designed to steal information or install malware.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00287", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several characteristics commonly associated with smishing or job scams. The message uses vague language (\"Salary upto <>\"), lacks specific company details beyond the general name \"HCL chennai,\" and directs the recipient to an external, potentially suspicious website (\"Indyarocks.com\") for an interview process. The combination of an unsolicited job offer, a vague salary, and a request to engage via an external link/call for an interview is a common tactic used in phishing/scamming attempts to harvest personal information or trick users into clicking malicious links.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00288", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message appears to be a personal update or a friendly communication between acquaintances. It contains no elements typically associated with smishing, such as urgent demands, suspicious links, requests for financial information, or impersonation of a known entity for malicious purposes. The tone is conversational and benign.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00289", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several characteristics of smishing. It uses an enticing offer (\"comprehensive guidance,\" \"20%-35% monthly earnings!\") to lure the recipient. It promotes a financial opportunity related to cryptocurrencies, which is a common theme in investment scams. The call to action directs the user to join a WhatsApp group via a provided link, which is a common tactic used by scammers to move victims to a less traceable platform. The promise of high, guaranteed returns is a major red flag.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00290", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message is classified as ham because it is an informal, conversational message sharing a personal anecdote about a video game experience (\"Dude avatar 3d was imp. At one point i thought there were actually flies in the room and almost tried hittng one as a reflex\"). It contains no suspicious elements such as urgency, links, requests for personal information, or brand impersonation typically associated with smishing attacks.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00291", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message claims an account has been locked due to \"unusual transactions,\" which creates a sense of urgency designed to prompt immediate action. More critically, the link provided, `https://institutohaquira.edu.pe/schwab/`, is highly suspicious. It attempts to impersonate Charles Schwab by embedding their name within a domain belonging to an educational institution (`institutohaquira.edu.pe`), which is a common tactic in phishing to appear legitimate while directing the user to a malicious or compromised site. The combination of an urgent security alert and a suspicious, non-official link strongly suggests a phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00292", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing.\n1. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates a legitimate financial institution, \"CITI.\"\n2. **Urgency/Threat:** It creates a sense of urgency and potential loss by stating the debit card is \"locked.\"\n3. **Suspicious Link:** It directs the user to a shortened, non-official link (`bit.ly/89BNsg81289`) to resolve the issue, which is a common tactic in phishing attacks to capture credentials or install malware.\n4. **Request for Action:** It demands immediate action (\"at earliest convenience\") via the provided link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00293", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham. The message is a very casual, friendly greeting (\"Gud mrng dear have a nice day..hope u r fine�\"). It contains no urgent requests, suspicious links, attempts at brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The language is informal, which is common in personal or casual communication.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00294", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses an unusual sender ID format (\"NFLXID#\"), which suggests impersonation of Netflix (NFLX). It creates a sense of urgency (\"Renew_your_bill _now!!\") and contains a suspicious, shortened URL (`//onx.la/00cfc`) designed to trick the recipient into clicking and potentially entering sensitive information or payment details. The mix of English and Spanish (\"O A Mensaje de texto ? A *Pay ?\") is also a common tactic in phishing attempts to cast a wider net.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00296", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham. The content is a casual, personal, and somewhat flirtatious message (\"How will I creep on you now? ;_;\"). It contains no elements typically associated with smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. It appears to be part of an ordinary, informal conversation.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00297", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It uses brand impersonation (CVS) to appear legitimate. It creates a sense of urgency (\"Reward ends in 2 hours\") to pressure the recipient into acting quickly. Most critically, it contains a suspicious, shortened, and non-official-looking URL (`kztpr.cc/toDJoir`) which is a common tactic used in phishing attacks to trick users into entering personal information or downloading malware. The promise of a \"prize\" for a \"quick survey\" is a classic lure used in these scams.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00299", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The sender impersonates the Internal Revenue Service (IRS), a common tactic in phishing attacks. It creates a sense of urgency and incentive (\"You will receive a tax refund of $573.00\"). Most critically, it directs the recipient to a suspicious link: `https://irs.gov.direct-capitals.com`. While it attempts to mimic the official IRS domain (`irs.gov`), the addition of `.direct-capitals.com` makes it a clear spoofed or malicious domain designed to harvest personal information. The request to \"confirm your information\" is a direct solicitation for sensitive data.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00301", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The sender impersonates the Internal Revenue Service (IRS), a common tactic in phishing. It creates a sense of urgency and incentive (\"You will receive a tax refund... but cannot process it\"). Most critically, it directs the recipient to a suspicious link (`https://irs.gov.tax-helping.com`). While the link attempts to mimic the official IRS domain, the addition of \"tax-helping.com\" makes it a clear spoofed or malicious domain, designed to harvest personal information. The inclusion of the IRS mission statement is a form of social engineering to build false trust.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00302", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses a generic, seemingly random string of characters (\"YUO7RBOV15142461 58964\") followed by a brand impersonation (\"AMZN: Fraud Alert\"). The core of the message is a \"Fraud Alert\" combined with a request for a \"Support Request,\" which creates a sense of urgency and fear—a classic social engineering tactic. Most critically, it contains a shortened, suspicious link (`https://bit.ly/42GN8U0`) that directs the user to an external site, likely designed to harvest credentials or personal information under the guise of resolving the supposed fraud.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00303", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several suspicious cues indicative of smishing. The message uses vague, overly enthusiastic language (\"deeply express the deepest care\") which is often a tactic in phishing attempts. More critically, it contains a suspicious, shortened URL (`yZCD3.INFO/Goxmx1m3VG`). Legitimate companies rarely use such cryptic, non-standard links for important reminders or offers. The message also attempts to leverage a positive sentiment (\"exceptional payment history\") to encourage a click, which is a common social engineering technique.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00305", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The use of an unsolicited \"randomly given\" reward is a common lure tactic. The message creates a sense of urgency (\"Reward ends in 5 hours\") to pressure the recipient into acting quickly without thinking. Most critically, it contains a suspicious, shortened URL (`vnnim.cc/mNGtaOl`) which is characteristic of phishing attempts designed to direct users to a malicious site. Furthermore, the message is impersonating a known brand (Walgreens) to gain trust.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00306", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message appears to be a casual, conversational text exchange between friends making plans. There are no indicators of phishing, such as urgent demands, suspicious links, requests for personal financial information, or impersonation of a known entity. The language is informal and relates to social plans.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00307", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency/Threat:** The message creates a sense of urgency by stating access is \"Limited\" and demanding action \"within 24 hours\" to prevent account loss.\n2. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates a well-known brand, Amazon, to gain the recipient's trust.\n3. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL (`http://www.bizzapp.co.uk/free/apps51/service?=61513`) is highly suspicious. It does not match the official Amazon domain (amazon.com or similar) and uses a generic, unrelated domain structure, which is typical of phishing sites.\n4. **Request for Action:** It directs the user to click a link to \"verify your account,\" which is a common tactic used by phishers to steal login credentials.\n\nThe combination of brand impersonation, urgency, and a non-official, suspicious link strongly classifies this as smishing.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00308", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several characteristics commonly associated with smishing, although it is not overtly malicious in the way a typical financial scam is. The primary suspicious cue is the use of a shortened, non-descriptive URL (`http://tinyurl.com/u8bvs55`). While the offer itself (free tickets) is enticing, the unsolicited nature of the message combined with the generic link is a common tactic used in phishing or spam campaigns to drive clicks. The inclusion of \"INVITATIONS ONLY\" adds a layer of manufactured exclusivity, which can be a social engineering tactic.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00310", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message contains highly informal language, slang (\"rupaul show,\" \"TOOL\"), and references to personal social media activity (\"your fb page\"). There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The tone is conversational and appears to be a personal, albeit somewhat aggressive or teasing, message between acquaintances.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00311", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing.\n1. **Urgency/Alarm:** It immediately alerts the recipient to a problem (\"Your card... has been temporarily blocked\"), creating a sense of urgency to click and resolve the issue.\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The URL provided (`https://unblockbofaeddprepaid.meeha.com.bd/asdfghjkl.html`) is highly suspicious. It does not appear to be an official Bank of America (BOFA) domain. The use of a generic, non-official domain structure is a classic phishing tactic.\n3. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates a legitimate financial institution (BOFA), which is a common tactic in phishing attacks.\n4. **Request for Action:** It directs the user to click a link to \"Unblock card,\" which is the mechanism used to steal credentials or personal information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00312", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It impersonates a legitimate entity (USPS) to create a sense of urgency regarding a package delivery issue. The request is to \"confirm your address\" via a provided link, which is a classic tactic for harvesting personal information. Furthermore, the URL, `https://usps.trackquick-pk.shop`, is highly suspicious. While it contains \"usps,\" the domain structure (`trackquick-pk.shop`) is not the official USPS domain and appears to be a hastily created, non-official phishing site. The unusual instructions (\"Please reply Y, then exit the text message and open it again to activate the link\") are also designed to confuse the recipient and manipulate them into clicking the malicious link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00313", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message appears to be a casual, personal greeting between two individuals (\"Mary\" and \"Alisa\"). There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The language is informal, consistent with a personal text exchange.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00314", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message contains several strong indicators of smishing. Specifically, it uses a sense of urgency (\"account will be disabled in 24 hours\") to pressure the recipient into immediate action. It also includes a suspicious, shortened link (`Inkd.in/etiYT6vd`) which is a common tactic in phishing attempts to direct users to malicious sites. Furthermore, it impersonates a bank (\"RBank\") and references a security breach, which is a classic social engineering technique used in phishing to create fear and prompt compliance.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00315", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham. The message is a casual, conversational inquiry (\"Today is 'song dedicated day..' Which song will u dedicate for me?\"). It lacks any of the typical indicators of smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, requests for personal information, or financial demands. It appears to be a friendly, informal message between acquaintances.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00316", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The sender uses a generic, official-sounding prefix (\"IRS-STATEMENT-CLAIM\") which is a common tactic for impersonating government agencies. The message itself is not a full text but a direct link/attachment notification for a PDF document. Government agencies like the IRS typically do not initiate contact via unsolicited SMS with attachments demanding immediate action or containing sensitive documents in this manner. The implied urgency and the nature of the document (a \"statement claim\") strongly suggest a phishing attempt designed to trick the recipient into opening a malicious file or clicking a link that leads to a credential harvesting site.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00317", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message contains personal, emotional content (\"I've cried enough today,\" \"I don't want to talk about this stuff anymore tonight\"). It does not contain any typical indicators of smishing, such as urgent demands, suspicious links, requests for personal information, or brand impersonation. The tone suggests a private conversation between individuals.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00318", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing.\n1. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates the Internal Revenue Service (IRS), a common tactic in phishing attacks.\n2. **Urgency and Threat:** It creates a sense of urgency (\"immediately,\" \"avoid potential delays or penalties\") and implies a negative consequence if action isn't taken.\n3. **Request for Personal Information:** It explicitly asks the recipient to \"confirm your information\" via a link, which is a request for sensitive personal data.\n4. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL, `https://irs.gov.safe-ordering.com`, is highly suspicious. While it contains \"irs.gov,\" the addition of `.safe-ordering.com` suggests it is a deceptive domain designed to look legitimate but is not the official IRS website.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00320", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses an unusual, non-standard sender ID (\"NTLFIX.ID# 328054\") and contains a highly suspicious, shortened, and obfuscated URL (`unusa.id/csv8`). The language is urgent and directive (\"Your-SUBSCRIPTION-IS-ON\", \"Renewal-your-bill-now://\"), attempting to prompt immediate action regarding a supposed subscription or bill. The combination of urgency, a suspicious link, and vague billing/subscription claims are classic hallmarks of phishing attempts designed to trick the recipient into clicking a malicious link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
