{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00342", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It uses a sense of urgency (\"Your Citi Bank Online ID was locked up\") to prompt immediate action. It impersonates a legitimate institution (Citi Bank). Most critically, it directs the user to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`https://cberrorfix.me/3NX`) rather than the official Citi Bank website, which is a classic tactic for credential harvesting. The request is to \"update your informations,\" which is a request for sensitive personal data.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00343", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The congratulatory message about passing an interview, followed immediately by a specific daily salary offer, is a common tactic used in job scams. The inclusion of a direct link to a WhatsApp number (`https://wa.me/...`) is a method used to direct the recipient to a platform where the scammer can engage in further conversation, often to extract personal information or trick the user into paying fees. While the message itself doesn't contain a traditional malicious link (like a shortened URL leading to a phishing site), the combination of an unsolicited job offer and the immediate push to a private messaging platform for \"next steps\" is highly indicative of a recruitment scam, which falls under the umbrella of smishing/phishing attempts.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00344", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses a sense of urgency (\"Delivery Failed,\" \"or item will be return to warehouse\") to pressure the recipient into immediate action. It contains a suspicious, non-standard URL (`hfmu.axisxnor.com`) which is likely designed to mimic a legitimate delivery or service site but is not a recognizable, trusted domain. Furthermore, it requests the user to click a link to resolve an issue, which is a classic tactic used in phishing attacks to steal credentials or install malware.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00346", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It impersonates a known financial institution (\"CHASE\"), which is a common tactic in phishing. It creates a sense of urgency or requires immediate action by presenting a security alert (\"A new payee has been linked in your app\"). Most critically, it directs the recipient to a suspicious, shortened URL (`https://ab.gl/CMSbi`) to \"verify your information,\" which is the primary mechanism for credential harvesting in smishing attacks.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00347", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message contains a highly suspicious cue: the explicit offer to share \"kvb acc details\" (likely referring to bank account details). This is a direct request or indication of sharing sensitive personal and financial information, which is a hallmark of phishing or smishing attempts, even if the tone is casual (\"K:)\"). Legitimate communication would not typically involve casually offering bank account details via an unsolicited or casual SMS.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00349", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses unusual characters and substitutions (e.g., \"I#R#S/\", \"FED3R4L\", \"W4S_B4N\", \"1R'S\", \"he! p\", \"F!x\") which is a common tactic to bypass spam filters and appear less formal or legitimate. It creates a sense of urgency (\"by the 1R'S\") regarding a \"FEDERAL RETURN,\" which is a classic phishing lure. Most critically, it contains a suspicious, non-standard URL (`//sl.ut.ac.id/1spp`) that directs the recipient to an external site, likely to harvest personal information or payment details.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00350", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It uses a sense of urgency (\"Your Online Chase Bank Account has been blocked\") to prompt immediate action. It impersonates a legitimate financial institution (Chase Bank). Most critically, it directs the recipient to a suspicious, shortened, and non-official link (`https://e.vg/GJZPS`) for verification, which is a common tactic used by phishers to steal credentials or install malware.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
