{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00439", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message \"Go fool dont cheat others ok\" contains no discernible suspicious cues typically associated with smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. Instead, it appears to be a very informal, conversational, and somewhat admonishing message between people who likely know each other. The language is casual and lacks the hallmarks of a phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00440", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham. The content is a casual, conversational message sharing personal activities (\"Watching cartoon, listening music & at eve had to go temple & church.. What about u?\"). There are no indicators of smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00441", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing or social engineering attempts. The sender is making an unsolicited request for personal photos under the guise of an \"art project.\" While the message claims payment and bonuses, this is a common tactic used in scams to entice victims into sharing private content. The request itself is vague (\"one of your picture\") and lacks professional context or verifiable credentials for the \"client\" or the artist. The combination of an unsolicited request for personal images and the promise of payment is a strong indicator of a potential scam designed to harvest personal data or exploit the recipient.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00442", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message \"I'm done. C ü there.\" contains no suspicious cues. It is a very casual, informal message using abbreviations (\"C ü\" for \"See you\") and expresses a simple sentiment (\"I'm done\"). There is no urgency, no suspicious links, no brand impersonation, and no request for personal information or payment. It appears to be part of an ordinary, personal conversation.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00443", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency and Threat:** The message creates a sense of urgency (\"Submit the required documents immediately to avoid delays and penalties\") regarding a tax issue.\n2. **Request for Action via Link:** It directs the recipient to click a link to \"confirm your information.\"\n3. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL, `https://irs.gov.tax-helping.com`, is highly suspicious. While it attempts to mimic the IRS domain (`irs.gov`), the addition of `.tax-helping.com` makes it a clear phishing domain designed to look legitimate but is not the official IRS website.\n4. **Impersonation:** It impersonates a government agency (IRS) to solicit sensitive information or payment.\n\nThese elements strongly suggest an attempt to trick the user into providing personal or financial data.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00444", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message \"That's necessarily respectful\" is a very short, vague statement. It lacks any typical indicators of smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. It reads like a fragment of a normal, albeit somewhat formal, conversation. Therefore, it is classified as ham.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00445", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses brand impersonation (\"Ne'tf!ix\") with deliberate misspellings and character substitutions (e.g., '!' for 'i', '_' for 's') to evade spam filters. It creates a sense of urgency (\"Your Subscr!pt!ons has_expired today\") and directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-official-looking link (`//@risu.io/fTVXM`) to resolve the supposed issue, which is a classic tactic for phishing.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00446", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several characteristics of smishing. It uses a job offer (\"Now Hiring Part-Time Mystery Shoppers\") which is a common lure in phishing attempts. The high pay rate ($295) for a part-time role is an attention-grabbing, often unrealistic, incentive. Most critically, it includes a suspicious, unsolicited link (`https://frontiershopper.com/apply-now/`) that directs the user to an external site to \"Apply today,\" which is the primary mechanism for credential harvesting or malware delivery in smishing attacks. While it includes an opt-out text, the core content is a deceptive solicitation.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00447", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS contains a direct link to a WhatsApp group. While WhatsApp itself is a legitimate platform, unsolicited links, especially those shared without context or from unknown senders, are a common tactic used in smishing to lure users into malicious groups or phishing schemes. The message lacks any context, urgency, or explicit request for personal information, but the nature of an unsolicited, direct link is inherently suspicious in the context of unsolicited SMS.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00448", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message appears to be a casual conversation between individuals, likely related to a shared academic or work project. There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The language is informal, which is typical of personal texting.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00449", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It uses brand impersonation (\"BofA Alerts\") to appear legitimate. It creates a sense of urgency and concern (\"A Direct Deposit to your Account has been placed under review\"), pressuring the recipient to act quickly. Most critically, it directs the user to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`https://scre-b0llafca.live/login`), which is a classic tactic used in phishing attacks to steal credentials.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00450", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The initial statement, \"Your bill is paid for March,\" might be a pretext to establish a sense of legitimacy or relevance. However, the immediate follow-up with an unsolicited \"little gift\" and a shortened, suspicious URL (`consensuscelebrated.com/5hTSjydwTh`) is highly indicative of a phishing attempt. Legitimate companies rarely send unsolicited \"gifts\" via SMS that require clicking an unknown link. The URL itself is not associated with a known, trusted entity.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00451", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message exhibits several characteristics of spam or unsolicited commercial/social messaging, but it lacks the typical hallmarks of a targeted smishing attack (like urgent requests for login credentials, financial information, or suspicious links related to a known brand). The language is overly sentimental, vague, and uses informal internet slang (\"Somewhr,\" \"4 u,\" \"dat,\" \"ur\"), which is characteristic of mass-sent spam or potentially a low-effort romance/dating scam, rather than a sophisticated phishing attempt. Since it does not contain a clear malicious payload (like a link or request for sensitive data), it is classified as ham (unsolicited spam/junk) rather than smishing.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00453", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The primary suspicious cues are the unsolicited nature of the message, the urgent tone implied by \"Pending Delivery,\" and the presence of a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`holdingspothere.com`) used to request confirmation of personal information (shipment address). Legitimate delivery notifications usually direct users to well-known courier websites (like FedEx, UPS, USPS) rather than a generic, unfamiliar domain.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00454", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message contains casual, personal conversation, including emotional expression and anecdotal storytelling about a dream. There are no indicators of phishing, such as urgent requests, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00455", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message contains informal language and appears to be a casual, personal conversation rather than a solicitation or attempt to trick the recipient. There are no suspicious cues such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The language suggests a personal exchange between acquaintances.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00456", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several suspicious cues indicative of smishing. The message uses brand impersonation (\"Home Depot\") to appear legitimate. More critically, it contains a suspicious link (`http://arsenicbon.shopping/tFet5j`). The domain name \"arsenicbon.shopping\" is highly irregular and does not belong to Home Depot, suggesting a phishing attempt designed to trick the recipient into clicking and potentially entering personal or financial information. The lure of a \"Monthly GiveAway\" is a common tactic used in phishing scams to create a sense of opportunity and urgency.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00457", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message contains multiple links and a clear call to action related to tracking an order within a \"Shop app.\" While the message is promotional and directs the user to an app, the presence of multiple, slightly different links (one using a shortened URL, one using a direct domain) and the general nature of order tracking notifications are common in legitimate marketing or transactional SMS from e-commerce businesses. There is no immediate, high-pressure urgency, request for sensitive personal information (like passwords or credit card numbers), or obvious signs of a malicious phishing attempt (like a highly suspicious or misspelled domain). The inclusion of \"Reply STOP to unsubscribe\" is a standard feature of legitimate marketing SMS.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00458", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency/Problem Creation:** It creates a sense of urgency by claiming a package delivery failure due to incomplete address information.\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL, `https://uspbb.top`, is highly suspicious. It does not use the official USPS domain (`usps.com`) and uses a generic, non-official top-level domain (.top), which is a common tactic in phishing.\n3. **Request for Action/Information:** It directs the recipient to click a link to \"confirm your address,\" which is a common method for harvesting personal information (PII) or credentials.\n4. **Unusual Instructions:** The complex instructions (\"Please reply to 1, then exit the SMS, open the SMS activation link again, or copy the link to Safari browser and open it\") are designed to confuse the user and guide them into a specific, potentially malicious, sequence of actions.\n\nThe combination of a fake brand impersonation (USPS), a suspicious link, and a request to verify personal details strongly classifies this as smishing.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00459", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message appears to be a legitimate order confirmation from a retailer. It provides specific order details (Order #, total amount), an estimated delivery date, and offers a customer service contact number. The inclusion of \"Reply STOP to opt-out\" is a standard feature in legitimate marketing or transactional SMS messages. There are no immediate red flags such as urgent threats, requests for login credentials, or suspicious, shortened links.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00460", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message claims a bill has been paid, which is a common tactic to build trust or create a sense of resolution, but it immediately follows this with an unsolicited \"gift\" linked to a suspicious, non-standard URL (`sirreeprematurely.com`). The presence of an unexpected, shortened, and unusual link is the most significant red flag, as it directs the recipient to an external, potentially malicious site. There is no context to suggest this message is legitimate from a known service provider.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00461", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses a vague, urgent-sounding alert (\"Private Message Alert\") and directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-standard URL (`http://scoldrunner.services/up18iuL8`). The request to \"use this code to reveal vxma\" is a classic tactic used in phishing to trick users into clicking a link or entering credentials. The domain name itself appears randomly generated and unrelated to any legitimate service.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00462", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several characteristics of smishing. It uses a promotional hook (\"Customer Appreciation Week,\" \"Receive up to $75 in CVS Store Rewards\") to entice the recipient. More critically, it includes a suspicious, shortened URL (`comfortstale.com/elPdWW`) that is not the official CVS domain, which is a common tactic in phishing attempts to direct users to a malicious site. The combination of a reward offer and an external, non-official link strongly suggests an attempt to trick the user into clicking and potentially providing personal information or downloading malware.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00463", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message promises an unrealistic and high return (\"earn 5000+ B_T-C investment every day\"), which is a classic lure in investment scams. Furthermore, it includes a suspicious, shortened URL (`www.awqvfz.rip/16logt`) that is not associated with any known, legitimate financial institution, suggesting it leads to a phishing or malware site. The combination of high-reward promises and a suspicious link is highly characteristic of phishing attempts.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00464", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It uses brand impersonation by referencing \"JP-Morgan.\" It creates a sense of urgency and alarm (\"Alerts! Your Accounts are on hold due to unauthorized activity\"). Most critically, it directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (\"jpmorgan9webio.com/\"), which is a classic tactic used in phishing attacks to steal credentials or personal information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00465", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message is classified as ham because it appears to be a casual, personal conversation between two individuals. There are no indicators of phishing, such as urgent demands, suspicious links, requests for personal information, or brand impersonation. The language is informal and conversational.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00466", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses a brand name (\"Altura Credit Union\") to impersonate a legitimate institution, which is a common tactic in phishing. It creates a sense of urgency or importance by framing it as an \"Account Notification.\" Most critically, it directs the recipient to click a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`https://bacindustries.net/alco/Alturacu`) to \"Open Message,\" which is a classic phishing lure designed to steal credentials or install malware.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00467", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency:** The message imposes a strict 12-hour deadline (\"within 12 hours\").\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The URL provided, `https://usps-tracking-tols-oh.com`, is not the official USPS domain (which would be usps.com) and appears designed to mimic a legitimate tracking site.\n3. **Request for Action/Information:** It prompts the recipient to click a link to \"confirm your address,\" which is a common tactic to harvest personal information.\n4. **Unusual Instructions:** The complex instructions (\"Please reply to Y, then exit the SMS, open the SMS activation link again, or copy the link to Safari browser and open it\") are designed to confuse the user and force them into a specific, potentially malicious, sequence of actions.\n\nThese elements strongly suggest an attempt at phishing rather than a legitimate communication from USPS.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00468", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham because it is a casual, conversational message asking for an opinion about a person's character. It contains no suspicious cues such as urgency, links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00469", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing.\n1. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates the Internal Revenue Service (IRS), a common tactic in phishing attacks.\n2. **Urgency and Threat:** It creates a sense of urgency (\"immediately,\" \"avoid potential delays or penalties\") and implies a financial benefit (tax refund) contingent on immediate action.\n3. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL, `https://irs.gov.safe-ordering.com`, is highly suspicious. While it contains \"irs.gov,\" the actual domain is `safe-ordering.com`, not the official IRS domain, indicating a spoofed or malicious site designed to look legitimate.\n4. **Request for Personal Information:** It explicitly asks the recipient to \"confirm your information\" via the link, which is a direct request for sensitive personal data.\n\nThese elements—impersonation, urgency, and a suspicious link leading to a request for personal data—are hallmarks of smishing.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00470", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency and Threat:** The message creates a sense of urgency (\"expedite the processing,\" \"avoid potential delays or penalties\") to pressure the recipient into acting quickly without thinking.\n2. **Request for Personal Information:** It explicitly asks the recipient to \"provide accurate personal information.\"\n3. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL, `https://irs.gov.direct-capitals.com`, is highly suspicious. While it attempts to mimic the legitimate IRS domain (`irs.gov`), the addition of `.direct-capitals.com` makes it a clear phishing domain designed to look official but is not the real IRS website.\n4. **Unusual Instructions:** The complex instructions on how to activate the link (\"Please reply 'y', then exit the message, reopen the message to activate the link, or copy the link and open it in the Safari browser\") are designed to confuse the user and guide them toward clicking the malicious link.\n\nThese elements strongly suggest an attempt to trick the user into revealing sensitive data or installing malware.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00471", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message appears to be a casual, conversational message between people who know each other, likely related to a recent event or shared experience (like a sports event, given the mention of \"coach\"). There are no indicators of phishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The content is benign and informal.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00472", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham. The message is a casual, conversational text exchange between what appears to be friends or acquaintances. It discusses a personal event (\"my trip got postponed\") and asks a simple, context-dependent question (\"you still stocked up?\"). There are no indicators of smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for sensitive personal information or payment.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00473", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham. The message consists of very informal, fragmented, and colloquial language (\"I dunno they close oredi not... Ü v ma fan...\"). There are no indicators of smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The content appears to be a casual, conversational exchange.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00474", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several classic signs of smishing. The sender claims to be from \"US.Postal Msg,\" impersonating a legitimate postal service. The message creates a sense of urgency (\"Your package is on hold\") to prompt immediate action. Most critically, it includes a suspicious, shortened link (`https://s.id/1rAQj`) which is designed to trick the recipient into clicking and potentially entering personal or payment information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00475", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The initial statement, \"Your bill is paid for March,\" might be an attempt to establish a false sense of legitimacy or context, potentially impersonating a utility or service provider. The inclusion of a \"little gift\" is a common lure used in phishing attempts to entice the recipient into clicking. Most critically, the message contains a suspicious, shortened URL (`burngeezers.com/X1ryc1i9J9`). This URL is not associated with a known, legitimate service and is designed to direct the user to a malicious site, likely to steal credentials or install malware.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00477", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is very brief and informal. It appears to be a personal query or a fragment of a conversation (\"How much she payed. Suganya.\"). There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The language is casual, suggesting it is likely a message between known parties.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00478", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It uses urgent language (\"CIBC BANK ALERT!\", \"has been suspended\") to create a sense of panic, which is a common social engineering tactic. Furthermore, the link provided, `https://Cibc-security-department2023.com`, is highly suspicious. While it attempts to mimic the official CIBC branding, the domain name is not the official CIBC domain and includes extra, suspicious characters (\"-security-department2023\") designed to look legitimate but are characteristic of phishing attempts. The message also requests the user to \"Verify your account,\" which implies the need to enter sensitive information on the linked site.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00479", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several characteristics of a scam, specifically a romance or dating scam, which falls under the umbrella of smishing because it uses SMS to lure the recipient into an interaction that likely leads to fraud. Key suspicious cues include:\n\n1. **Unsolicited Contact:** The message is completely unsolicited and appears out of context.\n2. **Vague/Emotional Hook:** It uses flattering, yet vague, language about a \"good friend\" to create an emotional connection or sense of opportunity.\n3. **Urgency/Pressure:** While not overtly urgent, it implies a time constraint (\"Now it is up to marriage age\").\n4. **Call to Action with a Link:** It directs the recipient to click a link (a WhatsApp link in this case) to proceed, which is a common tactic to move the conversation off the secure SMS platform into a controlled environment where phishing or grooming can occur.\n5. **Request for Engagement:** It asks the user to reply with a specific number (\"Reply the number '1'\") to initiate the interaction.\n\nWhile it doesn't explicitly ask for financial details in the initial text, the entire setup is designed to trick the user into engaging with a fraudulent profile or service, making it a form of social engineering/smishing.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00480", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several characteristics of smishing. It uses a formal, automated-sounding header (\"FRM:Unit-VJKXU SUBJ:CS_ID: 106109122 MSG:\"), which is common in automated phishing attempts. The content creates a sense of urgency/problem (\"Your shipment 55511 could not be delivered\") and directs the recipient to click a shortened, suspicious link (`https://lnkd.in/eZXKcEdF`) to resolve the issue (reschedule). This structure—a notification of a problem followed by a link to \"fix\" it—is a classic smishing tactic designed to trick users into entering personal or payment information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00481", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message contains a highly suspicious, non-standard URL (`www.nibtt9-upse.us//vofxbnno224123#`) which is likely designed to mimic a legitimate service (like UPS, suggested by the \"UPS\" in the subject line) but is clearly a phishing domain. The structure, including cryptic codes (`FRM:KemH_-UPS-10esF`, `:3lq9DWaATI-a`), is typical of automated, malicious messages attempting to look official while being nonsensical to a casual reader. The combination of an urgent-sounding context (implied by the tracking/shipping format) and a highly suspicious link is a classic smishing tactic.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00482", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The initial statement, \"Your bill is paid for March,\" might seem benign, but it is immediately followed by an unsolicited \"gift\" link. The URL, `vocalsprolonged.com/ZSgingd6vi`, is highly suspicious. It uses a non-standard, potentially unrelated domain name (`vocalsprolonged.com`) and contains a random-looking string of characters, which is typical of phishing links designed to trick users into clicking. The combination of a seemingly positive notification followed by an unexpected, unsolicited link is a common social engineering tactic used in smishing attacks to lure users to a malicious site.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00483", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses sensational and alarming language (\"A Tragic End Today For Dr.OZ!\") to create a sense of urgency and pique curiosity, a common tactic in phishing. Furthermore, it directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-standard URL (`exposurepavemen.com/080p6L`) rather than a known, legitimate news source. This combination of sensationalism and a suspicious link strongly suggests an attempt to trick the user into clicking a malicious link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00484", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message \"Yup bathe liao...\" is very short, informal, and appears to be part of a casual, ongoing conversation. There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The language is colloquial (\"liao\" is often used in informal Southeast Asian English). Therefore, it is classified as legitimate communication.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00485", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses a common tactic of announcing a \"WINNER\" to entice the recipient into clicking a link. The link provided, `http://107.175.219.12/Depot.php`, is a suspicious, non-standard IP address rather than a legitimate, recognizable domain for Home Depot, which is a major red flag. This combination of unsolicited prize notification and a suspicious link strongly suggests a phishing attempt designed to steal credentials or personal information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00486", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message contains informal language, abbreviations (\"U,\" \"4u,\" \"i didn't,\" \"2\"), and emotional content, suggesting a personal or conversational tone. There are no indicators of smishing, such as urgent demands, suspicious links, requests for personal information, or brand impersonation. The content appears to be a personal message between individuals.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00488", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several characteristics that suggest it might be a form of social engineering, although it is not a classic smishing attempt involving a malicious link or direct request for sensitive data. The tone is informal and conversational, which can be a tactic to build rapport or lower the recipient's guard. The phrasing, \"Got a pleasant surprise when i checked my balance -u c, i don't get statements 4 that acc,\" is vague and slightly unusual, which could be an attempt to prompt a response or elicit information under the guise of a casual inquiry about a shared or known account. However, without a suspicious link, explicit urgency, or a direct request for login credentials or payment, it leans more towards a potentially benign, albeit odd, personal message or a very low-effort social engineering attempt rather than a clear-cut smishing attack. Given the lack of overt malicious indicators (like phishing links or urgent threats), it is classified as ham, though caution is advised.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00490", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing.\n1. **Unsolicited Job Offer/High Pay:** The message presents an unsolicited, high-paying job opportunity ($400 per assignment) that requires minimal effort (15-30 minutes of shopping). This is a common tactic in job scams.\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The link provided (`https://form.jotform.com/222355830979062`) directs to a generic form-building service (Jotform) rather than an official National Retail Federation career portal. Scammers frequently use these platforms to collect personal information.\n3. **Brand Impersonation:** The message impersonates a legitimate organization (National Retail Federation®) to lend credibility to the fraudulent offer.\n4. **Vague Requirements:** While it names major retailers, the nature of the \"survey\" is vague, suggesting a potential data harvesting or fraudulent payment scheme.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00491", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is very brief and lacks any clear context, sender identification, or specific request. It appears to be a fragment of a conversation or a very poorly constructed message. There are no obvious indicators of smishing, such as urgent demands, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The content is too vague to definitively classify as malicious, leaning towards being benign or incomplete communication.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00492", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It uses brand impersonation by referencing \"VZW\" (Verizon Wireless). It creates a sense of urgency with the phrase \"your payment is now due.\" Most critically, it directs the recipient to click a suspicious-looking link (`http://mobile.vzw.com/paybill`) to handle a financial transaction, which is a classic tactic in phishing attacks. While it also provides a legitimate-sounding alternative (dialing a number), the inclusion of the direct, suspicious link is the primary red flag.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00493", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham. The message is a simple, informal notification about a delay (\"Goodmorning, today i am late for 1hr.\"). It lacks any of the typical indicators of smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, requests for personal information, or brand impersonation. It reads like a casual text message between acquaintances or colleagues.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00494", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message contains highly informal, affectionate, and somewhat suggestive language (\"Hey sexy buns!\", \"loverboy\", \"babe\"). It references a personal, shared context (\"thank your sister in law for those meatballs\"). There are no suspicious cues typically associated with smishing, such as urgent demands, suspicious links, requests for financial information, or impersonation of a known entity for malicious purposes. The tone suggests a personal, non-malicious communication between people who know each other well.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
