{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00554", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is very brief and lacks any typical indicators of smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. It appears to be a casual, conversational message, possibly referencing a person named \"chechi.\" The context is too minimal to definitively label it, but based on the content alone, it exhibits no suspicious characteristics.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00557", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing.\n1. **Urgency/Problem Creation:** It creates a sense of urgency by claiming a package delivery has failed due to incomplete address information, prompting immediate action.\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The URL provided, `https://usps.trckmypost.com`, is highly suspicious. While it attempts to mimic USPS, the domain name (`trckmypost.com`) is not the official USPS domain, indicating potential phishing.\n3. **Request for Personal Info:** The message explicitly asks the recipient to \"confirm your address in the link,\" which is a request for sensitive personal information.\n4. **Repetitive/Odd Instructions:** The instructions provided (\"Please reply to 1, then exit theSMS, open the SMS activationlink again, or copy the link toSafari browser and open it\") are convoluted and designed to confuse the user into clicking the malicious link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00558", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The content is unsolicited and suggestive (\"Area females wanting intimate date nights out on the town\"), which is a common tactic used in spam or scams. More critically, it contains a suspicious, shortened, and non-official-looking URL (`http://y.jgdfr.com/ZoAhX9Z`). This type of link is designed to trick the recipient into clicking, likely leading to a malicious website intended to harvest personal information or install malware. There is no legitimate context for this message.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00560", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several suspicious cues indicative of smishing. The message uses a brand name (\"T-MOB\") to create a sense of legitimacy (brand impersonation). It offers a \"giveaway\" as a lure, which is a common tactic in phishing. Most critically, it contains a shortened, suspicious URL (`puX30.com/5q5PfG9Xhm`) that directs the recipient to an external, untrusted site, likely to harvest personal information or credentials. The combination of a reward/giveaway and an unsolicited link is a strong indicator of a phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00561", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing.\n1. **Brand Impersonation:** It uses the \"BOA\" (Bank of America) name to appear legitimate.\n2. **Urgency/Threat:** It creates a sense of urgency and threat (\"probable association with a system vulnerability,\" \"prevent identity theft\") to prompt immediate action.\n3. **Suspicious Link:** The URL provided, `https://sec03boa.com/bRZQK`, is highly suspicious. While it contains \"boa,\" the domain structure (`sec03boa.com`) is not the official Bank of America domain, suggesting a phishing site designed to look similar.\n4. **Request for Action:** It demands the user \"Log in and verify your details,\" which is a common tactic in phishing attacks to steal credentials.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00562", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It impersonates a known financial institution (CITI), which is a common tactic in phishing. The message creates a sense of urgency or importance (\"Your contact information has been updated\") and directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`https://cltl-00.herokuapp.com/login`) to \"verify\" information. Legitimate banks typically do not ask users to click a link in an unsolicited SMS to verify account changes; they usually advise logging in through the official app or website.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00563", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message appears to be a casual, personal conversation between two individuals. It discusses a job situation, mentions a location (\"Exeter\"), and makes plans for the evening (\"be home about 3\"). There are no indicators of smishing, such as urgent demands, suspicious links, requests for personal information, or brand impersonation. The language is informal and conversational.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00566", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The message is vague (\"Don't ever leave home without it... the Tiger or the WOOD\"), which is a common tactic to pique curiosity and prompt a click. More significantly, it contains a shortened, suspicious URL (`http://healthdoor.boutique/d8uGZhsJ`). The domain structure and the use of a shortened link are red flags, as legitimate businesses rarely use such obscure or shortened links for important communications. The message lacks any clear context or sender identification, further increasing suspicion.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00567", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The primary red flags are the unsolicited nature of the job offer, the vague promise of a high daily income (\"An average of 6,000 TK can be received every day\"), and the immediate request to contact a personal WhatsApp number via a direct link. Legitimate job offers typically involve formal application processes, official company communication channels, and do not usually start with a vague \"Dear\" and immediately push the recipient to a personal chat link for job details. This structure is common in recruitment scams designed to lure victims into fraudulent schemes.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00568", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message impersonates \"T-mobile,\" a known brand, which is a common tactic in phishing. It uses a sense of urgency or incentive (\"Heads up: T-mobile one time pass. Get a reward\"). Most critically, the link provided, `https://aritc.yru.ac.th/redirect/78?url=bit.do/jmelgrud`, is highly suspicious. It does not appear to be an official T-Mobile domain, and the use of redirect services (`bit.do`) combined with an unrelated academic domain (`yru.ac.th`) is a classic sign of a malicious or deceptive link designed to harvest credentials or install malware.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00570", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses brand impersonation (\"T-MOB\") to appear legitimate. It creates a sense of incentive or urgency (\"Black Friday giveaway\") to entice the recipient. Most critically, it contains a suspicious, shortened URL (`gh196.COM/79HAVH1xY8`) which is characteristic of phishing attempts designed to trick users into clicking and entering personal or financial information. The context of an unsolicited \"giveaway\" following a payment history is a common social engineering tactic.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
