{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00815", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message appears to be a casual, conversational exchange between two people. It contains informal language (\"Ü dunno how 2 type it in word ar...\") and discusses a personal topic (\"When did i use soc... I use it only at home...\"). There are no indicators of smishing, such as urgent demands, suspicious links, requests for personal information, or brand impersonation.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00816", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It uses brand impersonation by referencing \"iPhone\" and \"Apple,\" but the provided URL is highly suspicious. The domain `wwww.soporteappleld.ocppl-com.de` is not an official Apple domain and uses unusual subdomains and characters, which is a common tactic in phishing to mimic legitimate sites. Furthermore, the message creates a sense of urgency and concern (\"Your lost iPhone has been connected to the internet,\" \"in lost mode turned on\") to prompt immediate action, directing the user to the malicious link to \"locate or lock\" the device.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00818", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is very brief and lacks any clear context, urgency, suspicious links, or requests for personal information or payment. It appears to be a very informal, truncated update, possibly from a friend or acquaintance regarding travel or arrival. The lack of any malicious indicators suggests it is likely a legitimate, albeit poorly written, message.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00820", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham. The message is a casual, conversational update about a training session (\"Sday only joined.so training we started today:)\"). It contains no urgent language, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The tone is informal and typical of a personal or work-related chat.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00821", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency and Threat:** The message creates a sense of immediate crisis (\"account has been locked,\" \"will be terminated\") with a strict deadline (\"before 12 hours\").\n2. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates a well-known brand, \"Amazon,\" to gain trust.\n3. **Suspicious Link:** It directs the user to a link (`www.linkedin.com/slink?code=d8KfdKXY`) that is highly suspicious. While it uses the Amazon name, the URL structure points to LinkedIn, which is a common tactic in phishing to mask the true destination or leverage a trusted platform's domain.\n4. **Request for Action/Verification:** It demands the user click a link to \"unlock\" or \"verify\" their account, which is a classic phishing mechanism designed to steal credentials.\n\nThe combination of urgency, brand spoofing, and a suspicious call-to-action makes this highly likely to be a phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00822", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The use of a \"Security Warning\" combined with an immediate alert (\"EBT-Card is currently Frozen!\") creates a sense of urgency designed to panic the recipient. Furthermore, it includes a call to action directing the user to contact a specific support number, which is a common tactic in phishing to divert the victim to a fraudulent support line. While it doesn't contain a direct link, the combination of an urgent security threat related to a government benefit program (EBT) and the immediate demand for contact information strongly suggests an attempt at social engineering or credential harvesting.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00823", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The content is highly suggestive and unsolicited, which is a common tactic in phishing attempts to entice the recipient into clicking a link. The link provided, `http://mphrbt.com/J24sIRz`, is a suspicious, non-standard URL that does not belong to a recognizable, legitimate service. Furthermore, the message attempts to create a sense of immediate interest or curiosity (\"See now\"), pressuring the recipient to click the link without proper verification.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00824", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing.\n1. **Typographical Errors/Obfuscation:** The message uses unusual punctuation and abbreviations (\"fo.r\", \"receiv.ed\", \"Th.ank\", \"littl.e\", \"pre.sent\") which is a common tactic to bypass spam filters or appear less formal/official.\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The inclusion of a shortened, non-standard URL (`d8iHL.Com/RjS6w33AXD`) is a major red flag. Legitimate companies usually direct users to their official, recognizable domain.\n3. **Unsolicited Offer/Request:** While it starts with a confirmation, it immediately pivots to an unsolicited \"loyalty present,\" which is often used as a lure to get the recipient to click the link to \"claim\" the reward, leading to credential harvesting or malware installation.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00827", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several classic indicators of smishing. It creates a sense of urgency (\"you still owe...\"), impersonates a legitimate service (UPS), and requests the recipient to interact with a \"secure link\" to pay a small, unexpected fee. This structure is designed to trick the user into clicking a malicious link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00828", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham. The message is a very informal, conversational query that lacks any typical indicators of smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. It appears to be a casual text exchange between people who know each other.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00829", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is a very casual, affectionate greeting (\"Gud ni8 dear..sleep well�\"). It contains no links, no requests for personal information or payment, and does not exhibit any signs of urgency or impersonation. It reads like a personal, benign message between acquaintances or loved ones.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00830", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The primary suspicious cues are the urgent nature of the notification (\"You have been in contact with someone who has the COV-19 Variant\") and the inclusion of a suspicious, non-official-looking link (`hse.service-report.com`). Legitimate health or government notifications typically direct users to well-known, official domains, not newly constructed or generic-sounding ones. The request to \"order a testing kit\" via this link is a common tactic used in phishing scams to harvest personal or payment information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00831", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. Specifically, it uses a vague, unsolicited \"gift\" as a lure, which is a common social engineering tactic. More critically, it contains a shortened, suspicious URL (`x0ang.info/hYE427wAom`). The domain name itself appears unusual and is not associated with a known, legitimate entity, suggesting it is designed to harvest credentials or personal information upon clicking. The combination of an unsolicited reward and a suspicious link strongly indicates a phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00832", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message is classified as ham. The language is informal and conversational (\"I think its far more than that but find out\"), and it directs the recipient to use a common, legitimate tool (Google Maps) for a non-urgent, context-dependent task related to their dorm. There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00833", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency:** The message imposes a strict deadline (\"within 12 hours\") to prompt immediate, unthinking action.\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL, `usps.upspb.com`, is highly suspicious. While it attempts to mimic the USPS domain, the inclusion of \"upspb\" suggests a potential typo-squatting or malicious domain designed to look legitimate.\n3. **Request for Action/Information:** It demands the user confirm or update personal address information via a link, which is a common tactic in phishing to steal PII.\n4. **Unusual Instructions:** The complex instructions (\"Please reply to 1, then exit the SMS, open the SMS activation link again, or copy the link to browser and open it\") are designed to confuse the user and guide them toward the malicious link.\n\nThe combination of urgency, a suspicious domain, and a request for personal data strongly points to a phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00834", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It impersonates a known brand (\"Netflix\"), creates a sense of urgency/problem (\"Due to a problem with your card, We are having some trouble with your current information\"), and directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`http://unbouncepages.com/...`) to update sensitive billing information. Legitimate companies typically use secure, official portals for such updates, not generic, suspicious links in unsolicited texts.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00835", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing.\n1. **Suspicious Sender/Format:** The message starts with an unusual format (\"1 of 2 FRM:japho-Fe d3x.Ne.ws - MSG:\"), which is not typical of official communication from a major carrier or logistics company.\n2. **Urgency/Threat:** It creates a sense of urgency by stating an \"Unsuccessful package from 1 April owed to you,\" implying a problem that needs immediate resolution.\n3. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL (`https://wwj4w-fedex.us/jWXHwj?47965169`) is highly suspicious. While it attempts to impersonate FedEx, the domain (`wwj4w-fedex.us`) is not the official FedEx domain, indicating a phishing attempt designed to steal credentials or personal information.\n4. **Request for Action:** It demands the recipient \"assume ownership and confirm for delivery,\" which is a common tactic used in phishing to trick users into clicking the malicious link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00836", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message exhibits several characteristics of a non-malicious, informal greeting, which aligns with 'ham'. There is no evidence of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The use of excessive punctuation, informal language (\"Helloooo...\", \"GUD MRNG\"), and overly cheerful, generic phrasing suggests a casual, friendly, or perhaps automated but benign greeting, rather than a targeted phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00837", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message appears to be a personal communication, likely from someone who is currently at a hospital and providing contact information. There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The context suggests a genuine update from an individual.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00838", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing.\n1. **Brand Impersonation:** It uses \"WFargo,\" impersonating Wells Fargo, a legitimate financial institution.\n2. **Urgency/Problem Creation:** It immediately creates a sense of urgency and a problem (\"Your billpay did not process as scheduled\").\n3. **Suspicious Link/Call to Action:** It directs the user to follow a link/handle (\"Follow Ipsf/wfargo\") to \"review,\" which is a common tactic to trick users into clicking a malicious link or providing credentials.\n4. **Vague Language:** The message is vague about what needs to be solved, pressuring the recipient to click for details.\n\nThese elements strongly suggest an attempt at phishing rather than a legitimate notification from the bank.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
