{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00847", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It uses a lure (a large, unexpected financial reward—a \"$1000 Stimulus Check\") to entice the recipient. Crucially, it includes a suspicious, shortened, and non-official-looking URL (`dtsaiel.com/OMDOK9EA`) and prompts the user to \"Confirm details,\" which is a classic tactic used by phishing scams to harvest personal or financial information. The urgency implied by the \"approved\" status pushes the recipient to act quickly without critical thought.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00848", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It uses brand impersonation (Amazon) to create a sense of legitimacy. It employs urgency (\"within 48 hours to avoid permanent\") to pressure the recipient into acting quickly without thinking. Most critically, it contains a suspicious link (`https://notification-accountid.lnk.to/resolveaccount?...`) which is not a standard Amazon domain and is designed to trick the user into clicking and potentially entering credentials or personal information. The claim of a \"billing issue\" is a common pretext used in phishing attacks.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00849", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It uses brand impersonation (Netflix) to create a sense of legitimacy. It employs urgency (\"placed on hold,\" \"as soon as possible,\" \"without any interruption\") to pressure the recipient into immediate action. Most critically, it directs the user to a suspicious, non-official-looking link (`https://restartmmberswebsite.uscreen.io/`) to provide sensitive billing information. Legitimate companies usually direct users to log in through their official app or website rather than clicking a link in an unsolicited SMS for billing updates.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00850", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It impersonates a well-known brand, Amazon, which is a common tactic in phishing. The message creates a sense of urgency and alarm by claiming the account has been \"locked due to unusual activity.\" Crucially, it directs the user to click a suspicious, shortened, and non-official-looking URL (`https://t.sidekickopen08.com/...`) to \"restore\" the account, which is a classic phishing mechanism designed to steal credentials or install malware. The inclusion of specific, yet potentially fabricated, details like the login location and browser adds a layer of false legitimacy.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
