{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00865", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. Specifically, it uses an unsolicited, informal greeting (\"You got a text from FunKatie88!\"), which is not typical of legitimate service notifications. More critically, it contains a suspicious, shortened, and non-standard URL (`http://ytdbby.com/A2RsGxy`) that directs the recipient to an external site, likely to harvest credentials or install malware. The call to action (\"Tap here to access inbox\") creates a sense of urgency or necessity to click.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00867", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message mimics a bank or financial alert, which is a common tactic in phishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency/Alert Tone:** It immediately presents a financial transaction (\"Debit of $450 has been made\"), creating a sense of alarm or the need for immediate action.\n2. **Suspicious Link:** It includes a shortened URL (`https://bit.ly/3Nx3ar6`). Shortened links are frequently used in phishing attempts to obscure the true destination of the link.\n3. **Request for Action:** It prompts the recipient to \"kindly look into details,\" which is designed to trick the user into clicking the malicious link to \"verify\" the transaction.\n\nThe combination of a financial alert, an urgent tone, and a suspicious link strongly suggests an attempt to steal credentials or personal information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00868", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It impersonates a legitimate financial institution, Wells Fargo, which is a common tactic in phishing. The message creates a sense of urgency (\"A new IP address has been detected attempting to login\") to prompt immediate action from the recipient. Most critically, it directs the user to a shortened, suspicious link (`https://bit.ly/3ErwRWW`) under the guise of needing to \"login and secure your information,\" which is a classic phishing lure designed to steal credentials.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00869", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message appears to be a casual, conversational text message between friends or acquaintances discussing a potential dinner plan. It mentions a specific deal (\"cheese pizza is $2\") from a known entity (\"Gumby's\") but does not contain any suspicious elements like urgent demands, suspicious links, requests for personal information, or unsolicited financial requests. The tone is informal and relates to social planning.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00871", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several classic signs of smishing. It impersonates a well-known brand (UPS) and creates a sense of urgency/problem (\"Your package unable to deliver\"). Crucially, it directs the recipient to a shortened, suspicious link (`https://is.gd/ZjGmy4`) under the guise of needing to \"re-check your zip code,\" which is a common tactic to trick users into entering personal or financial information on a malicious site.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00872", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing.\n1. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates Amazon, a well-known retailer, to gain the recipient's trust.\n2. **Urgency/Threat:** It creates a sense of urgency and threat by claiming the account has been \"suspended\" or \"locked\" due to \"unusual activity.\"\n3. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL (`https://lindsy352.preview.softr.app/?t=1659623895934`) is highly suspicious. It does not use the official Amazon domain, suggesting it is a phishing landing page designed to steal credentials.\n4. **Request for Action:** It demands immediate action (verification) via the suspicious link.\n\nThese elements combined are classic hallmarks of a phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00873", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is very brief and informal, suggesting a personal communication rather than a mass phishing attempt. The content (\"I'm in a movie... Collect car oredi...\") sounds like a casual message between acquaintances, possibly related to a shared plan or event. There are no immediate red flags such as urgent demands, suspicious links, requests for personal information, or impersonation of a known brand. The language is slightly informal (\"oredi\"), which is common in casual texting.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00874", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message \"Can do lor...\" is extremely brief and lacks any discernible context, urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. It appears to be an incomplete, informal conversational fragment. Without further context, it cannot be classified as smishing, as it contains no malicious indicators.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00875", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message offers an unsolicited incentive (\"$100 Store Credit\") for completing a survey, which is a common lure in phishing attempts. More critically, it contains a suspicious, shortened URL (`nearhonor.com/dZDij0`). This link is not from an official Walmart domain and is designed to trick the recipient into clicking, likely leading to a malicious site to steal personal information or install malware. The combination of an unexpected reward and a suspicious link is a classic smishing tactic.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00878", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The message creates a sense of urgency (\"can't be deliver,\" \"as soon as possible\") to prompt immediate action. It directs the recipient to click a shortened, suspicious link (`s.id/1vjjp`), which is a common tactic used in phishing attacks to harvest personal information or install malware. Furthermore, it impersonates a delivery service, a frequent target for these scams.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00879", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It uses a sense of urgency/opportunity (\"qualify to use a full policy pack... for just 19/mth!\"). Crucially, it contains a suspicious, shortened URL (`aaprmm6pc2.co/5zoily`) which is not associated with a known, legitimate insurance provider. Furthermore, it is a unsolicited promotional offer designed to drive clicks to an external, potentially malicious site.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00880", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The initial statement about the bill being paid is a common tactic to establish a false sense of legitimacy or reward. The inclusion of a link, especially one with a seemingly random or unusual domain name (`slappingtasting.com`), is a major red flag. The link is designed to entice the recipient to click, likely leading to a phishing site to steal personal information or install malware. The \"gift\" is a lure to make the recipient less suspicious of the link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00884", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing.\n1. **Urgency:** It creates a sense of urgency by stating the package \"will be returned\" and demanding action \"within 24 hours.\"\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL, `m.eitdi.uspsdi.site`, is highly suspicious. It does not appear to be an official USPS domain (which would typically be usps.com) and uses a complex, non-standard subdomain structure often employed by phishing sites.\n3. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates the United States Postal Service (USPS) to gain the recipient's trust.\n4. **Request for Action/Information:** It pressures the user to click the link to \"update the address,\" which is a common tactic to harvest personal information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00885", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message contains highly suggestive and inappropriate language (\"Get the Biggest Dick For Her\") combined with a suspicious, non-standard URL (\"spikefibrous.com/TrarTX\"). The combination of explicit content and a suspicious link strongly suggests a malicious or spam attempt, which falls under the umbrella of smishing or general spam/scamming, as it is not a legitimate communication.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00886", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The sender, \"Sammy,\" is unsolicited, and the message directs the recipient to a suspicious, shortened URL (`http://slzbyy.com/GvR0t2ZV`). This type of link is often used to host malicious content or phishing pages designed to steal credentials or install malware. There is no context provided to suggest this is a legitimate communication from a known contact or service.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00887", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency/Threat:** The message creates a sense of urgency (\"before 24 hours\") and uses a threat (\"we had to locked your account\") to prompt immediate action.\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The URL provided (`http://unbouncepages.com/accnt-sginupcontinues12512/`) is not a legitimate PayPal domain and appears to be a generic, suspicious landing page designed to harvest credentials.\n3. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates a well-known brand, PayPal, which is a common tactic in phishing attacks.\n4. **Request for Action/Information:** It demands the user validate sensitive information (billing address) via an external, untrusted link.\n\nThese elements strongly suggest an attempt to trick the recipient into providing personal or financial information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00888", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is highly suspicious. It uses a format mimicking an official document notification (\"IRS_Statement_ID40AJGAT9.pdf\") and immediately presents a downloadable PDF file. The IRS is known to communicate through official channels, and unsolicited links or attachments demanding immediate attention or containing official-looking documents are a classic tactic used in smishing attacks to trick recipients into downloading malware or providing sensitive information. The lack of any context or personalized greeting further increases the suspicion.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
