{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00895", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several suspicious cues indicative of smishing. Specifically, it uses a sense of urgency/incentive (\"New Incentive $100\") to entice the recipient. More critically, it contains a suspicious, non-standard URL (`areanitrogen.com/Leye1E`) which is likely a phishing domain designed to mimic a legitimate offer or brand, rather than an official Grab or CVS link. The combination of an unsolicited financial incentive and a suspicious link strongly suggests a phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00897", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The language is informal and grammatically poor (\"Perhaps * is much easy give your account identification, so i will tomorrow at UNI\"). The message explicitly requests \"account identification,\" which is a direct request for sensitive personal information. The vague nature of the message and the unusual phrasing suggest a phishing attempt rather than a legitimate communication.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00898", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message contains casual language, abbreviations (\"Thk,\" \"shld,\" \"4\"), and conversational filler (\"Haha\"). It discusses plans for lessons and seems like a continuation of a personal conversation. There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00899", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It impersonates a legitimate entity (USPS) to create a sense of urgency (\"package has arrived... but cannot be delivered\"). Crucially, it directs the recipient to click a suspicious link (`https://www.uspostalcenter.bond`). The domain name, `uspostalcenter.bond`, is not the official USPS domain and appears designed to look similar while being malicious. The request is to \"confirm your address,\" which is a common tactic used in phishing to harvest personal information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00900", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing.\n1. **Brand Impersonation:** It claims to be from \"Wells Fargo SecurityCenter,\" impersonating a legitimate financial institution.\n2. **Urgency/Criticality:** The use of \"Critical notification!\" creates a sense of urgency, pressuring the recipient to act quickly without thinking.\n3. **Suspicious Link:** The link provided is a Google Forms URL (`docs.google.com/forms/...`). Legitimate banks typically use their own secure, branded domains for critical security notifications, not generic survey/form builders like Google Forms.\n4. **Vague Content:** The message is highly generic (\"Critical notification!\") and requires the user to click the link to understand the supposed issue, which is a common tactic in phishing.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00901", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message presents a notification of a charge, which is a common tactic in smishing to create a sense of urgency or concern (fear of unauthorized charges). The inclusion of a specific, high-value item (\"MADSHNE 24\"\" Caged Ceiling Fan with Lights\") and a specific charge amount ($268.00) attempts to lend credibility. The call to action directs the recipient to call a specific phone number for \"assistance.\" While the message doesn't contain a suspicious link, the unsolicited nature of the charge notification, combined with the request to call a provided number to resolve a potential issue, strongly suggests an attempt at social engineering to trick the user into revealing information or confirming the charge.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00903", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The use of a vague, unsolicited \"giveaway\" is a common lure. More critically, it contains a suspicious, shortened URL (`AGO8D.COM/hViaOS66VB`) which is designed to trick the recipient into clicking and potentially entering personal or financial information. The message attempts to create a sense of reward/urgency (\"Congratulations!\") to prompt immediate action.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00904", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency and Threat:** The message creates a sense of immediate crisis (\"Your Amazon Account has been locked,\" \"Recovery your account immediately,\" \"within 48 hours to avoid permanent suspension\").\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL (`http://s952788954.onlinehome.us/?snpl_2_1_db77b4d1`) is highly irregular and does not resemble an official Amazon domain.\n3. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates a well-known brand (Amazon) to trick the recipient.\n4. **Request for Action:** It demands immediate action via a link, which is a common tactic in phishing attacks to steal credentials.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00906", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing/spam. The presence of a non-standard, cryptic link (`whatsapp://chat/join/?code=...`) combined with an unsolicited invitation to an investment group is highly suspicious. Furthermore, the message uses high-pressure, unrealistic financial promises (\"1K-10K usd a day\") and mentions cryptocurrency (\"Bitcoin,\" \"tcoin\"), which are common tactics used in investment scams. The fragmented and nonsensical nature of the text (\"my ears so that I could Bitcoin OF AMERICA ar. You are invited...\") suggests automated or poorly constructed spam.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00907", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It uses a sense of urgency (\"Fraud Alerts,\" \"unusual activities\") to prompt immediate action. It impersonates a known financial institution (Wells Fargo). Most critically, it directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`WellsfargoVerify.com/act`) to \"confirm activities,\" which is a classic tactic used in phishing to steal credentials or personal information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00909", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses a truncated sender ID (\"FRM:719132-Fed_EX\") which is often used to mask the true origin. The content creates a sense of urgency and concern (\"it will be returned to sender,\" \"We have made several attempts to reach you\"). Most critically, it contains a suspicious link (`https://jf245-fedex.me/y.jDxio?37588372`). The domain name (`jf245-fedex.me`) is not the official FedEx domain, indicating brand impersonation designed to trick the recipient into clicking a malicious link, likely to steal personal or payment information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00910", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is very brief and informal, discussing sports like football and cricket. There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The language is casual (\"lyk\"), suggesting a personal or informal conversation rather than a malicious attempt.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00911", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing.\n1. **Urgency/Threat:** The message creates a sense of immediate crisis (\"All funds are on hold due to multiple fraudulent direct deposits\").\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The URL provided (`Https://www.UnionBank.Org_@nx.tn/EjmCx`) is highly suspicious. It attempts to mimic the legitimate bank name (\"UnionBank.Org\") but uses an unusual and non-standard domain structure (`@nx.tn`), which is a common tactic in phishing to deceive users.\n3. **Request for Action:** It directs the user to click a link to resolve a serious financial issue, which is a classic phishing lure.\n4. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates a known financial institution (UnionBank).\n\nThese elements combined strongly suggest an attempt to trick the recipient into clicking a malicious link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00912", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is a very short, casual communication (\"K I'll be there before 4.\"). It contains no suspicious elements such as urgency, links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. It reads like a normal, informal text exchange between people who know each other.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00913", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of a scam, classifying it as smishing (or a similar form of online/textual fraud, often related to romance scams).\n\n**Evidence:**\n1. **Unsolicited Contact:** The message is a cold outreach to an unknown recipient.\n2. **Vague/Impersonal Greeting:** \"Hi there. nice to meet you.\" is generic.\n3. **Romance/Dating Scam Structure:** The message immediately pivots to a highly specific, yet vague, dating proposition (\"female friend with a good financial,\" looking for a marriage partner within a specific age range). This is a classic setup for romance scams.\n4. **Call to Action with a Link:** It demands an immediate action (\"Reply the number '1' and Click the link\") leading to a WhatsApp link (`https://wa.me/...`). While WhatsApp itself isn't inherently malicious, the context of the unsolicited, high-stakes proposition makes the link highly suspicious.\n5. **Urgency/Pressure:** The phrasing implies immediate action is required to connect with this \"friend.\"\n\nThe combination of unsolicited romantic solicitation and a directive to click a link strongly suggests a deceptive attempt to lure the recipient into a scam.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00914", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message is a casual, conversational text exchange. It contains no links, no requests for personal information or payment, and lacks any sense of urgency or impersonation of a brand. The language is informal and personal.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00915", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The primary red flags are the urgent tone (\"If you do not make this transaction, you must cancel before 4 hours\"), the inclusion of a shortened, suspicious link (`https://lnkd.in/ekwVSzPa`), and the notification of a financial transaction that the recipient may not have initiated or is being pressured to act upon immediately. While it mimics a transaction notification (a common tactic), the combination of urgency and the external link strongly suggests an attempt to trick the user into clicking to \"cancel\" or verify, which is typical of phishing.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00917", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing.\n1. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates the Internal Revenue Service (IRS), a highly trusted government entity, to gain the recipient's confidence.\n2. **Urgency/Incentive:** It uses the lure of a \"tax refund\" to entice the recipient into acting quickly.\n3. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL, `https://irs.gov.tax-helping.com`, is highly suspicious. While it starts with \"irs.gov,\" the actual domain is `tax-helping.com`, not the official IRS domain (`irs.gov`). This is a classic tactic of creating a lookalike domain.\n4. **Request for Personal Info:** The message explicitly asks the user to \"confirm your information\" via the link, which is a direct request for sensitive personal data.\n\nThese elements combined strongly suggest a phishing attempt designed to steal personal information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00918", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The message makes an exaggerated and unrealistic health claim (\"Just One cup right before sleeptime can shed of 39 - lbs\"), which is a common tactic in scams to entice clicks. Furthermore, it includes a suspicious, unsolicited link (`buyelect.co`) that directs the recipient to an external, potentially malicious website. The message is promotional and unsolicited, rather than a legitimate communication from a known service or contact.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00919", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The sender impersonates the Internal Revenue Service (IRS), a common tactic in phishing attacks. It creates a sense of urgency and incentive (\"You will receive a tax refund of $573.00\"). Crucially, it directs the recipient to a suspicious link: `https://irs.gov.direct-paying.com`. While it attempts to mimic the official IRS domain, the addition of \"direct-paying.com\" makes it a clear spoofed or malicious URL, not the official IRS website. Furthermore, it requests the user to \"confirm your information,\" which is a request for sensitive personal data.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00920", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is very brief and uses informal language (\"Gud ni8.swt drms.take care\"). It does not contain any suspicious cues such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. Instead, it appears to be a casual, friendly greeting or sign-off, which is characteristic of legitimate, informal communication between known parties.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
