{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00976", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It impersonates a well-known service (Netflix) and creates a sense of urgency (\"placed on hold,\" \"as soon as possible\") to prompt immediate action. Crucially, it directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`http://updatebillingpage.us:911@s956956807.onlinehome.us/swg`) to update sensitive billing information, which is a classic phishing tactic.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00978", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The primary cues are the unsolicited nature of the invitation, the promise of unusually high and quick returns (\"earn 500-5K usd a day\"), which is a classic hallmark of investment scams. Furthermore, the use of a suspicious, non-official link (a WhatsApp group link used for unsolicited financial schemes) combined with the vague and overly enthusiastic language points toward a phishing attempt designed to lure the recipient into a fraudulent scheme.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00979", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message exhibits several suspicious cues indicative of smishing. The message uses a format that mimics an official notification (\"FRM:Navy Federal Credit Union.DEBIT LOCKED SUBJ:Contact...\"). The claim that a \"DEBIT LOCKED\" requires immediate action is a common tactic used in phishing to create a sense of urgency and panic. While it provides a phone number instead of a direct link, the context of an unsolicited security alert from a financial institution is highly suspicious and warrants caution.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00980", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is very informal and conversational. It asks a simple question about a time (\"Wat time ü wan today?\"). There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The language is casual, suggesting it is likely a message between people who know each other.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00981", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. Specifically, it uses a sense of urgency (\"this will not last too long\") to prompt immediate action. More critically, it contains a suspicious, non-standard URL (`http://sidedeity.finance/lgUzySA5`) which is likely designed to harvest personal or financial information under the guise of a Costco promotion. While it mentions a known brand (Costco), the combination of urgency and the suspicious link strongly suggests a phishing attempt rather than a legitimate marketing message from the retailer.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00982", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message contains a file attachment named \"IRS_Statement_ID1G8J62KOZ.pdf\". The inclusion of \"IRS\" strongly suggests an impersonation of the Internal Revenue Service, a common tactic in smishing attacks. Furthermore, unsolicited PDF attachments, especially those purporting to be official tax documents, are a major red flag for phishing, as they can contain malware or lead to credential harvesting pages. The message lacks any context or greeting, which is typical of automated, malicious outreach.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00983", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message impersonates a known financial institution (\"citii bank\"), which is a common tactic in phishing. It creates a sense of urgency or necessity by demanding the user \"complete your debit pin again.\" Most critically, it includes a shortened, suspicious link (`https://bit.ly/35Xs2rW`) that directs the user to an external, unverified site, which is the primary mechanism for credential harvesting in smishing attacks. Legitimate banks rarely ask customers to re-enter sensitive credentials like a debit PIN via an unsolicited SMS link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00984", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is very short and lacks any typical characteristics of smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. It reads like a simple, informal statement or a response to a previous conversation, suggesting it is a piece of ordinary conversation.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00985", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The content is highly suggestive, unprofessional, and uses suggestive language (\"peter,\" \"ROCK her for hrs\") which is often used in malicious or spam campaigns to entice clicks. Crucially, it contains a suspicious, shortened URL (`http://balancediameter.com/H10QLy`). This link is not associated with any recognizable, legitimate service and is designed to trick the recipient into clicking, likely leading to a phishing site. The message lacks any context of a legitimate communication (like a bank alert or a known service notification) and instead relies on provocative, unsolicited content to drive traffic to the suspicious link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00987", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The message uses a generic, non-descriptive sender ID (\"KJN468\"), which is common in phishing attempts. It claims a \"Status: Completed\" for an unspecified \"Application,\" creating a sense of urgency or importance that prompts the recipient to click. Most critically, it contains a shortened, suspicious URL (`veilknitting.com/y8mXEyk`). The domain name itself, while not immediately recognizable as a major brand, is presented in a context designed to trick the user into clicking to view \"results,\" which is a classic tactic for credential harvesting or malware delivery.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00988", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency/Threat:** The message threatens account limitation and demands action \"within 24 hours.\"\n2. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates Amazon, a well-known retailer.\n3. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL (`http://www.bizzapp.co.uk/free/apps51/service?=61513`) is not an official Amazon domain (which would be amazon.com or similar) and appears to be a generic, suspicious link designed to harvest credentials.\n4. **Request for Action:** It directs the user to click a link to \"verify\" or \"restore\" their account, which is a common tactic in phishing attacks.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00989", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message is classified as ham because it is a casual, conversational text message between what appears to be acquaintances or friends. It contains no elements of phishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The language is informal and relates to scheduling or availability.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00990", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing or a similar type of unsolicited scam. The request to \"rate or like the hotel we booked\" is vague and unsolicited, suggesting a potential survey scam or a pretext to gain contact information. The inclusion of a specific WhatsApp number and the arbitrary restriction (\"under 20 years old not allowed\") are common tactics used in phishing attempts to create a sense of exclusivity or urgency, prompting the recipient to engage with the provided contact method. There is no clear, legitimate context provided for why the recipient is being asked to participate in this rating scheme.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00991", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message appears to be a casual, personal communication between friends. It contains no elements typically associated with smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The language is informal and conversational, suggesting a pre-existing relationship between the sender and recipient.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00993", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It uses brand impersonation by referencing \"RBC Bank.\" It creates a sense of urgency and alarm (\"temporarily locked due to suspicious activity\") to prompt immediate action. Most critically, it directs the user to a suspicious link (`https://si-rbcroyalbank.com/`). The domain name is not the official RBC domain and is designed to look similar, which is a classic phishing tactic to trick users into entering credentials or personal information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00994", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses a generic sender ID (a phone number) rather than a recognizable company name. It creates a sense of urgency (\"package was held... due to unforeseen situation\"). Most critically, it directs the recipient to a suspicious, shortened link (`t.ly/IqtWq`) to \"check out for instruction,\" which is a common tactic used by phishers to trick users into entering personal or financial information on a malicious site.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00995", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. Specifically, it uses a lure (a chance to win $50) to entice the recipient into interacting with a link. The link provided, `http://answerurge.network/J4rzuzfR`, is a non-standard, suspicious URL that is likely designed to harvest personal information or credentials. While it references a known brand (CVS), the context of the \"survey\" or \"contest\" is unsolicited and designed to drive traffic to an external, untrusted site.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00996", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The sender ID, \"IRS\\_TAX\\_RE,\" attempts to impersonate the Internal Revenue Service (IRS), a common tactic in phishing attacks. The message itself is a direct link to a PDF document, which is a typical delivery mechanism for malware or credential harvesting pages in smishing campaigns. Furthermore, the context of an unsolicited tax document from an official-sounding source creates a sense of urgency or importance, prompting the recipient to click without scrutiny.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00997", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing.\n1. **Urgency/Threat:** The message creates a sense of urgency and threat by stating, \"For your protection we have placed a hold on your account.\" This is a common tactic to make the recipient act quickly without thinking.\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL, `https://colegioina.edu.pe/nfcu/`, is highly suspicious. While it contains \"nfcu\" (likely referencing NavyFederal Credit Union), the domain structure (`colegioina.edu.pe`) appears unrelated or is a deceptive subdomain/path designed to look legitimate. Legitimate financial institutions use their official, recognizable domains.\n3. **Request for Action/Verification:** The message demands the user \"verify\" their account status by clicking the link, which is a classic phishing technique to steal credentials.\n4. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates a known entity, \"NavyFederal,\" to gain trust.\n\nThe combination of urgency, brand impersonation, and a suspicious, non-official link strongly points to a phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00998", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message \"Then. You are eldest know.\" is extremely brief, grammatically unusual, and lacks any discernible context, sender identification, or request for information or action. It does not exhibit typical hallmarks of smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal data/payment. Conversely, it is too cryptic to be classified as a standard, legitimate piece of communication (ham). Given the ambiguity and lack of malicious indicators, it is most likely a poorly constructed or nonsensical message, which leans away from being a targeted phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00999", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several characteristics that suggest it is not a typical smishing attempt, but rather a casual, informal communication. The language used (\"ü,\" \"dat\") is highly colloquial and informal, which is more indicative of a personal text exchange between acquaintances rather than a professional or malicious phishing attempt. There are no suspicious links, urgent demands, requests for personal information, or brand impersonation. The content is a simple, personal request for a call.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01000", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It uses exaggerated, unrealistic claims (\"shed 28 lbs in 4 weeks\") to create a sense of false hope or urgency, which is a common tactic in scams. Furthermore, it includes a suspicious, shortened URL (`wlness21.com/3qOV`) that directs the recipient to an external, unverified website, likely to harvest personal information or push a fraudulent product. The message is promotional and unsolicited, fitting the profile of phishing attempts designed to lure users into clicking malicious links.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01001", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message contains very informal language (\"Ü,\" \"juz,\" \"oni\") and appears to be a continuation of a casual, personal conversation. There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The tone is conversational and benign.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01002", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham. The message contains sentimental, general well-wishes and philosophical statements (\"Heart is empty without love...\", \"Good night & sweet dreams\"). It lacks any typical indicators of smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, requests for personal information, or brand impersonation. It reads like a generic, friendly, or automated goodnight message.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01003", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses a sense of urgency (\"A new login was detected\") to prompt immediate action. Crucially, it directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-standard URL (`http://e-d-d-safety022.duckdns.org/a`). The domain structure, using a dynamic DNS service like `duckdns.org` and an unusual subdomain, is highly characteristic of phishing attempts designed to mimic legitimate services (like \"EDD\"). Furthermore, it requests the user to click a link to verify an account, which is a common tactic in phishing scams.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01004", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. It uses a broad, enticing claim (\"amazing give-a-way\") to lure the recipient. More critically, it contains a suspicious, non-standard URL (`http://pricesever.financial/j07mFgmb`). Legitimate promotions from a major retailer like Costco would typically direct users to the official Costco website, not a generic, unfamiliar domain. The combination of an unsolicited offer and a suspicious link strongly suggests a phishing attempt designed to steal credentials or personal information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01005", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message \"Finish liao... U?\" is extremely brief and informal. It uses colloquial language (\"liao,\" \"U?\") which suggests a casual conversation between people who know each other, rather than a formal, unsolicited communication typical of phishing attempts. There are no suspicious cues such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The context appears to be an ordinary, informal exchange.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01008", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message claims that customs fees are due for a delivered item, creating a sense of urgency and requiring immediate action to continue the delivery. Furthermore, it directs the recipient to a suspicious-looking URL (`frische-lieferung-de.com`) to make a payment, which is a classic tactic used in phishing attacks to steal financial information. The context of an unexpected fee for a delivery is a common lure.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01009", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing.\n1. **Brand Impersonation:** It uses the USPS (United States Postal Service) name, a trusted entity, to gain the recipient's confidence.\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The URL provided, `https://cya.nz/4UJQ`, is highly suspicious. It uses a non-standard, short-domain URL (`cya.nz`) rather than the official USPS domain, which is a common tactic in phishing attacks.\n3. **Urgency/Action Required:** The message implies an immediate problem (\"Your delivery Address need to be corrected\") that requires the user to click the link to resolve.\n4. **Request for Action:** It prompts the user to take an action (correcting the address/enabling re-delivery) via an external, untrusted link.\n\nThese elements strongly suggest an attempt to trick the user into clicking a malicious link to steal information or install malware.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01010", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several characteristics of smishing. The primary suspicious cue is the unsolicited nature of the message combined with a highly promotional and exaggerated claim (\"your friends won't recognize you\"). More critically, it contains a suspicious, shortened, and non-official-looking URL (`kmhgsq98am.co/3injuan`), which is a common tactic used in phishing attempts to direct users to malicious sites. While it doesn't explicitly ask for payment or sensitive personal information in the visible text, the presence of a suspicious link promoting a product is a strong indicator of a phishing attempt designed to trick the user into clicking and potentially entering data.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01011", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message \"Jus finish bathing...\" is a very short, informal, and conversational message. It lacks any of the typical indicators of smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. It reads like a casual update between friends or family.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01012", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing.\n1. **Suspicious Link:** The URL `ibii.me/JA9wQ3` is a shortened, non-official domain, which is a common tactic in phishing to hide the true destination.\n2. **Impersonation/Pretext:** It impersonates the USPS (United States Postal Service) and uses a government/official-sounding pretext (\"tax return Cashier's Check\") to create a sense of importance or obligation.\n3. **Urgency/Action Required:** It demands immediate action (\"visit... to validate your current address for redelivery\"), pressuring the recipient to click before thinking critically.\n4. **Request for Personal Info:** The stated purpose of the link is to \"validate your current address,\" which is a request for personal information.\n\nThese elements combine to strongly suggest a malicious attempt to trick the user into clicking a phishing link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01013", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is a casual, conversational text message suggesting a meeting time and location (\"Hey tmr meet at bugis 930 ?\"). There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The language is informal and typical of communication between acquaintances.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01014", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses excessive leetspeak (e.g., \"Y0U,\" \"FED3R4L,\" \"W4S_B4N,\" \"1R'S,\" \"F!x\") which is a common tactic in phishing attempts to bypass basic spam filters or appear informal/urgent. It creates a false sense of urgency (\"by the 1R'S\") regarding a supposed \"FEDERAL RETURN\" issue. Most critically, it contains a highly suspicious and non-official-looking URL (`//sl.ut.ac.id/1spp`) which is designed to trick the recipient into clicking and potentially entering personal or financial information. The combination of urgency, official-sounding but vague threat, and a malicious link points directly to phishing.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01015", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It impersonates a legitimate entity (USPS) to create a sense of urgency (\"package has arrived... but cannot be delivered\"). It directs the recipient to click a suspicious, non-official-looking link (`https://track-uspos-tols.com`) to resolve an issue, which is a classic phishing tactic. Furthermore, it requests the user to take an action (confirming address via the link) that could lead to the harvesting of personal information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01016", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message exhibits several benign cues. It is a casual, friendly greeting (\"hiya hows it going in sunny africa?\"), uses informal language (\"u r avin\"), and contains a personal, affectionate closing (\"give that big old silver back a big kiss from me\"). There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment, which are hallmarks of smishing.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01017", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message contains casual, conversational language and shares a personal anecdote. There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. Therefore, it appears to be a normal, non-malicious text message.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01018", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham. The message is a very informal, conversational snippet (\"Me i'm not workin. Once i get job...\") that lacks any typical indicators of a phishing attempt. There is no urgency, no suspicious links, no brand impersonation, and no request for personal information or payment. It appears to be a casual update between acquaintances.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01019", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses an unusual, truncated sender ID (\"1-1/ CO.STCO\"), which is often a tactic to appear official or generic. It references a specific ID (\"CID#30859\") to create a sense of context or urgency related to an account. Most critically, it contains a suspicious, non-standard URL (`http://194.87.140.6#yfuy.php`). This URL is not from a recognizable, legitimate domain and is designed to trick the recipient into clicking, likely to harvest credentials or personal information. The inclusion of \"Send 0 to stop\" is a common feature in automated spam/phishing messages.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01020", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message contains several elements that suggest it might be a phishing attempt, although it is not overtly malicious like a typical smishing text with a suspicious link.\n\n**Evidence Analysis:**\n1. **Vague/Generic Language:** The message is very brief and uses generic identifiers (\"EBT Food Card Alert,\" \"ID:7608036558\").\n2. **Call to Action (CTA):** It directs the recipient to \"Contact our 24/7 departement\" via a phone number. While legitimate services use phone numbers, unsolicited alerts often use this method to drive users to a controlled environment or to gather information over the phone.\n3. **Lack of Context:** There is no context provided (e.g., \"Your benefit has been updated,\" or \"There is an issue with your account\"). This lack of specific information is a common tactic in phishing to create a sense of urgency or importance without giving away too much.\n4. **Format:** The inclusion of an \"AlertID\" mimics official notification systems.\n\nWhile there is no suspicious link (a major indicator of smishing), the combination of an unsolicited \"Alert\" regarding a sensitive benefit program (EBT) and the directive to call a specific number without prior context raises suspicion. It is designed to prompt immediate action based on an implied problem or update. Therefore, it leans towards being a smishing attempt designed to trick the user into calling a fraudulent number.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01022", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message provided is extremely vague and lacks any discernible context, sender identification, or specific request. It contains only placeholders (`&lt;#&gt;`) and a comparative statement (\"Its a big difference.\"). There are no obvious indicators of smishing, such as urgent demands, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. Because it is so generic and uninformative, it cannot be definitively classified as malicious, but it also does not exhibit the characteristics of a normal, legitimate conversation. Given the lack of any suspicious elements, it leans towards being benign or incomplete, rather than a targeted phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01024", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It uses an urgent tone (\"ALERT\") to create a sense of panic regarding a blocked card. It impersonates a legitimate entity, \"Bank of America Prepaid EDD Debit Card.\" Most critically, it directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`http//:boaeddprepaidservices.com`) to resolve the supposed issue, which is a classic tactic for credential harvesting or malware installation.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01025", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. Firstly, it creates a sense of urgency by claiming an \"incorrect delivery address,\" prompting immediate action. Secondly, it directs the recipient to an external link (`https://usa-exchange-us.com`) to resolve this supposed issue. The domain name, while appearing somewhat related to shipping/exchange, is generic and not clearly associated with a major, recognizable postal service or retailer, which is a common tactic in phishing to trick users into clicking. The request is to update personal information (delivery address) via an unsolicited link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01026", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The primary suspicious cues are the unsolicited nature of the invitation, the promise of unusually high and quick returns (\"earn 500-5K usd a day\"), which is a classic hallmark of investment scams, and the request to join via a link (a WhatsApp group link in this case) to participate in a financial scheme. The language is overly promotional and lacks any personal context, suggesting a mass phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01027", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing or spam. The content is highly suggestive and unsolicited, which is a common tactic in phishing or spam campaigns to entice a click. The link provided, `http://prmytz.com/9yJfrBg`, is a shortened or unusual domain that does not appear to belong to a legitimate, recognizable service, making it a high-risk element. The combination of suggestive content and an unsolicited, suspicious link strongly suggests an attempt to lure the recipient into clicking a malicious link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01028", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It impersonates a legitimate entity (U.S.P.S.), creates a sense of urgency/problem (\"cannot be delivered due to incomplete address information\"), and directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`https://authority-ustesters.top/`) to resolve the issue, which is a common tactic for phishing.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01030", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several classic signs of smishing. It uses an unsolicited \"congratulations\" hook to create excitement and urgency (\"Receive NOW\"). The link provided, `https://groupme.prizeforyou.site/`, is suspicious because it directs the user to a non-official, likely malicious, third-party domain rather than a known, legitimate brand website. Furthermore, the premise of an unexpected prize draw is a common social engineering tactic used in phishing attempts to trick users into clicking the link and potentially revealing personal information or downloading malware.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01031", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham. The message consists of affectionate, informal language (\"Love you virat..love u lot..\") and does not contain any suspicious elements such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. It appears to be a personal, casual communication.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01033", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message is classified as ham because it appears to be a casual confirmation or scheduling message between two parties regarding a meeting time (\"7.30 or 8.30\") for an event or activity named \"ice age.\" There are no suspicious cues such as urgency, requests for personal information, payment demands, or suspicious links.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01034", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message contains no elements typically associated with smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. Instead, it is a philosophical or motivational greeting, which is characteristic of benign, informal communication.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01035", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency/Problem Creation:** It creates a sense of urgency by claiming a package delivery failure due to incomplete address information.\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL, `https://usps.serviceid.online`, is highly suspicious. While it attempts to mimic USPS, the domain (`serviceid.online`) is not an official USPS domain, suggesting a phishing attempt to harvest credentials or personal data.\n3. **Request for Action/Information:** It directs the user to click a link to \"confirm your address,\" which is a common tactic in phishing to steal personal details.\n4. **Unusual Instructions:** The instruction to \"reply 1 and restart iMessage to activate the link or copy the link to open in your Safari browser\" is an unusual and often manipulative instruction designed to bypass security warnings or trick the user into a specific action.\n\nThe combination of a delivery notification pretext, a non-official link, and a request to confirm personal information strongly points to a malicious attempt.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01036", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The message appears to be a social invitation, but the inclusion of a shortened, non-standard URL (`http://csrlvl.com/X0yujpo`) is highly suspicious. Legitimate social invitations rarely require clicking an external, unfamiliar link to respond or confirm. The URL itself does not resemble any known, reputable social media or dating platform.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01037", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues. The language is informal and contains numerous abbreviations (\"U,\" \"Wat's,\" \"e\"), which is unusual for a formal or legitimate communication, especially one requesting personal information. The direct request for the recipient's \"road name of ur house\" is a clear attempt to solicit Personally Identifiable Information (PII). While it doesn't contain a suspicious link or explicit urgency, the nature of the request itself—asking for private residential details unsolicited—is highly characteristic of social engineering or phishing attempts, even if the sender is trying to appear casual.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01039", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message appears to be a casual, informal conversation between acquaintances or friends. It uses colloquial language (\"Yup,\" \"mah,\" \"ü,\" \"wat\") and discusses a social arrangement (a meeting today) and a logistical concern (leaving early/walking far). There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01040", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing.\n1. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates Netflix, a well-known service.\n2. **Urgency/Threat:** It creates a sense of urgency and threat by stating the membership has been \"placed on hold\" due to billing issues, pressuring the recipient to act immediately.\n3. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL (`http://updatebillingpage.us:911@s957584550.onlinehome.us/nod`) is highly suspicious. It uses a complex, non-standard domain structure, includes unusual characters/ports, and does not resemble the official Netflix domain.\n4. **Request for Sensitive Information:** The message explicitly asks the user to \"update your billing information,\" which is a common tactic in phishing attacks to steal financial details.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01041", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It impersonates a delivery service, creating a sense of urgency (\"to avoid delay in receiving the goods\"). It requests the recipient to take an action (replying \"OK\" and then updating an address) via a suspicious, shortened URL (`http://wausps.com/go/Tracking/X2F13`). The domain name itself appears non-standard and potentially malicious.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01042", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message \"It took Mr owl 3 licks\" is a well-known, nonsensical phrase, often used as a joke or a meme. It contains no elements typically associated with smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. Therefore, it is classified as legitimate, casual communication.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01043", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It uses brand impersonation (\"SAFEDRIVERALERT,\" \"SAFEDRIVERDISCOUNTPERKS\") to create a sense of legitimacy, but the core message involves an unexpected financial issue (\"over charged on your AutOPOlicy\") designed to provoke action. Crucially, it includes a suspicious, shortened, and non-official-looking URL (`kzrp1h.com/bff4948af7`) which is the mechanism for phishing. The combination of urgency (implied by the need to \"get REIMBURSED\" before it's too late) and a suspicious link requesting interaction is characteristic of smishing attacks.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01044", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message is a casual, conversational text about going out for tacos. It contains no urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. Therefore, it is considered legitimate communication.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01046", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several characteristics commonly associated with smishing or promotional scams, although it is not a direct phishing attempt asking for login credentials. The key suspicious cues are:\n\n1. **Unsolicited Offer/High Reward:** The promise of \"huge profits in the cryptocurrency market\" and a chance to \"win 1000USD\" is a classic tactic used in investment scams to entice recipients.\n2. **Vague/Impersonal Greeting:** Starting with \"Dear friends\" is generic and lacks personalization, which is common in mass spam/scams.\n3. **Urgency/Exclusivity (Implied):** The framing as an \"internal discussion group\" suggests exclusivity and a time-sensitive opportunity.\n4. **Suspicious Link/Platform:** While the link directs to WhatsApp (a legitimate platform), the context—being invited via an unsolicited SMS to a high-yield investment group—is highly suspicious. These groups often lead to pump-and-dump schemes or require upfront payments.\n\nWhile it doesn't ask for passwords, the nature of the unsolicited, high-reward financial solicitation via SMS strongly suggests a deceptive or predatory marketing scheme, fitting the broader category of smishing/spam.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01047", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is very brief and informal, asking a casual question about a \"match.\" There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The language is conversational and lacks the typical hallmarks of a phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01048", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency/Threat:** The message claims the payment was \"Unsuccesful\" and the account is \"temporary Locked,\" creating immediate pressure to act.\n2. **Suspicious Link/Attachment:** It directs the user to \"The Complete Details In The Attachment Below,\" which is a common tactic to trick users into clicking malicious links or downloading malware.\n3. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates a well-known brand, Netflix, to gain trust.\n4. **Request for Action/Information:** While not explicitly asking for a password, it demands the user to \"check the message in detail\" via the provided link/attachment, which is the mechanism for credential harvesting or payment redirection.\n\nThe combination of brand impersonation, urgency, and a directive to click an external link makes this highly characteristic of a phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01049", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses a sense of urgency (\"Your Subcript!ons will expire today\") to pressure the recipient into immediate action. It contains a suspicious, shortened URL (`//@risu.io/B10Sz`) which is characteristic of phishing attempts designed to direct users to malicious sites. Furthermore, the message is impersonating a service (implied by \"Subcript!ons\") and is requesting the user to \"re-new your bills,\" which is a common tactic to trick users into entering payment or personal information. The misspelling (\"Subcript!ons\") is also a common red flag.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01050", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message is a simple, conversational question asking for advice on shipping cars. It contains no elements typically associated with smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. It appears to be a genuine inquiry between individuals.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01051", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The message is unsolicited and contains a shortened, suspicious-looking URL (`http://ypzvtd.com/OmwsYW8`). The context is vague (\"Member-Ezdebra504 sent you these pix\"), which is a common tactic to lure the recipient into clicking the link, often under the guise of shared content or a notification. The link itself is not associated with a known, legitimate service.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01052", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The primary suspicious cues are the unsolicited nature of the \"Refund Validation Request,\" the specific, high-value amount mentioned ($863.35), and the presence of a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`sqanzq.-com/cb8183`). Legitimate companies typically use official domains and do not demand immediate validation through obscure links in an SMS for refunds. The combination of urgency (implied by the request for immediate cooperation) and a suspicious link strongly suggests a phishing attempt designed to steal credentials or personal information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01053", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is a very brief, casual greeting (\"Gud mrng dear hav a nice day\"). It contains no urgent requests, no suspicious links, no brand impersonation, and no requests for personal information or payment. The language is informal, which is common in personal communication. Therefore, it lacks any typical indicators of a phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01054", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message contains sexually suggestive and highly personal content. There are no typical indicators of smishing, such as urgent requests, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. Instead, it appears to be a personal, albeit inappropriate, message between two individuals.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01055", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The use of a seemingly official-looking code/identifier (\"BFT#568008\") combined with a sudden, unsolicited offer of a large sum of money (\"Receive your assigned $289.00 now\") is a common tactic to lure victims. The instruction to \"Type 'A' to transmit your grocery bonus to your selected institution\" is a call to action that implies a transaction or data submission, which is often the goal of phishing attempts, even if the link isn't explicitly present in this snippet (the reply mechanism itself can be a vector). The context is vague and overly promotional, lacking the typical personalized greeting or clear context of a legitimate grocery store communication.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01056", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses alarming language (\"DANGEROUS Files have breached your phone\") to create a sense of urgency and fear, a common tactic in phishing. It also contains a suspicious, shortened URL (`nhavgrz.com/rNBNOmoo`) which is designed to trick the recipient into clicking a malicious link. Furthermore, the message implies a security threat requiring immediate action, which is a classic social engineering technique used in smishing attacks.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
