{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01085", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It impersonates a known brand (\"Netflix\") and creates a sense of urgency/problem (\"Your payment was unsuccessful\"). Crucially, it directs the recipient to a suspicious link (`https://www.2netflix-mybilling.com`). The domain name is slightly altered from the official Netflix domain, which is a common tactic in phishing attempts to trick users into entering sensitive information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01086", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message impersonates a legitimate entity, \"USPS,\" which is a common tactic in phishing. It creates a sense of urgency (\"couldnt make the delivery... at today\") to prompt immediate action. Most critically, it contains a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`vpmEvening49tw.4k1pkmal.in/#?.fkz2`) that directs the recipient to input personal information (their address) under the guise of rescheduling a delivery. Legitimate postal services typically use official domains and do not send unsolicited links via SMS for address verification.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01088", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It uses brand impersonation (\"BOFA-ALERT\") to appear legitimate, creates a sense of urgency and alarm (\"account has been locked due to suspicious activities\"), and directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`http://hnter.ml`) under the guise of needing to provide information for \"protection.\" Legitimate banks typically do not lock accounts and demand immediate action via an unsolicited link in an SMS.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01089", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message is highly obfuscated, containing random strings of characters (\"FRM:gP8e_-UPS-NFIGe\", \"IsEqWKMBuM-a4\") and a suspicious, non-standard URL (\"www.92wbj4jzupst.com//49epb8Rw17003#\"). The inclusion of \"UPS\" in the header suggests brand impersonation, a common tactic in phishing attempts, but the rest of the message is gibberish, which is designed to look like a tracking or notification code while directing the user to a malicious link. There is no clear, legitimate context, and the structure is characteristic of automated, deceptive messages.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01090", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message is classified as ham because it is a casual, conversational statement that does not contain any typical indicators of a phishing attempt. There is no urgency, no suspicious links, no brand impersonation, and no request for personal information or payment. It reads like a personal, informal text exchange.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01091", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message contains no elements typically associated with smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. Instead, it appears to be a casual, personal message expressing feelings of loneliness and mentioning a personal item (\"new bong\"). The tone is informal and conversational.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01092", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing.\n1. **Urgency/Threat:** It creates a sense of urgency by stating the package \"will be returned,\" pressuring the recipient to act quickly.\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL (`v4.hvg.in/.861G6`) is highly suspicious. It does not appear to be an official USPS domain (which would typically be usps.com or a related official subdomain) and uses a generic, obfuscated domain structure often employed in phishing attacks.\n3. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates the United States Postal Service (USPS), a common tactic in phishing to gain trust.\n4. **Request for Action/Information:** It directs the user to click a link to \"update the address,\" which is the mechanism used to harvest personal or payment information.\n\nThese cues strongly suggest a malicious attempt to trick the user into providing sensitive data.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01093", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several characteristics of smishing. It creates a sense of urgency (\"failed $57.78 @ Eleven\") and references a specific, potentially unauthorized transaction, which is a common tactic to trick recipients into acting quickly. Furthermore, it provides a direct phone number to \"reach us,\" which is a common method used by scammers to initiate a fraudulent conversation. The combination of an unexpected financial alert and a directive to contact an external number strongly suggests a phishing attempt rather than a legitimate notification from a known service.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01096", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message appears to be a casual, informal conversation between two parties discussing the completion of a document or project (mentioning \"conclusion,\" \"contents pg,\" and \"references\"). There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The language is highly abbreviated and informal, suggesting a personal or academic context rather than a malicious attempt.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01097", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message contains no typical indicators of smishing, such as urgent requests, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. Instead, the content is highly personal, conversational, and relates to a specific, private situation (likely concerning someone's health or well-being). The language is informal and context-dependent, suggesting a communication between people who know each other well.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01098", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency:** The message imposes a strict 12-hour deadline (\"within 12 hours\").\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The URL provided, `https://usphm.top`, is not the official USPS domain (which would be usps.com) and uses a suspicious top-level domain (.top), suggesting impersonation.\n3. **Request for Action/Information:** It prompts the recipient to confirm address information via the link, which is a common tactic to harvest personal data.\n4. **Unusual Instructions:** The complex instructions (\"Please reply to Y, then exit the SMS, open the SMS activation link again, or copy the link to Safari browser and open it\") are designed to confuse the user and guide them toward a specific, potentially malicious, action.\n\nThese elements strongly suggest an attempt to trick the user into clicking a malicious link or providing personal details under the guise of a legitimate USPS notification.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01100", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message appears to be a casual, conversational exchange between two people planning an activity (yoga). There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The tone is informal and friendly.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01101", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several characteristics commonly associated with smishing. It mimics a legitimate transaction notification, specifically referencing a charge, an item description (\"Domesis Matthewson Recliner Charcoal Gray\"), and an order ID (\"AMZ1649751\"). The inclusion of a phone number and the implied urgency (\"If you DO NOT...\") suggests the sender wants the recipient to take immediate action, likely to dispute the charge or verify the transaction. While it doesn't contain a direct link, the structure is designed to look like a fraudulent notification to prompt a call to a potentially malicious number.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01102", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham. The message is a casual, conversational inquiry (\"Wat would u like 4 ur birthday?\"). It lacks any of the typical indicators of smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for sensitive personal information or payment. It appears to be a personal message between acquaintances.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01103", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The use of \"Instant freebies\" creates a sense of urgency and enticement, a common tactic in phishing. More critically, the URL provided, `http://rgjgj.capital/i16nf0`, is highly suspicious. It does not resemble the official HomeDepot domain and uses a generic, non-branded top-level domain (.capital), which is characteristic of malicious shorteners or phishing sites designed to trick users into clicking. The combination of an unsolicited offer and a suspicious link strongly suggests an attempt to steal credentials or install malware.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01104", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. Specifically, it uses a vague, unsolicited \"gift\" as a lure, which is a common tactic in phishing. More critically, it contains a suspicious, shortened URL (`T Mobile POSC9.XYZ/YgSpZZjZoh`). While it attempts to impersonate \"T Mobile,\" the unusual structure and the presence of a non-standard domain segment (`POSC9.XYZ`) strongly suggest it is not an official T-Mobile communication. The combination of an unsolicited reward and a suspicious link is a major red flag.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01105", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham because it is a very casual, conversational message (\"Lol now I'm after that hot air balloon!\"). It contains no elements typically associated with smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. It appears to be part of an ordinary, informal conversation.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01106", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses an urgent and exciting tone (\"YOUR SATURDAY PACKAGE! ; It Is Finally Out For Delivery\") to prompt immediate action. Crucially, it contains a suspicious, non-standard URL (`http://drilldeliver.com/xbgssimr/BmzxcMn`). This URL does not appear to belong to a known, legitimate delivery service and is designed to trick the recipient into clicking, likely to steal personal information or install malware. The combination of urgency and a suspicious link is a classic smishing tactic.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01107", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency and Threat:** The message creates a sense of immediate crisis (\"account has been suspended,\" \"Recovery your Netflix account immediately,\" \"within 48 hours to avoid permanent suspension\").\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL (`https://ffm.to/plans-membership`) is a shortened, non-official domain, which is a common tactic used in phishing to hide the true destination.\n3. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates a well-known brand (Netflix) to gain the recipient's trust.\n4. **Request for Action/Information:** It pressures the user to click a link to \"recover\" the account, which is the mechanism used to steal credentials or personal information.\n\nThese elements strongly suggest a malicious attempt to trick the user into providing sensitive data.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01108", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses urgent and threatening language (\"Second and final advise: We W! Il Block Your gadget promptly\") to create a sense of panic, a common tactic in phishing. It also contains a suspicious, shortened URL (`congvd.me/X2Onlwz97M`), which is designed to trick the recipient into clicking a malicious link. The phrasing is also unprofessional and contains typos/odd capitalization (\"W! Il\", \"me$sage's\"), which is typical of mass phishing attempts.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01109", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message appears to be a personal conversation snippet, not a solicitation or attempt to trick the recipient. It contains informal language, typos (\"cme,\" \"hos,\" \"2morow,\" \"wil\"), and discusses personal plans (\"I will come,\" \"want to go to hos tomorrow\"). There are no suspicious cues such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01110", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message exhibits several suspicious cues indicative of smishing.\n1. **Vague Sender/Greeting:** It addresses \"T-mobile subscribers\" generally, which is a common tactic in mass phishing attempts rather than a personalized communication.\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The link provided, `shorte.be//f?$=62344?2619646`, is a shortened URL. Shortened links are frequently used in phishing to obscure the true destination of the malicious site.\n3. **Vague Call to Action:** The message asks for \"feedback,\" which is a common pretext used by scammers to trick users into clicking a link that leads to a fake survey or login page designed to steal information.\n4. **Lack of Specificity:** Legitimate service providers usually provide more context or a direct, recognizable link when requesting feedback.\n\nThe combination of a generic greeting, a shortened, suspicious link, and a vague request strongly suggests a phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01111", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency and Threat:** The message uses high-pressure language (\"time sensitive correspondence,\" \"Failure to respond... may result in a loss or denial of your unemployment benefits\") to provoke immediate action without careful thought.\n2. **Suspicious Link:** It directs the user to a shortened, non-official-looking URL (`https://t.ly/Ot6za`) rather than the official, known website for the unemployment service.\n3. **Impersonation:** It impersonates an official government or benefits agency (Unemployment benefits).\n4. **Request for Action/Login:** It pressures the user to log in via the provided link to view sensitive correspondence, which is a common tactic to steal credentials.\n\nThe combination of urgency, threat, and a suspicious link strongly suggests a phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01112", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several characteristics of spam or unsolicited commercial messaging, but it lacks the typical hallmarks of a targeted smishing attack (like urgent threats, requests for login credentials, or suspicious links). The tone is overly familiar, generic, and suggestive, which is common in spam/scam attempts designed to lure recipients into further interaction, but it doesn't fit the definition of phishing (which requires a deceptive attempt to steal information). Therefore, it is classified as general spam/ham, not smishing.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01113", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is a very casual, conversational greeting (\"So what u doing today?\"). It contains no urgent language, no suspicious links, no brand impersonation, and makes no requests for personal information or payment. These characteristics align with a normal, informal text exchange between acquaintances.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01114", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message exhibits several characteristics that suggest it is not a typical smishing attempt, but rather a form of spam or a social engineering/engagement tactic, possibly related to a quiz or challenge.\n\n**Evidence Analysis:**\n1. **Urgency/Threat:** There is no immediate threat or urgent demand for action (like \"Your account will be suspended\").\n2. **Suspicious Links:** There are no suspicious links present.\n3. **Brand Impersonation:** There is no clear impersonation of a known brand or institution, although the mention of \"CAT question\" suggests an academic context.\n4. **Requests for Personal Info/Payment:** The message does not explicitly ask for personal information or payment. It asks for an answer to a math problem.\n5. **Content Nature:** The content is a riddle/quiz format (\"Only 2% students solved this CAT question...\"). This is a common tactic used in viral messages or engagement spam, rather than a direct phishing attempt designed to steal credentials or money.\n\nWhile the message is unsolicited and potentially spammy, it lacks the core components of a *smishing* attack (i.e., a malicious link or direct request for sensitive data under false pretenses). It is more accurately classified as general spam or a low-level engagement scam, but based strictly on the definition of smishing (SMS phishing), it does not meet the criteria. Therefore, it is classified as ham (legitimate in the sense that it is not a phishing attempt, even if it is spam).\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01117", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is very brief and conversational, containing simple well-wishes (\"ok....take care.umma to you too...\"). There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The tone is informal and friendly, suggesting a casual exchange between known parties.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01118", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. It presents an unsolicited offer (\"full coverage auto for less than 30 a month\") which is a common tactic used in phishing to entice clicks. Furthermore, it includes a shortened, suspicious link (`slkt.io/UY1T`) that directs the recipient to an external, untrusted site, and it pressures the recipient with a call to action (\"or text end\") to encourage immediate engagement. The message is unsolicited and attempts to lure the user into clicking a link to potentially steal information or install malware.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
