{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01168", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. It presents an unsolicited job offer with an unusually high compensation ($600 weekly) for a remote role, which is a common tactic in job scams. Furthermore, it directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`https://miborealityasociate.wixsite.com/mibor`) to \"learn more,\" which is a classic phishing vector designed to harvest personal information or credentials. The tone is promotional and urgent in the sense that it is an immediate opportunity.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01169", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits a casual, informal tone and uses slang (\"imma,\" \"n shit\"). It does not contain any typical indicators of smishing, such as urgent demands, suspicious links, requests for personal information, or impersonation of a known entity (like a bank or delivery service). Instead, it sounds like a personal, informal message between acquaintances.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01170", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing.\n1. **Urgency/Threat:** It creates a sense of urgency by alerting the user to an unauthorized login, pressuring them to act immediately.\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL (`https://secur-34b0a-protect.duckdns.org/auth/`) is highly suspicious. It uses a non-standard, dynamically generated domain (`duckdns.org`) and contains generic, obfuscating subdomains, which is characteristic of phishing attempts rather than official bank domains.\n3. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates a legitimate entity (\"Edd account\"), attempting to trick the recipient into believing the message is from their bank.\n4. **Call to Action:** It directs the user to click a link to \"regain access,\" which is the primary goal of credential harvesting in smishing attacks.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01171", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message creates a sense of urgency (\"online access locked!\"), impersonates a known financial institution (Chase Mobile), and directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`http://jmp.su/vrfy`) to \"identify your details.\" This combination of urgency, brand impersonation, and a request for sensitive information via a suspicious link is characteristic of phishing attempts.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01172", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several suspicious cues indicative of smishing. The message claims a bill has been paid, which is a common tactic to build trust or prompt a response. More critically, it includes a shortened, suspicious URL (`mentioningdears.com/nzWjdtrM93`) presented as a \"gift.\" Legitimate companies rarely send unsolicited messages with shortened, unknown links promising free gifts, especially when related to billing. The lack of a clear sender ID or recognizable brand further increases suspicion.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01173", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several benign cues. It is a casual, conversational message that appears to be a social interaction, not a solicitation or attempt to trick the recipient. There are no urgent demands, suspicious links, requests for personal information, or attempts at brand impersonation. The tone is apologetic and friendly, suggesting a simple mistake in dialing.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01174", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency/Problem Creation:** It creates a sense of urgency by claiming a package delivery failure due to incomplete address information.\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The URL provided, `https://usppsn.top`, is highly suspicious. It does not use the official USPS domain (`usps.com`) and uses a generic, non-official top-level domain (.top), which is a common tactic in phishing.\n3. **Request for Action/Information:** It directs the user to click a link to \"confirm your address,\" which is a common method for harvesting personal information (PII).\n4. **Unusual Instructions:** The instruction to \"reply Y, then exit the text message and open it again to activate the link\" is an unusual and often manipulative tactic designed to confuse the user or bypass security checks.\n\nThe combination of a fake brand impersonation (USPS), a suspicious link, and a request to verify personal details strongly points to a phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01175", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message is classified as ham because it is a casual, personal greeting and well-wishing message. It contains no elements of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment, which are the hallmarks of smishing.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01176", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The primary suspicious cues are:\n1. **Urgency/Alarm:** The message immediately alerts the recipient to a critical issue (\"Your EDD debit has been Temporarily DISABLED\"), creating a sense of panic to prompt immediate action.\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The URL provided, `http://myverifylink.cf`, is highly suspicious. It uses a generic, non-official domain (`.cf`) and is not associated with a known, legitimate financial institution.\n3. **Impersonation:** It attempts to impersonate a financial service (\"EDD debit\").\n4. **Call to Action:** It directs the user to click the suspicious link to resolve the supposed problem.\n\nThese elements strongly suggest an attempt to trick the user into entering sensitive information or clicking malware.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01177", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message appears to be a casual, personal communication between two individuals (\"di\" and \"yijue\") regarding a pre-arranged meeting time and location. There are no indicators of phishing, such as urgent demands, suspicious links, requests for personal financial information, or impersonation of a known brand. The language is informal, consistent with a personal text exchange.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01178", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates the Internal Revenue Service (IRS), a common tactic in phishing.\n2. **Urgency and Threat:** It creates a sense of urgency (\"immediately,\" \"avoid potential delays or penalties\") to pressure the recipient into acting without thinking.\n3. **Request for Personal Information:** It explicitly asks the recipient to provide \"accurate personal information.\"\n4. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL, `https://irs.gov.direct-capitals.com`, is highly suspicious. While it contains \"irs.gov,\" the addition of a secondary, unrelated domain (`direct-capitals.com`) indicates it is not the official IRS website and is designed to look legitimate (a technique called URL spoofing).\n\nThe combination of impersonation, urgency, and a suspicious link requesting personal data strongly classifies this as a phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01179", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham. The message is a personal, affectionate inquiry (\"Where are you lover? I need you...\") and contains no elements typically associated with smishing, such as urgency related to a financial threat, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. It appears to be a personal communication.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01180", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message \"Ü ready then call me...\" is very brief and lacks any specific context, urgency, suspicious links, or requests for personal information or payment. The use of \"Ü\" instead of \"You\" is a minor stylistic choice but doesn't inherently indicate malicious intent. Without any further information, the message appears to be a casual, informal communication between known parties.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01182", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The phrase \"tiny reward for u\" is vague and often used as a lure. More critically, it contains a shortened, non-standard URL (`C1t8S.com/YSjV01xa09`). Legitimate businesses typically use their official, recognizable domain names for invoices and rewards, not obscure, shortened links. The combination of an unexpected link following a transactional notification (invoice) is a common tactic to trick users into clicking a malicious link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01183", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates the Internal Revenue Service (IRS), a common tactic in phishing.\n2. **Urgency and Threat:** It creates a sense of urgency (\"immediately,\" \"avoid potential delays or penalties\") and implies a financial benefit that requires immediate action.\n3. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL, `https://irs.gov.secure-fundhub.com`, is highly suspicious. While it contains \"irs.gov,\" the actual domain is `secure-fundhub.com`, which is not the official IRS domain, indicating a spoofed or malicious landing page.\n4. **Request for Personal Information:** It explicitly asks the recipient to \"confirm your information\" via the link, which is a classic phishing request for sensitive data.\n\nThe combination of impersonation, urgency, and a non-official link requesting personal data strongly classifies this as smishing.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01184", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is a simple, conversational question asking about a specific person's involvement in a task (\"clearing cars\"). There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The language is informal and appears to be part of a normal, personal conversation.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01185", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses brand impersonation by referencing \"EDD.BOA,\" suggesting a connection to Bank of America (BOA). It creates a sense of urgency and alarm by stating, \"EDD has disabled this card. ADDITIONAL VERIFICATION REQUIRED.\" Most critically, it includes a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`http://serviceprocesses.me/kfuf3`) which is designed to trick the recipient into clicking and entering sensitive information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01186", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message exhibits characteristics of unsolicited, potentially manipulative communication, often associated with catfishing or romance scams, which can be a form of social engineering similar to smishing, although it doesn't contain typical technical phishing elements like suspicious links or urgent payment requests. The message is highly personal, flattering, and attempts to build an emotional connection quickly (\"LOOK AT AMY URE A BEAUTIFUL, INTELLIGENT WOMAN AND I LIKE U A LOT\"). This unsolicited, intense personal approach is a common tactic used in social engineering scams to gain trust before escalating to a request for money or personal information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01187", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message appears to be a personal, social outreach message. It references a previous interaction on a dating/social app (\"Coffee Meets Bagel\") and proposes a casual meeting. There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The tone is conversational and informal.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01188", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It impersonates a legitimate entity (USPS) to create a sense of urgency regarding a package delivery issue. The request is to click a suspicious link (`https://usps-address-amend.com`), which is not the official USPS domain, and to confirm personal address information. The unusual instructions regarding replying 'Y' and then re-opening the text message are also common tactics used in phishing attempts to trick the user into interacting with the malicious link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
