{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01191", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message \"Babe, I need your advice\" is very brief and lacks any immediate red flags typically associated with smishing, such as urgent demands, suspicious links, or requests for financial information. The use of an affectionate term (\"Babe\") suggests a personal relationship, which is a benign cue. While this message *could* be a precursor to a scam (e.g., \"Babe, I'm in trouble, click this link\"), on its own, it is too generic and lacks the malicious components to be classified as smishing. It reads like a normal, informal text message between partners or close friends.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01192", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. Specifically, it uses a vague, unsolicited \"gift\" as a lure, which is a common social engineering tactic. More critically, it contains a shortened, suspicious URL (`yrt1x.info/RbGWPZ6FRQ`) that is not a standard T-Mobile domain, suggesting an attempt to direct the recipient to a malicious site for credential harvesting or malware installation. The combination of a holiday greeting (creating a positive emotional hook) and an unsolicited link is highly indicative of phishing.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
