{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01213", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues. It uses an informal, personal greeting (\"Hey hun\"), which can be a tactic to lower the recipient's guard. More significantly, it includes a suspicious, shortened, and non-standard URL (`http://kxqghk.com/ROpuoNr`). Legitimate communication from a known contact would typically not direct the recipient to an unknown, randomly generated link for a simple conversation setup. The combination of a personal appeal and a suspicious link strongly suggests an attempt to trick the recipient into clicking a malicious site.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01214", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The greeting \"Hi dear\" is overly familiar and generic, which is a common tactic in phishing attempts. The message promises a job offer (\"Your CV has passed\") and a salary, which is a common lure in job scams. Crucially, it directs the recipient to a WhatsApp link (`https://wa.me/8801909866268`) under the guise of a \"Pruitt Group Business Account.\" While WhatsApp links themselves aren't inherently malicious, in the context of an unsolicited job offer, they are used to initiate contact where the scammer can then extract personal information or request payments. The combination of an unsolicited job offer and a direct link to a personal/business contact channel strongly suggests a deceptive attempt.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01215", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It uses brand impersonation by referencing \"CHASE BANK ALERT.\" It creates a sense of urgency and alarm (\"We detected an unusual attempt...\"). Most critically, it directs the recipient to a suspicious link (`https://secur06-chase-verify.my03.com/?Verify`). The URL is not the official Chase domain and uses obfuscation (e.g., '0' instead of 'o') and a generic, non-official subdomain structure, which is a classic phishing tactic designed to trick users into entering personal information or credentials.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01216", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham.\n\nEvidence: The message is a generic, sentimental, and somewhat flowery message that appears to be a personalized greeting or a chain message, often shared socially. It contains no urgent demands, no suspicious links, no requests for personal information or payment, and does not impersonate a known brand or service in a deceptive manner. The language is informal and conversational, fitting the pattern of non-malicious social messaging.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01217", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message is classified as ham because it is an informal, conversational text message between what appears to be acquaintances or friends. It discusses a change in timing (\"Tmr timin still da same wat\") related to a personal commitment (\"got lesson until 6\"). There are no suspicious cues such as urgency, links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01218", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses a lure (\"Freebie by Home Depot!\") to entice the recipient. Crucially, it contains a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`http://profaneblo.yokohama/DhA4u1`). Legitimate companies like Home Depot would typically use their official domain names for promotions, not a random, unrelated URL. The combination of a high-value offer and a suspicious link is a classic smishing tactic designed to trick users into clicking and potentially entering personal or financial information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01219", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The primary suspicious cues are the unsolicited notification of a large sum of money (\"Your $1000 Stimulus Check for November has been approved\"), which is a common lure in phishing attempts. Furthermore, it includes a suspicious, shortened URL (`ebgicer.com/mDWj392A`) that directs the recipient to an external, likely malicious, site to \"Confirm details.\" Legitimate government or financial institutions typically do not send unsolicited links via SMS to confirm the receipt of funds.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01220", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The offer of \"Quick freebies\" is a common lure used in phishing attempts. More critically, the URL provided, `http://surveysens.yokohama/9vePTP`, is highly suspicious. It uses a non-standard, potentially deceptive domain structure (`surveysens.yokohama`) and is not the official domain for Home Depot, indicating brand impersonation for malicious purposes. The combination of an unsolicited, high-value offer and a suspicious link strongly suggests a phishing attempt designed to steal information or install malware.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01221", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It impersonates a legitimate entity (USPS) to create a sense of urgency (\"Your package cannot be delivered\"). Crucially, it directs the recipient to a suspicious, shortened URL (`https://rb.gy/00dc0`), which is a common tactic used in phishing to hide the true destination of the link. Furthermore, it prompts the user to click the link to resolve an issue, which is a classic social engineering technique used in phishing attacks to harvest personal information or install malware.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01222", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The opening, \"Hey hun, wanna chat and get to know one another?\", is a common tactic used in romance or catfishing scams to build rapport quickly. The inclusion of a suspicious, non-standard URL (`http://cgmfdk.com/E2Ysk70`) is a major red flag, as legitimate personal communication rarely directs users to obscure, randomly generated links for casual conversation. The combination of an overly familiar, unsolicited opening and a suspicious link strongly suggests an attempt to lure the recipient to a malicious site.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01223", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message appears to be a casual, personal text exchange between acquaintances or colleagues. It discusses scheduling (\"Are we doing the norm tomorrow?\"), work/study commitments (\"I finish just a 4.15 cos of st tests\"), and administrative tasks (\"Need to sort library stuff out at some point tomo - got letter from today - access til end march so i better get move on!\"). There are no suspicious elements such as urgent demands, requests for personal financial information, unsolicited links, or impersonation of a known brand.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01226", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message is classified as ham because it is a casual, personal communication describing a physical symptom (sore throat) and contains no elements of phishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. It reads like a normal text exchange between acquaintances.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01227", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several classic signs of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Unsolicited \"Good News\" and Lottery Claims:** The message claims the recipient has been randomly selected as a winner of a large sum of money ($316.3 million), which is a common tactic in lottery scams.\n2. **Urgency/Action Required:** It pressures the recipient to take immediate action (\"To verify your winning kindly send a text to Sandra To claim your winnings\").\n3. **Suspicious Link:** While the provided link is to YouTube, the context of a sudden, unsolicited \"win\" is highly suspicious, and the link is used to drive engagement related to the scam.\n4. **Vague and Overly Broad Claims:** The message is filled with excessive, unrelated, and overly sentimental language about charity, God, and helping various groups, which is designed to build false trust and emotional appeal.\n5. **Request for Personal Action/Information (Implied):** Although it doesn't explicitly ask for bank details in the SMS, it demands a specific action (\"send a text to Sandra\") to \"claim your winnings,\" which is the initial step in a phishing or data-harvesting scheme.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01228", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several classic indicators of smishing. The message uses brand impersonation (\"US POSTAL\") to appear legitimate. It creates a sense of urgency/problem (\"Package cannot be delivered\") to prompt immediate action. Most critically, it directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (\"postalportal.site\") to resolve the issue, which is a common tactic for phishing to steal credentials or personal information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01229", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message appears to be a personal communication, likely from a friend or family member, offering advice or reassurance regarding a health situation (\"sleep the pain is not a surgical emergency\"). There are no indicators of phishing, such as urgent demands, suspicious links, requests for personal information, or brand impersonation. The tone is casual and conversational.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01230", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message is classified as ham because it is a casual, personal greeting between two individuals (\"How was your day? Hey, Kelly! Do you remember me? I'm Monica\"). There are no indicators of phishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, requests for personal information, or brand impersonation.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01232", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The opening, \"Amazing news!, your application has been accepted,\" is designed to create a positive emotional hook to encourage immediate action. The critical piece of evidence is the inclusion of a suspicious, shortened URL (`http://klkln.cc/vnbcbtd`). Legitimate organizations rarely use generic, shortened, or unfamiliar domains for important application updates; they typically direct users to their official, recognizable website. The message implies a required next step (\"Finish the process\") which is a common tactic used in phishing to trick users into clicking the malicious link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
