{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01292", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message creates a sense of urgency (\"If not access could be locked\"), which is a common social engineering tactic. Furthermore, it requests sensitive information (\"confirm DEBIT info and email on file\") and directs the recipient to a shortened, suspicious link (`bit.ly/87VBJ987`) to complete the action. This combination of urgency, request for sensitive data, and a suspicious link strongly suggests a phishing attempt designed to steal credentials or financial information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01293", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It impersonates a well-known brand (Netflix), creates a sense of urgency (\"We have failed 3 times to extend your sub, fix your bill now\"), and directs the recipient to a suspicious, shortened link (`lnkd.in/g7jb5b_7`) to resolve a supposed billing issue. Legitimate companies typically use official app notifications or direct website logins rather than unsolicited SMS links for critical account changes.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
