{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01331", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The primary suspicious cues are the urgent tone (\"Your deliveries have been stopped\") and the presence of a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`uspdelmvsub.info/Resd`). Legitimate delivery services typically use their official domain names and do not usually require users to click a shortened or unfamiliar link to resolve a minor labeling issue. The request implies an action (updating information) that is often used in phishing attempts to harvest data.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01332", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It impersonates a known financial institution, \"Comerica,\" which is a form of brand impersonation. It creates a sense of urgency and concern (\"Your Comerica banking profile has shown some irregularities\"), a common tactic in phishing. Most critically, it directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`http://alert-comerica.com`) rather than the bank's actual domain, requesting the user to \"update your online banking profile,\" which is a request for sensitive information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
