{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01428", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It impersonates a legitimate entity (U.S.P.S.), creates a sense of urgency/problem (\"cannot be delivered due to incomplete address information\"), and directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-official-looking link (`https://uspseth.top`). Legitimate postal services typically do not use unsolicited SMS messages with suspicious links to request address confirmation.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01429", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency/Problem Creation:** It creates a sense of urgency by claiming a package delivery failure due to incomplete address information.\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL, `https://usps.com.uspqq.shop`, is highly suspicious. While it starts with \"usps.com,\" the actual domain is `uspqq.shop`, which is not the official USPS domain and is designed to look like it.\n3. **Request for Action/Information:** It directs the user to click a link to \"confirm your address,\" which is a common tactic to steal personal information.\n4. **Unusual Instructions:** The complex instructions (\"Please reply to 1, then exit the SMS, open the SMS activation link again, or copy the link to Safari browser and open it\") are designed to confuse the user and ensure they interact with the malicious link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01430", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses suggestive and unsolicited content (\"intimate notice,\" \"seeking no-strings partners\"), which is a common tactic in spam or phishing attempts to entice clicks. More critically, it contains a suspicious, shortened, and non-standard URL (`http://hygrjv.com/2oyhOQV`). This link is designed to direct the recipient to an external, potentially malicious site, and the context of the message is highly irregular and unsolicited.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01431", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses brand impersonation by referencing \"WELLS FARGO.\" It creates a sense of urgency and fear (\"your account is suspended,\" \"regain access now!\"). Most critically, it directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`http://VERIFYWELLSFARGO.GA`), which is a common tactic in phishing attacks to steal credentials or personal information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01432", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses brand impersonation by referencing \"BANKOFAMERlCA[EDD]\" (a slight misspelling/variation of the real name). It creates a sense of urgency and alarm by stating a \"failure in processing your WEEKLY BENEFlTS.\" Most critically, it directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (\"BankOfAmericaDebit05.com\") to resolve the supposed issue, which is a classic phishing tactic designed to steal credentials or personal information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01433", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It impersonates a legitimate entity (\"Citizen Bank\") and creates a sense of urgency and fear (\"potential risk,\" \"locked your access as a precaution\"). Crucially, it directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`https://citizen3.swaratechnology.com/indexs.php?inc`), which is a common tactic used in phishing attacks to steal credentials. Legitimate banks typically do not lock accounts and demand immediate login via an unsolicited link in an SMS.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01434", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The message uses vague, urgent language (\"Heads up,\" \"Get your chance now\") to prompt immediate action. It includes a suspicious, non-standard URL (`mtnl.eu///5EyN6`) which is likely designed to mimic a legitimate service but is not the official T-Mobile domain. Furthermore, the message references a \"T-Mobile Limited pass,\" suggesting an unsolicited offer or notification that requires the user to click the link, which is a common tactic in phishing attacks to steal credentials or install malware.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01435", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several characteristics of a sales pitch or unsolicited marketing, which is a form of spam, but it does not contain the typical high-risk indicators of smishing (SMS phishing). Specifically, it does not include:\n1. **Urgency tied to a threat or immediate action:** While it mentions a \"hot market,\" the call to action is a soft inquiry (\"have you thought about making a move?\").\n2. **Suspicious links:** There are no URLs provided.\n3. **Requests for sensitive personal information or payment:** The message is purely informational and conversational, asking a general qualifying question.\n4. **Aggressive brand impersonation:** It introduces itself as a local agent, which is a legitimate business practice, not a deceptive impersonation of a known entity (like a bank or tech company).\n\nBecause the message is a direct, non-threatening sales inquiry without any malicious elements (links, urgent demands for data), it is classified as ham (legitimate marketing/spam, but not phishing).\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01436", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS contains a casual greeting (\"haha hahahahaha\") followed by a physical address and a shortened, suspicious-looking URL. The combination of an informal opening with an unsolicited link and address is a common tactic in smishing to lure the recipient into clicking a malicious link, often disguised as a legitimate location or contact. The URL structure (`stews.mom/JTsRpeLZ`) is not associated with a known, reputable service and appears designed to be deceptive.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01437", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It impersonates a legitimate entity (Bank of America - BOA) and creates a sense of urgency (\"probable association with a system vulnerability,\" \"prevent identity theft\"). Crucially, it directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`https://sec03boa.com/bRZQK`), which is a common tactic used in phishing attacks to steal credentials. Legitimate banks typically do not send unsolicited SMS messages demanding immediate login via a shortened or unusual link to address security vulnerabilities.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01438", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is a simple confirmation of a food order (\"4 tacos + 1 rajas burrito, right?\"). There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. This suggests it is part of an ongoing, legitimate conversation, likely with a friend or a known service provider.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01441", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It uses a promotional lure (\"Congrats! Grab a chance to win prizes\") to entice the recipient. More critically, it contains a suspicious, non-official link (`himeji.sutekinet.info/rank.cgi?mode=link&id=201&url=//nxm.us/ivXmv`). Legitimate companies like T-Mobile typically direct users to their official domain for contests, not to obscure, third-party URLs. The combination of an unsolicited prize notification and a suspicious link is a classic smishing tactic designed to harvest credentials or install malware.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01442", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency/Threat:** The message creates a sense of urgency (\"cannot be delivered,\" \"within 24 hours\") to prompt immediate action.\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The URL provided (`https://usrpxs.top`) is not a recognizable, legitimate domain for a major postal service (like USPS, Royal Mail, etc.) and appears to be a generic, suspicious domain designed for phishing.\n3. **Request for Action/Information:** It pressures the user to click a link to \"confirm your address,\" which is a common tactic to steal personal information.\n4. **Unusual Instructions:** The instructions to \"reply to Y, then exit the text message and open it again to activate the link\" are highly irregular and designed to confuse the user or bypass security checks.\n5. **Brand Impersonation:** It attempts to impersonate a legitimate entity (\"The US Postal team\").\n\nThese elements strongly suggest a malicious attempt to trick the recipient into revealing personal data or installing malware.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
