{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01576", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. Specifically, it claims a financial deduction (\"$404 has been deducted from your account\") which is a common tactic to create urgency and prompt the recipient to check their account or respond. While it provides an order ID, the unsolicited nature of the notification, combined with the generic contact number provided, raises red flags. Legitimate companies usually provide more specific account details or direct users to their official app/website rather than just a phone number in an SMS for such sensitive updates.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01577", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message contains a direct link to a WhatsApp group, which is a common vector for phishing or spam. While the message itself doesn't explicitly demand personal information or payment, the context of being invited to an \"internal information sharing discussion group\" using a generic link is suspicious. Phishers often use such invitations to lure users into groups where they can be targeted with more malicious content, or to gather information about the recipient's interests or affiliations. The lack of any identifying sender information or context makes the invitation inherently untrustworthy.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01578", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing.\n1. **Unsolicited Contact/Impersonation:** The message appears to be from a generic entity (\"C VS customer!\") and unsolicited, which is a common tactic in phishing.\n2. **Incentive/Lure:** The promise of \"payment for your viewpoint\" is a common lure used to entice recipients into clicking a link.\n3. **Suspicious Link:** The URL provided (`http://intriguehardy.directory/TTjI4KRA`) is highly suspicious. It uses a non-standard, potentially disposable domain (`.directory`) and contains random characters, which is typical of malicious shorteners or phishing sites, rather than a legitimate corporate survey platform.\n4. **Vagueness:** The message lacks specific context about who \"C VS\" is or what the survey pertains to.\n\nThese elements strongly suggest an attempt to trick the recipient into clicking a malicious link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01579", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham. The message is a casual communication regarding a pickup time (\"Please give it 2 or i will pick it up on Tuesday evening about 8 if that is ok\"). It lacks any of the typical indicators of smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for sensitive personal information or payment. It appears to be part of an ordinary, informal conversation.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01580", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several suspicious cues indicative of smishing. The message uses a brand name (\"T-MOB\") to create a sense of legitimacy (brand impersonation). It offers a \"gift\" as a reward for \"outstanding payment history,\" which is a common lure in phishing attempts. Most critically, it contains a shortened, suspicious URL (`SG201.com/OwWSP7qcg6`) that directs the recipient to an external site, likely to harvest credentials or personal information. The combination of a reward offer and an unsolicited link is a classic smishing tactic.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01582", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is very casual and conversational. It contains no urgent requests, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The language (\"leh\") suggests an informal, personal conversation, which is characteristic of legitimate, non-malicious communication.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01583", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message claims to be from the \"IRS\" (Internal Revenue Service), which is a common tactic used in phishing attacks to impersonate a trusted authority. Furthermore, it includes a link/attachment reference (\"MOP1QHS.pdf\") and mentions a \"TAX REFUND,\" creating a sense of urgency and incentive to click. Legitimate government agencies rarely send unsolicited tax refund documents via short, cryptic SMS messages with external links.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01584", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing.\n1. **Unsolicited Contact/Vague Greeting:** The greeting \"Dear CV S Shopper!\" is generic and impersonal, suggesting a mass-sent message rather than a personalized communication.\n2. **Incentive/Bait:** The promise of payment for a \"very short survey\" is a common tactic used in phishing scams to entice clicks.\n3. **Suspicious Link:** The URL, `http://limitsucceed.directory/KI5AudhL`, is highly suspicious. It uses a generic, non-official domain (`.directory`) and contains random characters, which is typical of malicious shorteners or phishing sites.\n4. **Lack of Context:** There is no context provided about who is running the survey or why the recipient was selected.\n\nThese elements strongly suggest an attempt to trick the recipient into clicking a malicious link, classifying it as smishing.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01585", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message exhibits several suspicious cues indicative of smishing. The sender ID format \"FRM:\" followed by a URL suggests an automated or potentially spoofed source. The message itself uses high-pressure marketing language (\"Oprah's Biggest Give Away Ever!\") to entice the recipient. Crucially, it contains a suspicious link, `www.OprahDeals.sbs`, which is likely designed to mimic a legitimate Oprah-related site but is used to trick users into clicking, potentially leading to a phishing page to steal personal information or credentials.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01586", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses a seemingly official-sounding sender identifier (\"T-MOBILE\") but directs the recipient to a highly suspicious, non-standard URL (`http://194.87.141.136///E.php`). The URL structure is unusual and does not resemble a legitimate T-Mobile domain. Furthermore, the message implies a transaction or reward (\"THANK YOU!\") and includes a call to action (sending '0' to STOP), which is a common tactic in phishing attempts to trick users into interacting with malicious links or services.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01587", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message contains highly informal, suggestive, and personal language (\"Hey sexy buns!\", \"YM\", \"I think of you\"). There are no indicators of smishing, such as urgent requests, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The tone suggests a personal, casual conversation between acquaintances or romantic interests.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01588", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham. The message is a casual, conversational text in Singlish (\"Anything lor but toa payoh got place 2 walk meh...\"), which translates roughly to \"Anything, but Toa Payoh has a place to walk, right?\". It contains no suspicious elements such as urgency, links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. It appears to be a casual social communication.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01589", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham. The message is a very short, informal personal update (\"Im done. Just studyn in library\"). It contains no suspicious elements such as urgency, links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. It reads like a casual text exchange between friends or acquaintances.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01591", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The primary red flags are the suspicious link, the urgent/authoritative tone mimicking a government agency (IRS), and the request to interact with the link to \"activate\" something. The domain `irs.gov.direct-paying.com` is highly suspicious; while it contains \"irs.gov,\" the actual domain is `direct-paying.com`, not the official IRS domain (`irs.gov`). Furthermore, the instruction to reply \"y\" and then open the link again is a common social engineering tactic used in phishing attacks.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01592", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The sender impersonates the Internal Revenue Service (IRS), a common tactic in phishing attacks. It creates a sense of urgency and incentive (\"You will receive a tax refund of $573.00\"). Crucially, it directs the recipient to a suspicious link: `https://irs.gov.direct-capitals.com`. While the link attempts to mimic the official IRS domain (`irs.gov`), the addition of `.direct-capitals.com` makes it a clear spoofed or malicious domain. Furthermore, it requests the user to \"confirm your information,\" which is a direct request for sensitive personal data.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01594", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It uses aggressive marketing language (\"100% PURE,\" \"powerful & natural\") combined with a direct call to action (\"Get yours now!\"). Most critically, it contains a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`naturalcbdgummy.xyz/RKbHGkiTU9`) which is characteristic of phishing attempts designed to trick users into clicking and potentially entering personal or payment information. While it doesn't explicitly demand sensitive data in the text, the unsolicited nature, the promotional urgency, and the suspicious link strongly suggest a deceptive marketing or phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01595", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is very brief and informal, asking a simple question about receiving picture messages. There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The language is casual, suggesting a personal conversation between known parties.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
