{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01598", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It creates a sense of urgency (\"For your $965.30 refund, we need your confirmation\") and directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`aprpok.com/2pRGFz`) to \"authenticate\" something. Legitimate companies typically use secure, well-known domains for such sensitive actions, and unsolicited refund notifications demanding immediate online authentication via a strange link are a classic phishing tactic.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01599", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It uses brand impersonation (\"AMAZON\") to create a sense of legitimacy, but immediately follows this with a high-pressure tactic (account restriction) to induce urgency. Crucially, it directs the user to a suspicious, shortened link (`Inkd.in/gHghxQ3U`) rather than a known, official Amazon domain, which is a classic phishing technique designed to steal credentials or personal information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
