{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01616", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses a generic, slightly awkward phrasing (\"We're all ears, help us to improve the T-Mobile experience you affiliation\") which is often used in phishing attempts to appear legitimate. Most critically, it contains a suspicious, shortened URL (`https://Isto.me/n29v1`) embedded within a string that also includes what looks like a tracking or internal path (`software/agrieuro/c47604-98571?url=`). Legitimate companies usually provide clear, official links, not convoluted ones pointing to URL shorteners for simple feedback requests. The combination of brand impersonation (T-Mobile) and a suspicious link strongly suggests an attempt to trick the recipient into clicking a malicious link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01617", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses urgent language (\"will-expired-t oday\") to pressure the recipient into immediate action. It also contains a suspicious, shortened URL (`9qr.de/itCwLh`) which is characteristic of phishing attempts designed to direct users to malicious sites. Furthermore, it implies a subscription or billing issue, a common pretext used in phishing scams to trick users into providing payment or personal information. The grammar and phrasing are also unprofessional, which is another common red flag.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01618", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The primary suspicious cues are:\n1. **Urgency:** The message creates a sense of urgency by stating the package \"cannot be delivered\" and demanding action \"within 12 hours.\"\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL, `usps.uspsgu.com`, is highly suspicious. While it attempts to mimic the USPS domain, the inclusion of \"uspsgu\" suggests a typo-squatting or malicious domain designed to look legitimate but is not the official USPS website.\n3. **Request for Information:** The message prompts the recipient to click a link to \"confirm your address,\" which is a common tactic used by phishers to harvest personal information.\n\nThe combination of urgency, a non-official-looking domain, and a request to verify personal details strongly classifies this as a phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01619", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message is classified as ham because it is a casual, conversational text message. It contains no suspicious elements such as urgency, links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. It appears to be a personal apology or explanation from a friend or acquaintance.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01620", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The primary red flag is the URL: `https://irs.gov.tax-helping.com`. While it attempts to mimic the official IRS domain (`irs.gov`), the addition of `.tax-helping.com` makes it a clear phishing domain designed to look legitimate. Furthermore, the message creates a sense of urgency/action by instructing the recipient to reply \"y\" and then open a link, which is a common tactic used in phishing attacks to trick users into clicking malicious links or providing information. The inclusion of the IRS mission statement is a form of brand impersonation intended to build false trust.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01621", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is very brief and appears to be a simple statement of intent or a casual message, lacking any typical indicators of a phishing attempt. There is no urgency, no suspicious links, no brand impersonation, and no request for personal information or payment. The content is benign and conversational.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01622", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several characteristics commonly associated with smishing. Specifically, it uses brand impersonation (Home Depot) to lend credibility to the request. While the request itself (participating in a review) sounds benign, the inclusion of a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`http://flexibleupward.com/5KP7du`) is a major red flag. Legitimate companies usually direct users to their official domains for reviews, not to third-party, obscure links. The urgency is mild (\"by October 12, 2022\"), but the combination of brand spoofing and a suspicious link strongly suggests a phishing attempt designed to harvest personal information or credentials.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01623", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses sensational and exaggerated claims (\"Breakthrough new miracle CBD Gummies that fixes Type-2 immediately,\" \"Drops A1C On The Spot!\"), which is a common tactic in scams to create false urgency and appeal to health anxieties. It promotes a product with an unsolicited, suspicious link (`imperialautumn.com/QNmNNbJ`). Furthermore, the content is highly promotional and unsolicited, characteristic of spam or phishing attempts designed to drive traffic to a dubious website for a purchase or data harvesting.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01624", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message contains a suspicious link (`tinyurl.com/y22vnsel`) and an unexpected monetary amount (`848.77`) associated with a name (\"Michael\"). The use of a URL shortener is a common tactic in smishing attacks to obscure the true destination of the link, which is often malicious. The message lacks any context to explain why this amount is being sent or what the link pertains to, creating a sense of ambiguity and potential urgency/deception.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01625", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is a very short, casual confirmation of a plan (\"Yeah sure I'll leave in a min\"). It contains no suspicious elements such as urgency, links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. It reads like a normal, informal text exchange between people who know each other.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01626", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham. The message is a very casual, abbreviated conversation between two people (\"7 lor...\", \"Change 2 suntec...\", \"Wat time u coming?\"). It lacks any of the typical indicators of smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. It appears to be a routine, informal communication between acquaintances.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01627", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The primary suspicious cues are:\n1. **Urgency/Problem Creation:** It creates a sense of urgency by claiming a package delivery failure due to incomplete information.\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL (`https://uspostal.after-salescenter.xyz`) is highly suspicious. It attempts to mimic a legitimate postal service (USPS) but uses a non-official, complex, and unusual domain structure (`after-salescenter.xyz`), which is a common tactic in phishing.\n3. **Request for Action/Information:** It directs the user to click a link to \"confirm your address,\" which is a common vector for harvesting personal information.\n\nThe combination of a delivery notification pretext, urgency, and a clearly non-official, suspicious link strongly classifies this as smishing.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01628", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The sender ID, \"KatieT881,\" is generic and not associated with a known, legitimate contact or service. The message contains a shortened, suspicious-looking URL (`http://gfxmth.com/6vDFZpW`) which is designed to trick the recipient into clicking. Furthermore, the context—a notification about a \"bang buddy proposal\"—is vague and potentially designed to entice the user into clicking the link out of curiosity or perceived relevance. There is no clear, legitimate context provided for this unsolicited link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01629", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message is a brief, conversational reply that seems to be continuing a previous discussion about \"safety aspects and some other issues.\" There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The tone is informal and conversational, suggesting it is part of a legitimate, ongoing dialogue.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01630", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses a lure (\"5 Minute Survey For $100 Gift\") to entice the recipient, which is a common tactic in phishing. More critically, the provided link, `http://operateepidemic.com/sotelQ`, is highly suspicious. It does not belong to Home Depot, suggesting brand impersonation is being used to trick the user into clicking a malicious or deceptive site. The combination of a high-value incentive and a non-official, suspicious URL points directly to a phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01631", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency and Threat:** The message creates a sense of urgency (\"Recovery your account immediately,\" \"within 48 hours to avoid permanent suspension\").\n2. **Request for Action/Information:** It demands immediate action regarding billing information, which is a common tactic in phishing.\n3. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL (`http://www.netflix.com:911@s955341113.onlinehome.us/ven`) is highly suspicious. While it attempts to mimic \"netflix.com,\" the actual domain (`onlinehome.us`) is unrelated and the structure is convoluted, indicating a malicious redirection.\n4. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates a well-known brand (Netflix) to gain trust.\n\nThese elements strongly suggest an attempt to trick the recipient into clicking a malicious link to steal credentials or personal information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01632", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues. The sender, \"Phoebe,\" is using a casual, personal tone, which can be a tactic to lower the recipient's guard. The link provided, `http://tmgcrg.com/5rBu34D`, is a shortened, non-standard URL that does not belong to a recognizable, legitimate service. Furthermore, the vague nature of the request (\"Did you view my invite? I need to get out. Let me know:\") combined with the suspicious link suggests the message is attempting to trick the recipient into clicking a malicious link, which is characteristic of smishing.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01633", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It impersonates a legitimate entity (USPS) and creates a sense of urgency/problem (\"could not be delivered due to incomplete address information\"). Crucially, it directs the recipient to click a suspicious link (`https://usps.uipackage.com`). While the domain attempts to mimic USPS, the inclusion of \"uipackage.com\" suggests it is not the official USPS domain. Furthermore, it requests the user to confirm personal information (address) via this external link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01634", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency/Problem Creation:** It creates a sense of urgency by claiming a package delivery has failed due to incomplete address information.\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The provided link, `https://uspsverify.org`, is highly suspicious. Official USPS domains are typically `usps.com`. The use of \"verify\" and a slightly altered domain is a common phishing tactic.\n3. **Request for Action/Information:** It directs the user to click a link to \"confirm your address,\" which is a direct attempt to harvest personal information.\n4. **Unusual Instructions:** The complex instructions (\"Please reply to 1, then exit theSMS, open the SMS activationlink again, or copy the link toSafari browser and open it\") are designed to confuse the user and ensure they interact with the malicious link.\n\nThe combination of impersonating a trusted entity (USPS), creating a delivery problem, and directing the user to a non-official, suspicious link to provide personal data strongly classifies this as smishing.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
