{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01641", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses urgent and alarming language (\"Important message,\" \"Card Locked\") to create a sense of panic, which is a common social engineering tactic. It impersonates a legitimate financial institution (VISA DEBIT Card). Furthermore, while it provides a phone number, the context of a sudden \"lock\" combined with the generic nature of the alert suggests an attempt to trick the recipient into calling a fraudulent support line to \"resolve\" the issue, often leading to the theft of personal or financial information. The inclusion of an \"Alert Code\" adds a layer of false authenticity.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01642", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The primary suspicious cues are the unsolicited offer of money (\"Netflix is Reimbursing you $100\"), the creation of a false sense of urgency (\"Time Left to Claim (48) hrs\"), and the presence of a highly suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`http://carecondemn.com/BYMRKW`). Legitimate companies like Netflix typically do not send unsolicited messages promising large reimbursements via SMS that require clicking an unfamiliar link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
