{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01775", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It impersonates a legitimate financial institution (Wells Fargo) and creates a sense of urgency (\"is on Hold\"). Crucially, it directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`https://wellsfargo-zellepayment.com`) to take action, which is a classic phishing tactic designed to steal credentials or personal information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01776", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is very casual and conversational. It contains no urgent language, no suspicious links, no brand impersonation, and no requests for personal information or payment. The content suggests a simple rescheduling or continuation of a personal conversation.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01777", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits characteristics of a personal, informal communication (\"Hey babe, what r you up to? I'm home alone, u want to come by?\"). However, the inclusion of a suspicious, shortened, and non-descript URL (`http://zvsjxy.com/4qAcRgK`) immediately following a personal invitation is a significant red flag. While the initial text is benign, the unsolicited link is a common tactic used in smishing attacks (often called \"romance scams\" or \"sextortion\" when used in this context) to trick the recipient into clicking a malicious site, which could lead to malware installation or credential theft. The combination of a personal lure and a suspicious link strongly suggests malicious intent.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01779", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message appears to be informal, conversational, and uses colloquial language (\"lor,\" \"xy\"). It does not contain any typical indicators of smishing, such as urgent demands, suspicious links, requests for personal information, or impersonation of a known brand. The content suggests a casual social arrangement or update between acquaintances.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01780", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several characteristics commonly associated with smishing. The primary suspicious cue is the inclusion of a shortened, non-descript URL (`ssg.gl/27130d`). While the message attempts to establish legitimacy by mentioning \"Loopback Rewards,\" the immediate call to action is to click an external link to \"download our mobile app,\" which is a common tactic used by phishing scams to trick users into installing malware or entering credentials. The inclusion of \"Reply STOP to OPT OUT\" is a standard feature in legitimate marketing SMS, but it does not negate the risk posed by the suspicious link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01781", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing or a scam. Specifically, it uses vague, urgent-sounding language (\"join,\" \"investment plan,\" \"1K-10K usd a day\") to entice the recipient. It includes a non-standard, potentially malicious link format (`whatsapp://chat/?code=...`) which is often used in phishing attempts to direct users to a specific chat or platform under false pretenses. Furthermore, the mention of high, guaranteed returns (\"1K-10K usd a day\") is a classic hallmark of investment scams. The message lacks any legitimate context or verifiable sender information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01782", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is very short and lacks any discernible context, urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. It appears to be a very casual, incomplete, or conversational reply (\"Yes, my reg is Ciao!\"). Without any further context, there are no strong indicators of phishing or smishing.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01783", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several characteristics of smishing. It uses promotional language (\"We found Rent to Own homes near you,\" \"Easy approval\") combined with a direct call to action via a suspicious-looking URL (`approved.rent2owncheaps.com`). The message is unsolicited and attempts to lure the recipient into clicking a link to view housing opportunities, which is a common tactic in phishing scams to steal personal or financial information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01784", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message appears to be a casual, personal communication between two individuals. It contains informal language (\"Hai ana tomarrow am coming on morning,\" \"ll be there in sathy\"), discusses a planned meeting or trip (\"coming on morning,\" \"go to RTO office\"), and ends with a simple request for a reply. There are no indicators of smishing, such as urgent demands, suspicious links, requests for financial information, or impersonation of a known entity.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01785", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It impersonates a legitimate government body (HMRC) to create a sense of urgency and entice the recipient. The core request is to click a link to claim a supposed refund, which is a common tactic in phishing. Furthermore, the URL provided, `https://gov-tax.refundpr.com/`, is highly suspicious. While it contains \"gov\" and \"tax,\" the domain structure (`refundpr.com`) is not the official HMRC domain, indicating a spoofed or malicious site designed to harvest personal or financial information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01786", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message contains only numerical data (\"Wewa is 130. Iriver 255. All 128 mb.\") and does not exhibit any typical characteristics of smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The content appears to be a brief, coded, or technical update, which is consistent with a legitimate, albeit cryptic, communication (ham).\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01787", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses unusual characters and substitutions (e.g., \"FEDER4L,\" \"W4S_B4N,\" \"F!x\") to mimic legitimate text while evading spam filters, which is a common tactic in phishing. It creates a sense of urgency and implies a problem (\"RETURN W4S_B4N\") that needs immediate resolution. Most critically, it contains a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`sl.ut.ac.id/1spp`) and prompts the recipient to click it, which is the primary goal of smishing attacks to steal credentials or install malware.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01788", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message appears to be a personal communication from someone named Cess, introducing a new number and apologizing for a potential wrong number. There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The tone is casual and apologetic, suggesting a legitimate, albeit slightly awkward, personal outreach.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
