{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01793", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham. The message is a casual, personal communication between family members (\"Hi mom\") regarding a change in plans (\"we might be back later than\"). There are no suspicious cues such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The context is entirely benign and conversational.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01794", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing.\n1. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates the Internal Revenue Service (IRS), a common tactic in phishing attacks.\n2. **Urgency and Threat:** It creates a sense of urgency (\"immediately,\" \"avoid potential delays or penalties\") to pressure the recipient into acting without thinking.\n3. **Request for Personal Information:** It explicitly asks the recipient to provide \"accurate personal information.\"\n4. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL, `https://irs.gov.direct-capitals.com`, is highly suspicious. While it contains \"irs.gov,\" the domain structure is clearly not the official IRS website and is designed to look legitimate (a technique called typosquatting or domain spoofing).\n5. **Unusual Instructions:** The instructions on how to access the link (\"Please reply 'y', then exit the message, reopen the message to activate the link, or copy the link and open it in the Safari browser\") are convoluted and designed to confuse the user or bypass standard security warnings.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01795", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing.\n1. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates the Internal Revenue Service (IRS), a common tactic in phishing attacks.\n2. **Urgency and Threat:** It creates a sense of urgency (\"immediately,\" \"avoid potential delays or penalties\") to pressure the recipient into acting without thinking.\n3. **Request for Personal Information:** It explicitly asks the recipient to \"provide accurate personal information,\" which is the primary goal of phishing.\n4. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL, `https://irs.gov.direct-capitals.com`, is highly suspicious. While it contains \"irs.gov,\" the domain structure is clearly manipulated and does not point to the official IRS website.\n5. **Unusual Instructions:** The instructions on how to access the link (\"Please reply 'y', then exit the message, reopen the message to activate the link, or copy the link and open it in the Safari browser\") are convoluted and designed to confuse the user or bypass security warnings.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01796", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message is classified as ham because it is an informal, conversational text exchange between two parties. It contains no suspicious elements such as urgency, links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The content is personal and casual.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01797", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham. The message is very informal, uses abbreviations (\"Ya ok,\" \"vl,\" \"c,\" \"witin,\" \"il\"), and appears to be a casual, conversational update regarding a future reply. There are no suspicious cues such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01798", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham. The message is a casual, conversational inquiry referencing a social media platform (\"Facebook\") and is not making any urgent demands, requesting personal information, or containing suspicious links. It appears to be a message between acquaintances.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01799", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message appears to be a personal communication between family members (\"Umma\" suggests a mother or close relative). It contains personal updates about travel plans (\"our life will begin from qatar\") and a request for prayer, which are benign elements of personal conversation. There are no suspicious cues such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01800", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several benign cues. It is an informal, conversational message discussing a social event (\"party good the other night?\"), personal plans (\"get home tomorrow at 5ish\"), and a shared experience (\"relaxing time ever!\"). There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment, which are hallmarks of smishing.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01801", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is highly unusual and contains nonsensical or misspelled words (\"lanre fakeye's Eckankar details\"). It does not exhibit typical smishing characteristics like urgent threats, requests for immediate payment, or links to suspicious websites. Instead, it reads like a fragmented, possibly accidental, or highly informal personal communication. The lack of any clear malicious intent or standard phishing indicators suggests it is likely a benign, albeit strange, message.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01802", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The use of a \"Security Warning\" combined with an immediate, alarming claim (\"EBT-Card is currently Frozen!\") creates a sense of urgency designed to panic the recipient. Furthermore, it includes a call to action that directs the user to contact a specific support number, which is a common tactic used by scammers to trick victims into revealing personal information or providing payment details over the phone. The message is unsolicited and highly suspicious in nature.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01803", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The message is promotional in nature, advertising \"vision correction procedures\" from \"Clearview Laser.\" The presence of a shortened, non-standard URL (`https://urlsr.com/LY9llxL`) is a major red flag, as these links are often used to direct users to malicious or phishing websites. While it doesn't contain extreme urgency or an explicit request for immediate payment, the unsolicited nature of the advertisement combined with the suspicious link strongly suggests a phishing attempt designed to capture personal information or drive traffic to a deceptive site.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01804", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message is classified as ham because it is an informal, conversational text between what appears to be acquaintances. It contains no suspicious elements such as urgency, links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The language is casual (\"Aight,\" \"mu\"), suggesting a personal context.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01805", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing.\n1. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates USPS, a legitimate service.\n2. **Urgency/Problem Creation:** It creates a sense of urgency and a problem (\"cannot be delivered due to incomplete address information\") to prompt immediate action.\n3. **Suspicious Link:** The provided link, `https://uspsusa-us.com`, is highly suspicious. While it uses \"usps,\" the domain structure is not the official USPS domain (which is typically usps.com), suggesting a phishing attempt designed to look legitimate.\n4. **Request for Action:** It directs the user to click a link to \"confirm your address,\" which is a common tactic to harvest personal information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01806", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several classic signs of a potential scam, classifying it as smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Unsolicited Offer/High Reward:** The promise of guaranteed, high daily profits ($5,000-$8,000) and a chance to win a large cash bonus is an unrealistic lure often used in financial scams.\n2. **Urgency/Exclusivity:** The language suggests an exclusive \"financial insider discussion group,\" creating a sense of FOMO (Fear of Missing Out).\n3. **Suspicious Link:** While the link leads to WhatsApp (which is a legitimate platform), the context of an unsolicited message promising massive, guaranteed returns via a link is highly suspicious and is the mechanism used to direct the user into a potentially malicious or high-pressure environment.\n4. **Request for Action:** It prompts the user to reply and click a link to gain access to this promised wealth.\n\nThe message lacks any legitimate context (e.g., from a known financial institution or friend) and relies entirely on exaggerated financial promises to entice the recipient.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01809", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It uses a sense of urgency (\"ALERT\") regarding a specific, unauthorized transaction ($48.00 at Uber). It impersonates a legitimate financial institution (NFCU). Most critically, it directs the recipient to a suspicious link (`http://nfcucardservices.org`) to resolve the supposed issue, which is a classic tactic used in phishing to steal credentials or install malware.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01811", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is a short, emotionally charged, and somewhat vague statement. It does not contain any typical indicators of smishing, such as urgent requests, suspicious links, requests for personal information, or brand impersonation. The language is personal and reflective, suggesting it could be a personal message or a general, non-malicious broadcast.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01812", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. It uses a friendly, yet unsolicited, approach (\"Hi, I'm Justin!\") to introduce a high-value, time-sensitive offer (\"government credit that could be worth enough money to keep your business running\"). The message creates a sense of urgency and opportunity (\"Don't let the time change throw off your business's finances,\" \"Take advantage of...\"). Furthermore, it prompts the recipient to respond with a keyword (\"YES for info\"), which is a common tactic used in phishing to capture contact information or direct the user to a malicious link (though no link is present in this snippet, the call to action is highly suspicious). The offer of free, substantial government money is a classic lure in financial scams.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01813", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message contains no typical indicators of smishing, such as urgent demands, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. Instead, it reads like a personal, informal message expressing emotional distress and making a casual social comment about the weekend. The language is conversational and lacks the malicious intent characteristic of phishing attempts.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01814", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is a very casual, conversational greeting and question. It contains no urgency, no suspicious links, no brand impersonation, and no requests for personal information or payment. The tone is informal and friendly.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01815", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several characteristics commonly associated with smishing. It mimics a legitimate bank notification (\"Transaction Alerts: your B O A card was used...\"). The inclusion of a specific, large transaction amount and date creates a sense of urgency and alarm, prompting the recipient to act immediately. While it provides a phone number instead of a direct link, the intent is to trick the user into calling a potentially fraudulent number to \"resolve\" the supposed unauthorized charge, which is a common tactic in phishing. The impersonation of a known brand (Bank of America - B O A) is a key indicator.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01818", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses a format that mimics an official alert (\"FRM:ChaseBanking-Alert-3887 SUBJ:CALL: 812.809.4904 Now! MSG:MSG 17001 Account.Locked!\"). The use of \"ChaseBanking-Alert\" suggests brand impersonation (Chase Bank). The content itself creates a sense of extreme urgency (\"Now!\") and threatens a negative consequence (\"Account.Locked!\"), which is a classic social engineering tactic used in phishing attacks to prompt immediate, unthinking action. While there is no explicit link provided in this snippet, the combination of brand impersonation, urgency, and a threat of account lockout strongly suggests an attempt to trick the recipient into calling the provided number or clicking a subsequent link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01819", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency:** The message imposes a strict 12-hour deadline (\"within 12 hours\").\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The URL provided, `https://usps.address-trace.shop`, is highly suspicious. While it attempts to impersonate USPS, the domain name (`address-trace.shop`) is not an official USPS domain and is designed to look legitimate while directing users to a potentially malicious site.\n3. **Request for Action/Information:** It pressures the recipient to click a link to \"confirm your address,\" which is a common tactic to harvest personal information.\n4. **Unusual Instructions:** The complex instructions (\"Please reply to Y, then exit the SMS, open the SMS activation link again, or copy the link to Safari browser and open it\") are designed to confuse the user and guide them into a specific, potentially compromised, workflow.\n\nThese elements strongly suggest a phishing attempt rather than a legitimate communication from USPS.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01820", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message exhibits several characteristics of spam or unsolicited content, but it does not fit the typical profile of smishing (SMS phishing). Smishing usually involves malicious intent, such as tricking the recipient into clicking a link or providing sensitive information (like passwords or credit card numbers) under false pretenses (e.g., \"Your account has been suspended, click here to verify\").\n\nThis message is a bizarre, rambling, and nonsensical piece of text, likely intended as a joke, a form of spam/bot message, or simply random noise. It contains no urgent requests, no suspicious links, and no attempt to impersonate a legitimate service or brand to steal data. The language is heavily abbreviated and informal, which is a common trait of low-effort spam, but the *content* itself is not phishing.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01821", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The message is an \"Inbound friend solicitation\" from an unknown or suspicious-looking handle (\"WetSara002\"). Crucially, it includes a shortened, non-standard URL (`http://dcydpl.com/grguJq2`) which is a common tactic used in phishing to hide the true destination of the link. The instruction to \"Respond now\" creates a sense of urgency, pressuring the recipient to click without thinking.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01822", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency and Threat:** The message creates a sense of immediate crisis (\"Your Amazon Account has been locked,\" \"Recover your account immediately,\" \"within 48 hours to avoid permanent suspension\").\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL (`http://s953190961.onlinehome.us/?_recovrAccount`) is highly suspicious. It does not use the official Amazon domain and appears to be a generic, potentially malicious domain designed to mimic a legitimate service.\n3. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates a well-known brand (Amazon) to trick the recipient.\n4. **Request for Action/Credentials:** It pressures the user to click a link to \"recover\" the account, which is the typical mechanism used by phishers to steal login credentials.\n\nThese elements strongly suggest a phishing attempt rather than a legitimate communication from Amazon.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01823", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits numerous spelling and grammatical errors (\"Do u konw waht is rael FRIENDSHIP Im gving yuo an exmpel: Jsut ese tihs msg.. Evrey splleing of tihs msg is wrnog.. Bt sitll yuo can raed it wihtuot ayn mitsake.. GOODNIGHT &amp; HAVE A NICE SLEEP..SWEET DREAMS..\"). This highly informal, deliberately misspelled style is characteristic of spam or low-effort phishing attempts designed to look like a casual, perhaps juvenile, message, but it lacks the typical high-pressure tactics (urgency, suspicious links, requests for payment/info) associated with classic smishing. Instead, it appears to be a form of spam or a deliberately nonsensical/meme-like message, which falls under the broader category of unsolicited, non-legitimate communication, but it does not fit the specific definition of *smishing* (SMS phishing targeting credentials/money). Given the options, it is not legitimate communication (ham). However, since it doesn't contain the hallmarks of a *phishing* attempt, classifying it strictly as smishing is inaccurate. It is best categorized as spam/junk, but if forced to choose between smishing and ham, it leans away from ham. Since it is not attempting to trick the user into giving up data or money, it is not smishing. Therefore, it is not ham either, but in the context of this binary choice, it is not legitimate. We will classify it as not ham.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01824", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message consists only of a shortened, suspicious-looking URL: `http://broug.training/lu89eN`. There is no accompanying text, but the presence of an unsolicited, non-standard link is a major red flag. The domain \"broug.training\" does not immediately suggest a legitimate, well-known service, and the use of a shortened/obscure link is a common tactic in phishing attempts to hide the true destination. The lack of context or sender identification further increases suspicion.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01825", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several classic signs of smishing. The use of high-pressure language (\"Stock won't last, get in quick!\") creates a false sense of urgency, a common tactic in phishing. Furthermore, the link provided is a shortened URL (`https://bit.ly/3xmZNuq`), which obscures the true destination of the link, making it impossible to verify the legitimacy of the offer without clicking. While it appears to be a sales promotion, the combination of urgency and the suspicious link strongly suggests an attempt to trick the recipient into clicking a malicious link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01826", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham. The content is a casual, conversational statement about cricket players (Gautam Gambhir and Virender Sehwag) being out of the ODI series. There are no suspicious cues such as urgency, links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. It appears to be a piece of informal chat or commentary.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01828", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is very brief and lacks any clear context. It contains a directive (\"Pls go there today\") and a demand (\"I dont want any excuses\"). The inclusion of the placeholder `&lt;#&gt;` suggests a missing or obscured link/location, which is a common tactic in phishing attempts to hide the destination. The tone is demanding and urgent, which are classic social engineering tactics used in smishing to pressure the recipient into immediate action without critical thought. While it could be a legitimate, albeit aggressive, message from a known contact, the ambiguity and the suggestive nature of the placeholder strongly lean towards a suspicious or malicious intent.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01829", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The sender impersonates the Internal Revenue Service (IRS), a common tactic in phishing. It creates a sense of urgency and incentive (\"You will receive a tax refund... but cannot process it\"). Most critically, it directs the recipient to a suspicious link (`https://irs.gov.safe-paying.com`). While the domain *starts* with \"irs.gov,\" the addition of \"safe-paying.com\" makes it a clear attempt to mimic the official site while routing the user to a malicious or deceptive third-party domain. Furthermore, it requests the confirmation of \"incomplete personal information,\" which is a classic phishing lure designed to steal sensitive data.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01830", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message exhibits several characteristics that suggest it is not a typical smishing attempt, but rather a personal, aggressive, and potentially harassing message. It does not contain the common hallmarks of smishing, such as:\n1. **Suspicious Links:** There are no URLs provided.\n2. **Urgency/Threats related to financial/account compromise:** The threat is personal (\"I will report you to your supervisor\") rather than related to banking, account hacking, or immediate financial loss.\n3. **Brand Impersonation:** It is not impersonating a known company or service.\n4. **Requests for Personal Info/Payment:** It does not ask for login credentials, passwords, or money.\n\nInstead, the message is a direct, confrontational communication regarding a perceived privacy violation. While it is aggressive, it does not fit the definition of \"smishing\" (SMS phishing, which aims to trick the recipient into giving up sensitive information or money via a deceptive SMS). Therefore, it is classified as ham (legitimate communication, albeit hostile).\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01832", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham. The message is a very casual, personal, and conversational text. It contains no elements typically associated with smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The use of an affectionate term (\"princess\") suggests a pre-existing, informal relationship between the sender and receiver.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01833", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several characteristics of a personal, informal conversation rather than a typical smishing attempt. There is no urgency, no suspicious links, no brand impersonation, and no request for sensitive personal information or payment via an external, suspicious method. The content suggests a personal debt negotiation between two parties who likely have an existing relationship (\"Hi, sorry I was in Australia and I'm back\"). The mention of a specific time and location (\"off work at 7pm in Encinitas\") further grounds it in a personal context.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01834", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It creates a sense of urgency (\"account status has been restricted\") to prompt immediate action. Furthermore, the link provided, \"http://eddbofaprocess.info,\" is highly suspicious. It uses a non-official, generic-looking domain name, which is a common tactic used by phishers to mimic legitimate services. The message also requests the user to \"update your information,\" which is a classic precursor to harvesting personal or financial data.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
