{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01841", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It uses brand impersonation by referencing \"PNC,\" a legitimate bank. It creates a sense of urgency and alarm (\"Alart: We recently noticed a suspicious operation on your account, for the safety of your account\"). Most critically, it directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`pnc.srcrwanda.com`), which is a common tactic in phishing attacks to steal credentials or personal information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01842", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several suspicious cues indicative of smishing. The message uses vague, overly promotional language (\"Holiday little surprise gift,\" \"reciprocal loyalty\") which is a common tactic in phishing to entice clicks. More critically, it contains a suspicious, shortened URL (`NU711.iNfo/ph9mqgxzrR`). This link is designed to direct the recipient to a malicious or deceptive website, likely to steal personal information or credentials under the guise of receiving a \"gift.\" The lack of specific context or a clear, verifiable sender identity further raises suspicion.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01843", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The primary cues are the sense of urgency (\"Your debit has been barred from use\"), the threat of service interruption, and the inclusion of a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`https://www.assistance-usrprocess.com`). Legitimate banks typically do not send urgent SMS messages directing users to click an external, unfamiliar link to resolve security issues; they usually advise logging into the official app or website directly. The domain name itself appears designed to mimic a legitimate service but is clearly constructed for phishing.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01844", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message contains casual conversation (\"Nvm...\", \"leh\") and discusses a personal situation (wearing sport shoes, being late). There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. Therefore, it appears to be a normal, legitimate text exchange.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01845", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It impersonates a legitimate service (FedEx) to create a sense of urgency and prompt action. The inclusion of a tracking code adds a layer of perceived legitimacy. Most critically, the link provided, `e3fmr.info/onAyXsVfomA`, is a suspicious, non-official domain, which is a hallmark of phishing attempts designed to steal information or install malware. The request is to \"set delivery preferences,\" which is a common pretext used in smishing attacks.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01846", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency/Threat:** The message creates a sense of immediate crisis (\"Your Account has been limited,\" \"We have locked your Account ID\").\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The URL provided (`http://s950928458.onlinehome.us`) is highly irregular, does not resemble an official PayPal domain, and uses a generic, suspicious structure.\n3. **Request for Action/Verification:** It demands the user click a link to \"verify your Account\" to resolve the supposed issue, which is a common tactic to steal credentials.\n4. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates \"Paypal service,\" a well-known financial institution, to gain trust.\n\nThese elements collectively point to a phishing attempt designed to trick the recipient into providing sensitive information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01847", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It impersonates a legitimate entity (\"BANK OF AMERICA EDD ALERT SERVICES\") and creates a sense of urgency (\"locked and under review,\" \"reactivate and reopen your card now\"). Crucially, it directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`http://resolveeddcards.com/reopennow`) under the guise of a \"secured website link\" to provide sensitive account information. Legitimate banks typically do not initiate account lockouts via unsolicited SMS demanding immediate action through a third-party link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01848", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing/scamming behavior. The message uses vague, fragmented language (\"adjustment, bunch chair click to a,join, whatsapp://chat/?code=Jhg4JbfHxgplRPfeSxakwK\") which is often a tactic to bypass spam filters or appear less formal. Crucially, it promotes an unsolicited investment opportunity (\"tcoin\" analysis group) promising extremely high, unrealistic returns (\"1K-10K usd a day\"). This promise of quick, massive wealth is a classic hallmark of investment scams. The inclusion of a direct link/protocol (`whatsapp://chat/?code=...`) is used to direct the user to a platform where the scam can be executed, often leading to a phishing attempt or a request for cryptocurrency/personal details.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01850", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It uses a lure of a large, unexpected financial reward (\"$1000 October Stimulus Check\") to create urgency and entice the recipient. Crucially, it directs the user to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`leftfxn.com/aEdebm5r`) under the guise of \"confirming details,\" which is a classic tactic for credential harvesting or malware delivery. Legitimate government or financial institutions typically do not initiate contact via unsolicited SMS with such high-value offers requiring immediate link clicks.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01851", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message contains informal language and appears to be a casual, personal conversation between two individuals. There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The language is colloquial and does not resemble typical phishing attempts.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01852", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham. The message is a very casual, conversational inquiry (\"I know you are serving. I mean what are you doing now.\"). It lacks any of the typical hallmarks of smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. It appears to be a personal check-in from someone who knows the recipient.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01853", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits characteristics of a social engineering attempt, specifically a potential romance or hookup scam, which falls under the broader category of smishing or phishing, even if it doesn't explicitly ask for financial details in this instance. The primary suspicious cue is the inclusion of a shortened, non-standard, and untrustworthy URL (`http://ypzvtd.com/OmwsYW8`) immediately following a casual, personal invitation. Legitimate social invitations rarely include unsolicited, suspicious links. The link is designed to lure the recipient to a malicious site, making it a phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01854", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The sender ID, \"IRS\\_TAX\\_RE-FUND\\_OY2PTO2,\" attempts to impersonate a legitimate government entity (IRS) while including suspicious, non-standard characters and a random string (\"OY2PTO2\"). The message itself is a direct link to a PDF document, which is a common tactic used in phishing to trick recipients into downloading malware or revealing sensitive information under the guise of an official document (a tax refund). The combination of impersonation and an unsolicited document link is highly suspicious.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01856", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The sender impersonates the Internal Revenue Service (I.R.S.), a common tactic in phishing. It creates a sense of urgency and incentive (\"You will receive a tax refund... but cannot process it...\"). Most critically, the link provided, `https://irs.gov.safe-paying.com`, is highly suspicious. While it attempts to mimic the official IRS domain (`irs.gov`), the addition of `.safe-paying.com` makes it a clear domain spoof or a malicious third-party site designed to harvest information, rather than the legitimate IRS website. Furthermore, it requests the user to take action (provide personal information) via the link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01858", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing.\n1. **Urgency/Threat:** It creates a sense of urgency and potential loss (\"temporarily locked due to unusual activity\").\n2. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates a legitimate bank, RBC Canada.\n3. **Suspicious Link:** The URL provided, `http://RBC-CANADA-Verify-Secure-Online.Com`, is highly suspicious. Legitimate banks use their official, well-known domain names (e.g., `rbc.com`), not variations with hyphens and extra words appended to a generic domain structure. The use of `http` instead of `https` is also a red flag for a financial institution.\n4. **Request for Action:** It directs the user to click a link to \"Regain Access,\" which is a common tactic to steal credentials.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01859", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message consists only of a shortened URL: `https://b.link/h21bc br7ca2`. There is no accompanying text, context, urgency, or mention of a specific brand. The presence of a shortened, non-descriptive link is a common tactic used in smishing attacks to hide the true destination of the link. Without any context, the message is highly suspicious and fits the pattern of a phishing attempt designed to trick the recipient into clicking the link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01861", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message \"Nope... C ü then...\" is a very short, informal text exchange. It contains no links, no requests for personal information or payment, no sense of urgency, and does not impersonate any brand. It appears to be a casual, conversational reply between two people who know each other.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
