{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01893", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. It uses brand impersonation (CVS) to appear legitimate, and it includes a suspicious, non-standard URL (`http://catchingbudding.directory/8BWT1REM`). The promise of a large, unsolicited reward (\"up to 100$\") creates a sense of urgency or temptation, which is a common tactic in phishing attempts to trick users into clicking the link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01894", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency and Threat:** The message creates a sense of immediate crisis (\"Your Amazon Account has been locked,\" \"Recover your account immediately,\" \"within 48 hours to avoid permanent suspension\").\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL (`http://s953495841.onlinehome.us/fix?_recovrAccount`) is highly suspicious. It does not use the official Amazon domain and appears to be a generic, potentially malicious domain designed to mimic a legitimate service.\n3. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates a well-known brand (Amazon) to trick the recipient.\n4. **Request for Action:** It demands immediate action via a link, which is a common tactic in phishing attacks to steal credentials.\n\nThese elements strongly suggest the message is an attempt at phishing rather than a legitimate communication from Amazon.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01895", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. Specifically, it offers an unsolicited \"gift\" as a reward for a \"payment record,\" which is a common lure in phishing attempts. More critically, it contains a shortened, suspicious URL (`n5d4q.info/KmxyTy5uNN`). Legitimate companies rarely send unsolicited gift links via SMS, and the combination of a vague reward and an unknown domain strongly suggests a phishing attempt designed to trick the recipient into clicking the link and potentially entering personal or financial information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01896", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The primary suspicious cues are the urgent nature of the delivery issue, the request to click an external link to resolve the problem, and the highly suspicious URL itself (`https://usp-swexy.top`). The domain name is not associated with any major, legitimate courier service, suggesting it is a phishing attempt designed to steal personal information or credentials.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01897", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. Specifically, it uses a vague, unsolicited \"gift\" as a lure, which is a common social engineering tactic. More critically, it contains a shortened, suspicious URL (`d3f7a.info/6CVRmQm71`). Legitimate companies rarely send unsolicited links promising gifts via SMS, and the domain structure is not associated with a known, reputable brand. The combination of an unexpected reward and a suspicious link strongly suggests an attempt to trick the recipient into clicking and potentially providing personal information or downloading malware.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01898", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing.\n1. **Brand Impersonation:** It uses the name \"CITI Alerts,\" impersonating a legitimate financial institution.\n2. **Urgency/Threat:** It creates a sense of urgency and threat by claiming the debit card has been \"blocked due to unusual activity,\" prompting immediate action from the recipient.\n3. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL, `citi-actvity.org`, is highly suspicious. It is not the official CITI website and contains a common misspelling/variation (\"actvity\" instead of \"activity\"), a tactic used in phishing to mimic legitimate domains.\n4. **Request for Action/Verification:** It directs the user to click a link to \"Verify your details,\" which is a classic tactic to steal login credentials or personal information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01899", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message contains casual, conversational language (\"No I'm in the same boat,\" \"Still here at my moms,\" \"Check me out on yo\"). It does not exhibit any typical characteristics of smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The content appears to be a personal update between acquaintances.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01900", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing.\n1. **Unsolicited Offer/Bait:** The message offers a \"donation valued at $100\" to the recipient, which is a common tactic to entice clicks.\n2. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates \"Costco,\" a well-known retailer, to gain trust.\n3. **Call to Action with Suspicious Link:** It directs the user to click a link (`[link]`) to \"respond to this two question survey\" in exchange for compensation. This link is the primary vector for phishing.\n4. **Vague/Generic Language:** The phrasing (\"appreciated customer,\" \"for your efforts\") is generic and lacks specific context that a real company communication would usually have.\n\nThe combination of brand impersonation, an unsolicited financial incentive, and a request to click an external link makes this highly suspicious.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01901", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message contains several suspicious cues indicative of smishing. The format, starting with \"FRM:1B|UPS-ZOF05 SUBJ:\", resembles automated or system-generated messages, often used in phishing attempts. Most critically, it includes a URL: \"www.pyr-upsi.website//isr1i2pF19389#\". This URL is highly suspicious because it uses a non-standard domain structure (\"pyr-upsi.website\") and appears to be an attempt to impersonate a legitimate service like UPS (United Parcel Service), which is a common tactic in smishing. The message itself is cryptic and lacks any clear, legitimate context, instead presenting a string of codes and a suspicious link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01902", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing.\n1. **Brand Impersonation:** It uses the name \"Costco,\" a well-known retailer, to lend credibility.\n2. **Suspicious Link:** It contains a shortened, non-standard URL (`f2gpy.info/RzNKEws Zve`). Legitimate companies rarely use such generic, shortened links for prize notifications.\n3. **Unsolicited Prize/Urgency:** The message claims the recipient won a prize (\"Airpods draw\") based on a receipt, which is a common tactic in phishing to entice clicks.\n4. **Request for Action (Implied):** The link strongly implies the user needs to click it to claim the prize, which is the goal of smishing attacks.\n\nThe combination of brand spoofing, an unsolicited prize, and a suspicious link strongly indicates a phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01903", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message \"did u get that message\" is very brief and lacks any specific content, links, requests for personal information, or indicators of urgency or impersonation. It reads like a casual, informal follow-up question between two people who are already in communication. Therefore, it exhibits no suspicious cues associated with smishing.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01904", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The primary cues are the creation of a sense of **urgency** (\"Your Debit.Card have been locked!\"), which is a common tactic to make recipients act without thinking. Furthermore, it provides a specific, seemingly official-looking **account ID** and directs the user to call a specific, potentially spoofed, phone number. The tone is alarming and demands immediate action, which is characteristic of phishing attempts designed to steal credentials or personal information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01905", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is very brief and contains only a greeting (\"Hola\"), a username/identifier (\"Everly42\"), and a file attachment notification (\"PDF Document -74 KB\").\n\n**Evidence:**\nThe message lacks any explicit urgent language, direct requests for personal information or payment, or clear brand impersonation. However, the unsolicited nature of receiving a file attachment from an unknown sender (implied by the generic greeting and identifier) is a common tactic used in smishing or malware distribution. The attachment itself is the primary suspicious element. While it could be a legitimate file shared between known parties, in the context of unsolicited SMS, an unexpected file attachment is a significant red flag for phishing or malware delivery.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, the presence of an unsolicited, uncontextualized file attachment leans towards a suspicious/malicious intent rather than a benign, expected communication.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01906", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It creates a sense of urgency (\"Refund available until 11/12\") and requests an action (confirming a refund) via a suspicious, shortened, and non-standard URL (`mfrvvic.com/Byxyo8KX`). While it mentions a refund (a potentially legitimate event), the unsolicited nature, the specific financial amount, and the requirement to click an external, suspicious link to \"confirm\" the transaction are classic phishing tactics designed to steal credentials or personal information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
