{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01943", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message appears to be a casual, personal communication between two individuals. It uses informal language (\"I am in office:)\", \"whats the matter..\", \"msg me now\", \"i will call you at break:)\") and does not contain any elements typically associated with smishing, such as urgent demands, suspicious links, requests for personal information, or brand impersonation. The tone suggests an ordinary conversation between acquaintances or colleagues.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01944", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses a sense of urgency (\"Expires (11:59PM PST)\"), which is a common tactic in phishing to pressure the recipient into acting quickly without thinking. Furthermore, it contains a suspicious, non-standard URL (`gpmkg0.online/Ym5Yb tifldKh`), which is characteristic of malicious links designed to harvest credentials or install malware. The message also implies an account or service issue (\"Michael Pending Mobile Alert\"), suggesting brand impersonation or a fake service notification.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
