{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01946", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The language is highly suggestive and unsolicited (\"OMG you can add so much length and girth\"), which is unusual for legitimate communication. More critically, it contains a suspicious, shortened URL (`quotienteminent.com/UbMa99`) that is likely designed to trick the recipient into clicking and entering personal or financial information. The combination of suggestive, unsolicited content and a suspicious link strongly suggests a phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01947", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message contains no elements typically associated with smishing, such as urgent requests, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. Instead, it is a highly informal, humorous, and nonsensical message that appears to be a personal joke or a random, non-malicious communication.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01948", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It creates a sense of urgency (\"potential risk to your confidential data,\" \"Take action\") to prompt immediate action from the recipient. Furthermore, it directs the user to a suspicious, shortened URL (`wklnv.me/nK7NWdxQXN`), which is a common tactic used in phishing attacks to hide the true destination of the link. The message attempts to leverage fear regarding data security to trick the user into clicking.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01949", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits characteristics of a chain message or a social engineering tactic, often used in spam or low-level scams, but it does not contain the typical hallmarks of *smishing* (SMS phishing). Smishing usually involves impersonating a legitimate entity (like a bank or delivery service) to trick the recipient into clicking a malicious link or providing sensitive personal/financial information. This message is a playful, low-stakes \"game\" that asks for a simple, non-sensitive response (\"which colour suits me the best?\"). While it is unsolicited and potentially spammy, it lacks the malicious intent or the specific phishing vectors (urgent threats, fake login pages, requests for passwords/credit card numbers) that define smishing. Therefore, it is classified as ham (legitimate/non-malicious spam).\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01950", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It impersonates a legitimate entity (USPS) and creates a sense of urgency (\"Please update the address in time\"). Most critically, it directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`m.address.dfxwpnxh33ko.site`), which is a common tactic used in phishing attacks to steal personal information or credentials.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01951", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several characteristics of smishing. The primary suspicious cues are the unsolicited nature of the invitation, the promise of \"Daily profit sharing plan,\" which is a common lure in investment scams, and the inclusion of a direct link to join a group chat. While WhatsApp links themselves are not inherently malicious, when paired with promises of guaranteed profits, they strongly suggest a phishing or scam attempt designed to lure the recipient into a fraudulent scheme. There is no legitimate context provided for this invitation.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01952", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message appears to be a casual, informal conversation between two people. It uses slang and abbreviations (\"i'm,\" \"workin,\" \"oso,\" \"Thgt,\" \"ur,\" \"fren's\") and asks a simple, personal question about the recipient's work situation. There are no indicators of smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01953", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message contains several suspicious cues indicative of smishing. Specifically, it demands immediate action (\"within the next 30 mins\") and requires the recipient to enter a specific code into a messaging application (LINE). The presence of a seemingly random alphanumeric string (\"JFOQLtyexga\") suggests a tracking or verification mechanism often used in phishing attempts. The message is urgent and directs the user to an external action, which is a common tactic in smishing to bypass critical thinking.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01954", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham. The message is very informal, using abbreviations (\"ü\" for you, \"n\" for and, \"ar\" for are), and appears to be a casual arrangement or confirmation between people who know each other. There are no indicators of phishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01955", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The message uses an urgent tone (\"Quick review... is required\") and includes a seemingly official-looking identifier (\"ALERT-BANKINGID: it55XX2445 / NotificationID:9aAyyy0AaA\"). Crucially, it directs the recipient to a phone number (+18333320502) rather than a verified, official bank website, which is a common tactic in phishing attempts to trick users into providing information over the phone or via a malicious link (though a link isn't explicitly present, the call-to-action via a non-standard number is highly suspicious). The combination of banking context, urgency, and an external, unverified contact method strongly suggests an attempt at social engineering.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01956", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The primary suspicious cues are the unsolicited notification about a \"shipment issue\" (a common phishing lure) and the presence of a suspicious link. The URL, `http://uspsdeliveryrd.ga/`, is highly suspicious because it uses a non-official domain (`.ga`) and attempts to impersonate a legitimate service (USPS). Furthermore, it creates a sense of urgency by implying a problem with a recent shipment that needs immediate attention.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01957", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It impersonates a legitimate entity (USPS) to create a sense of urgency (\"Your package cannot be delivered\"). Crucially, it directs the recipient to a suspicious, shortened URL (`https://rb.gy/1esm1`) rather than the official USPS website, which is a common tactic in phishing attacks to capture personal information or install malware. The request to \"Click the link to inquire\" is a direct call to action designed to trick the user.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01958", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham. The content is a very casual, personal, and conversational message (\"Horrible bf... I now v hungry...\"). It contains no elements typically associated with smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01960", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The message claims a package has been sent, which is a common pretext for phishing. More critically, it includes a suspicious link (`http://tbmuy.ncmie.com`). The domain name appears random and does not belong to a recognizable, legitimate shipping or courier service, suggesting it is designed to trick the recipient into clicking and potentially entering personal or payment information. The tone is directive (\"Veuillez le vérifier et le recevoir\"), adding a slight element of urgency or required action.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01961", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Unsolicited Job Offer:** The message is an unsolicited advertisement for a job, which is a common tactic in phishing scams.\n2. **Vague/High Reward:** The promise of earning \"$5-$100 daily\" for minimal effort (\"Work 2-3 hours daily on mobile\") is an unrealistic lure often used in scams.\n3. **Urgency/Time Limit:** The mention of a deadline (\"Vacancy till 31st march, 2020\") creates a sense of urgency to prompt quick, unthinking action.\n4. **Suspicious Link:** The use of a shortened URL (`http://bit.ly/WHO-Jobs-Offer`) is highly suspicious. While the link attempts to impersonate a legitimate organization (WHO), the use of a generic shortener makes it impossible to verify the destination safely, and the context suggests it leads to a malicious site.\n5. **Impersonation:** It attempts to leverage the authority of a major health organization (WHO) to gain trust.\n\nThe combination of an unsolicited, high-reward job offer, urgency, and a suspicious link strongly points to a phishing attempt designed to steal personal information or credentials.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01962", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The content uses a celebrity name (\"Dr Oz\") to lend false credibility (brand/authority impersonation) and promotes a product (\"Secret CBD for Dick Gains\") in a sensational or unsolicited manner. Most critically, it contains a suspicious, shortened URL (`http://fireelse.community/nm6pkc`) which is designed to trick the recipient into clicking and potentially entering personal or financial information. The combination of unsolicited promotion, celebrity association, and a suspicious link strongly suggests a phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01964", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message \"Lmao but its so fun...\" contains no suspicious cues. It is a very casual, conversational message using common internet slang (\"Lmao\"). There is no urgency, no suspicious links, no brand impersonation, and no request for personal information or payment. Therefore, it is classified as legitimate communication.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01965", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses a sense of urgency (\"Ready to Ship Upon Confirmation\") to prompt immediate action. More critically, it contains a suspicious, non-standard URL (`http://107.175.219.12/ipad.php`). Legitimate companies typically use their official, recognizable domain names, not random IP addresses, for shipping confirmations. The message also implies a transaction or order confirmation (CID#86290), which is a common lure in phishing attempts.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01966", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several classic hallmarks of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Threat/Urgency:** The message uses high-pressure language (\"WARNING,\" \"urgent basis,\" \"lawsuit,\" \"arrest warrant,\" \"frozen\") to induce panic and prompt immediate action without verification.\n2. **Impersonation:** It impersonates a government agency (I.R.S. - Internal Revenue Service), a common tactic in phishing scams.\n3. **Threat of Severe Consequences:** It threatens severe repercussions (arrest, freezing assets) if the recipient does not comply immediately.\n4. **Call to Action via Phone Number:** It directs the recipient to call a specific, non-official phone number, which is a common method for scammers to capture personal information or demand payment.\n\nThere are no benign cues present; the entire message is designed to deceive and frighten the recipient.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01967", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It uses a lure (\"Earn up to $100 in Walmart Store Value\") to entice the recipient. Crucially, it directs the user to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`encumberrapidit.com/b4xUQc`) to claim the reward, which is a common tactic in phishing attacks. The combination of a financial incentive and an external, untrusted link is highly suspicious.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01969", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message contains no suspicious cues typically associated with smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. Instead, it is a sentimental, philosophical, or emotional message using informal language and abbreviations (\"Wat,\" \"dearer,\" \"dat,\" \"u,\" \"dem\"). This style is characteristic of personal communication or general social messaging, not a phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01971", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The sender uses a generic, official-sounding prefix (\"IRS_TAX_REFUND_ID_#\") which attempts to impersonate a legitimate government agency (the IRS). The message immediately presents a PDF document with a specific ID, creating a sense of official documentation and urgency related to a tax refund. While the message itself doesn't contain a direct link or explicit request for payment, the delivery of an unsolicited, official-looking document via SMS is a common tactic used in smishing campaigns to trick recipients into opening the file, which could contain malware or lead to a phishing site.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01972", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency/Problem Creation:** It creates a sense of urgency by claiming a package delivery failure due to incomplete address information.\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL, `https://usps.trckspost.com`, is not the official USPS domain (which would be usps.com). The use of a slightly altered or unrelated domain is a classic phishing tactic.\n3. **Request for Action/Information:** It directs the user to click a link to \"confirm your address,\" which is a common method for harvesting personal data.\n4. **Unusual Instructions:** The complex instructions (\"Pleae reply to 1, then exit theSMS, open the SMS activationlink again, or copy the link toSafari browser and open it\") are designed to confuse the user and ensure they interact with the malicious link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01973", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing.\n1. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates the Internal Revenue Service (IRS), a government entity often targeted by scammers.\n2. **Urgency/Incentive:** It uses the lure of a tax refund ($573.00) to prompt immediate action.\n3. **Request for Personal Information:** It explicitly asks the recipient to \"confirm your information,\" which is a common tactic to steal sensitive data.\n4. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL, `https://irs.gov.tax-helping.com`, is highly suspicious. While it starts with \"irs.gov,\" the actual domain is `tax-helping.com`, not the official IRS domain (`irs.gov`). This is a classic example of a deceptive URL designed to look legitimate.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01975", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham because it is a very casual, conversational message between what appears to be acquaintances or colleagues. It uses informal language (\"mag meeting,\" \"avo\") and makes a simple scheduling request. There are no indicators of smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for sensitive personal information or payment.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01976", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several characteristics that suggest it might be a form of unsolicited or potentially deceptive outreach, although it lacks the most overt red flags of classic smishing (like a suspicious link or immediate demand for payment/credentials).\n\n**Evidence Analysis:**\n1. **Authority/Impersonation:** The sender claims to be a \"Sergeant Vo, a career counselor for the military.\" This attempts to establish authority and trust.\n2. **Enticement/Lure:** The message offers highly attractive incentives: \"Army benefits and programs,\" \"money for education,\" and \"up to $50,000 in bonuses.\" This is a common tactic used in scams to lure recipients into engagement.\n3. **Unsolicited Contact:** The message is an unsolicited outreach (\"I'm reaching out to see if you would be interested...\").\n4. **Lack of Immediate Threat/Link:** Unlike typical smishing, there is no immediate sense of urgency or a malicious link provided.\n\n**Conclusion:**\nWhile the message is not a textbook phishing attempt (it doesn't demand a password or click a malicious link), the combination of unsolicited contact, the use of military authority, and the promise of large, easy money strongly suggests it is a \"pig butchering\" style scam or a recruitment scam designed to harvest personal information or lead to financial fraud. In the context of classifying SMS threats, this manipulative, high-reward, unsolicited outreach falls under the umbrella of malicious/phishing attempts, making it more suspicious than a legitimate, expected communication.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01977", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It impersonates a delivery service, creating a sense of urgency (\"to avoid delay in receiving the goods\"). Crucially, it includes a suspicious, shortened URL (`http://usedps.com/go/Tracking/Q11U3`) which is designed to trick the recipient into clicking and potentially entering personal information or payment details. The request to \"reconfirm your address\" via a link is a classic phishing tactic.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01978", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It impersonates a well-known brand, Amazon, which is a common tactic in phishing. It creates a sense of urgency (\"before 24 hours\") to pressure the recipient into acting quickly without thinking. Most critically, it directs the user to a suspicious, non-Amazon domain (`unbouncepages.com`) via a shortened or unusual link, which is designed to harvest credentials or personal information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01979", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several classic signs of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency/Threat:** The message creates a sense of urgency by stating the account \"has suspend\" and demanding action \"within 2 days before account will be suspended.\"\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL, `http://www.xprswebsite.com/free/userf111/redirf111/?=5826865`, is not an official Amazon domain (which would be amazon.com or similar). It uses a generic, suspicious-looking domain.\n3. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates Amazon, a well-known brand, to trick the recipient.\n4. **Request for Action/Verification:** It demands the user click a link to \"verify\" and \"unlock\" their account, which is a common tactic to steal credentials.\n\nThe combination of brand impersonation, a high-pressure deadline, and a non-official link strongly indicates a phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01981", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message is classified as ham because it is a casual, conversational greeting that appears to be initiating a personal connection. There are no suspicious cues such as urgency, requests for personal information, payment demands, or suspicious links. The content suggests a genuine attempt to reconnect with an acquaintance.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01982", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham. The message is a casual request for an email address, framed within a personal context (\"convey regards to achan, amma\") and signed by a name (\"Rakhesh.Qatar\"). There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for sensitive personal information or payment, which are hallmarks of smishing. The tone is informal and conversational.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01983", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several characteristics of a potential scam, although it is not overtly aggressive like typical smishing. The primary suspicious cue is the unsolicited nature of the contact regarding a property sale. A stranger \"finding\" your house and immediately reaching out to inquire about a sale is highly unusual and often used in real estate scams or phishing attempts to gather personal information under the guise of a legitimate inquiry. While there is no immediate link or demand for payment, the unsolicited nature and the vague, personal approach are red flags.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01984", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham because it contains no suspicious cues. It is a short, personal message expressing positive sentiment and affection (\"Everybody had fun this evening. Miss you.\"). There is no urgency, no suspicious links, no brand impersonation, and no request for personal information or payment. It reads like a casual, legitimate text between friends or acquaintances.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
