{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02003", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message contains highly emotional and complimentary language (\"thank you for this breather,\" \"wonderful and a blessing\"). It does not contain any typical indicators of smishing, such as urgent requests, suspicious links, requests for personal information, or brand impersonation. The tone suggests a personal, appreciative communication, making it likely to be legitimate correspondence between individuals.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02004", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several characteristics that lean towards being suspicious, although it lacks the typical hallmarks of a direct phishing attempt (like a suspicious link or urgent financial request). The language is informal and slightly vague (\"Pls speak with me,\" \"I wont ask anything other then you friendship\"). This type of vague, personal outreach, especially when unsolicited, is often a tactic used in social engineering or catfishing, which can precede or be part of a broader scam (like romance scams, which often use SMS). The lack of context makes it inherently suspicious.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02005", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The primary suspicious cues are the urgent nature of the delivery issue, the request to click an external link to resolve the problem, and the highly suspicious URL itself (`https://usp-sbuxt.top`). The domain name is not associated with a legitimate, well-known courier service, suggesting it is a phishing attempt designed to steal personal or delivery information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02006", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing.\n1. **Brand Impersonation:** It uses the name \"USPS,\" impersonating a legitimate postal service.\n2. **Suspicious Link:** It contains a shortened URL (`https://bit.ly/387Mzes`). Shortened links are frequently used in phishing attempts to obscure the true destination of the link.\n3. **Implied Urgency/Action Required:** The message implies an action is needed (\"Re-schedule\") regarding a parcel, which is a common tactic to prompt immediate clicks.\n\nWhile the message claims to be from USPS, the combination of the unsolicited nature, the generic notification, and the use of a suspicious shortened link strongly suggests it is an attempt to trick the recipient into clicking a malicious link, making it smishing.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02007", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The sender is impersonating USPS, a legitimate brand, which is a common tactic in phishing. The message creates a sense of urgency (\"Your delivery has not completed\") to prompt immediate action. Most critically, it directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-official URL (`uspstrack-ot.info`) rather than the official USPS website. This combination of brand impersonation, urgency, and a suspicious link strongly suggests a phishing attempt designed to steal personal information or payment details.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02008", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message contains informal, colloquial language (\"Aiyo a bit pai seh ü noe...\", \"leh\") and appears to be a personal, conversational update about someone's health or appearance. There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. Therefore, it is classified as legitimate communication.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02009", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing or a similar form of social engineering. The sender initiates contact unsolicited (\"Saw your number on your profile\"), which is a common tactic in phishing attempts. The inclusion of a shortened, suspicious-looking URL (`slp2.xyz/sPldg93jsOW7`) is a major red flag, as these links are often used to direct victims to malicious websites designed to steal credentials or install malware. The casual, slightly vague nature of the message (\"Lets chat\") combined with the immediate push to an external, unknown link suggests an attempt to lure the recipient into a risky interaction.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02010", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It impersonates a well-known brand (Netflix) and creates a sense of urgency (\"Your payment cannot be completed. To keep access...\"). Crucially, it directs the user to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`http://netflix-membership.com`) rather than the official Netflix domain, which is a classic phishing tactic designed to steal login or payment information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02011", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency/Threat:** The message states, \"Your Membership is on hold,\" creating a sense of immediate need to act to prevent service interruption.\n2. **Request for Action/Information:** It demands the user \"update some trouble with your current billing information,\" which is a common tactic to steal financial details.\n3. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL (`http://membership-normally.com:911@s956520694.onlinehome.us/atcc`) is highly suspicious. It uses a mix of seemingly legitimate-sounding text (`membership-normally.com`) combined with a complex, non-standard domain structure (`onlinehome.us`) and includes an unusual port number (`:911@`). This structure is characteristic of phishing attempts designed to look legitimate while routing to a malicious server.\n4. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates \"Netflix Pte. Ltd.,\" a well-known brand, to gain the user's trust.\n\nThe combination of urgency, a request for sensitive information (billing details), and a highly suspicious link strongly classifies this as smishing.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02015", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message contains two separate verification codes, which is unusual but not inherently suspicious. The language is formal (\"This is an automated message,\" \"We will not contact you directly to request any personal information\"), and it provides instructions regarding the code's expiration. There are no suspicious links, urgent threats, or direct requests for sensitive personal information or payment. This structure is typical of legitimate two-factor authentication (2FA) or account verification messages sent by services.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02016", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several benign cues. It is an informal, conversational text message between what appears to be friends or acquaintances. It discusses a social event (\"wildlife talk\") and uses casual language and abbreviations (\"u want2come,\" \"that2worzels\"). There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment, which are hallmarks of smishing.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
