{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02050", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses vague, enticing language (\"additional benefits here. dont worry over bills we are here to help Michael....\") which is a common tactic to lure recipients. Crucially, it contains a suspicious, non-standard URL (`v41qle.jz4721.net`) that is designed to look like a legitimate link but is likely malicious. The inclusion of an opt-out instruction is a common feature in unsolicited marketing or phishing attempts.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02051", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The sender is impersonating a legitimate entity (\"BANKOFAMERICA\"), which is a common tactic in phishing. The message creates a sense of urgency (\"immediately\") to pressure the recipient into acting without thinking. Most critically, it directs the user to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (\"boasmsportal.online\") rather than the bank's known, official website. Furthermore, it requests the user to \"Update your account information,\" which is a direct request for sensitive personal data.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02052", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message contains a suspicious link (`Pbpls.COM/E5JyPE6w5t`) presented as a \"surprise gift.\" While the initial part (\"Your payment for September is paid. Thanks.\") sounds like a confirmation, the unsolicited link immediately following this confirmation is a common tactic in smishing to trick users into clicking on a malicious site, often disguised as a reward or further information. The domain name itself (`Pbpls.COM`) is generic and not associated with a known, legitimate service, which is a strong indicator of phishing.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02053", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is a motivational or philosophical quote. It contains no elements typically associated with smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The tone is benign and conversational.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02055", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It uses brand impersonation by falsely claiming to be from \"The US Treasury Department.\" It creates a sense of urgency and opportunity by offering a large sum of money (\"up to $2,200 every week\"). Most critically, it directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`https://americfamilyassist.com`) to \"Apply,\" which is a classic tactic used in phishing scams to steal personal information or deploy malware.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02057", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message mimics a notification from a known financial application (Cash App), which is a form of brand impersonation. It creates a sense of urgency by alerting the recipient to an unauthorized transaction. Crucially, it provides a phone number for the recipient to contact if the transaction is not authorized, which is a common tactic used by scammers to direct victims to a fraudulent support line. While it doesn't contain a direct link, the structure and context are designed to panic the user into taking immediate action via the provided contact information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02058", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham because it contains a casual, personal apology and rescheduling of plans. There are no suspicious cues such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The language is informal and conversational, consistent with a personal text exchange.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02059", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham. The content is a casual, conversational message between individuals (\"Ü go home liao? Ask dad to pick me up at 6...\"). It contains no suspicious cues such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. It appears to be a routine communication between family members or friends.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02060", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency:** The message imposes a strict 12-hour deadline (\"within 12 hours\").\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The URL provided, `https://cargowatch-address.com`, is not the official USPS domain and appears designed to mimic a legitimate tracking or delivery service.\n3. **Request for Action/Information:** It prompts the recipient to click a link to \"confirm your address,\" which is a common tactic to harvest personal information.\n4. **Unusual Instructions:** The complex instructions (\"Please reply to Y, then exit the SMS, open the SMS activation link again, or copy the link to Safari browser and open it\") are designed to confuse the user and force them into a specific, potentially malicious, sequence of actions.\n\nThe combination of urgency, a non-official link, and a request to verify personal details strongly suggests a phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02061", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. It uses a vague, unsolicited, and somewhat aggressive tone (\"summertime is done and ur still looking big in the mirror\") to create a sense of urgency or insecurity, which is a common tactic in phishing. Furthermore, it includes a suspicious, non-standard URL (`http://voicetabular.boutique/3djn3c6x`) that is designed to look like a commercial site but is likely a malicious link intended to harvest personal information or install malware. The message is a direct solicitation for a product/service without any prior context or legitimate branding.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02063", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message \"Ü come lt 25 n pass to me lar\" is highly informal, contains numerous typos and abbreviations (\"Ü,\" \"lt,\" \"n,\" \"lar\"), and lacks any clear context, sender identification, or request for information or action. It reads like a very casual, possibly incomplete, personal text message between acquaintances. There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal data/payment that would suggest phishing.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02064", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham. The content is a casual, conversational message regarding a personal matter (\"Did u fix the teeth?if not do it asap.ok take care.\"). It lacks any of the typical indicators of smishing, such as urgent demands for personal information, suspicious links, financial requests, or impersonation of a known entity.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02065", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The primary suspicious cues are the promise of unusually high, guaranteed earnings (\"earn 5K-50K every day\") with minimal effort, which is a classic lure in financial scams. Furthermore, it directs the recipient to join a group via a link, which is a common tactic to funnel users into a malicious or deceptive environment. The vague nature of the \"BTC discussion group\" combined with the high reward promise suggests a pyramid scheme or investment scam, which falls under the umbrella of phishing/smishing.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02066", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The primary suspicious cue is the link provided: `https://usps.comhelphn.com`. While it attempts to mimic the USPS domain, the addition of \"helphn.com\" makes it a clear spoofed or malicious domain, not the official USPS website. Furthermore, the message creates a sense of urgency (\"could not be delivered\") and requests the user to click a link to \"confirm your address,\" which is a common tactic used in phishing to harvest personal information. The instruction to \"reply Y, activate the or copy the link link, and open it in your browser\" is also slightly convoluted and designed to prompt immediate action.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02067", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham because it appears to be a casual, context-specific message, likely related to a game or activity involving someone named \"Kallis.\" There are no indicators of phishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, requests for personal information, or brand impersonation.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02068", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It uses a promotional hook (\"CVS Appreciation Week,\" \"Earn up to $100 in CVS Credit\") to entice the recipient. Crucially, it directs the user to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`swellingcipher.com/wnX1r9`) rather than the official CVS website. This combination of a lure and a suspicious link is a classic tactic used in phishing attacks to steal credentials or personal information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02069", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses a high-pressure, enticing offer (\"fantastic ACA rates, some as low as 0/month\") to lure the recipient. Crucially, it contains a suspicious, non-standard URL (`storage.googleapis.com/getaca/getaca.html?fbluxyR7Z4`). Legitimate healthcare or government programs typically direct users to official, well-known domains (like healthcare.gov), not generic cloud storage links with obfuscated parameters. The combination of a financial incentive and a suspicious link is a classic smishing tactic designed to trick users into clicking and potentially entering personal or financial information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02070", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The use of \"URGENT\" creates a sense of immediate pressure, a common tactic in phishing. It impersonates a financial institution (\"USB,\" likely a misspelling or abbreviation for a bank) and claims \"Suspicious behavior has been observed,\" which is designed to alarm the recipient. Crucially, it directs the user to call a specific, non-standard phone number to \"authenticate,\" which is a classic social engineering technique to steal credentials or personal information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02072", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message is a very casual, conversational text message. It contains no urgent language, no suspicious links, no brand impersonation, and no requests for personal information or payment. It appears to be a normal, benign exchange between two people.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02073", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several classic signs of smishing. It impersonates a well-known service (Netflix), creates a sense of urgency (\"placed on hold,\" \"as soon as possible to continue using your account without any interruption\"), and prompts the recipient to take an immediate action, which is likely clicking a suspicious link (implied by the truncated message, but the context strongly suggests a link to update billing information). This is a common tactic used in phishing attacks to steal login credentials or financial details.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02074", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The primary suspicious cues are:\n1. **Urgency/Problem Creation:** It creates a sense of urgency by claiming the package has the \"wrong address\" and is \"temporarily suspended,\" pressuring the recipient to act immediately.\n2. **Suspicious Link:** It contains a shortened, non-standard URL (`https://wsssend.com/revalidate/k8h5`) that is likely designed to harvest credentials or personal information.\n3. **Unsolicited Contact:** The sender is not in the recipient's contact list, which is typical for phishing attempts.\n4. **Action Required:** It demands a specific action (replying to `[ok]`) while simultaneously directing the user to a suspicious link, a common tactic to trick users into clicking or providing information.\n\nThe combination of an urgent delivery issue, an unknown sender, and a suspicious link strongly points to a phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02076", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message contains highly suggestive and unsolicited sexual content, which is a strong indicator of spam or malicious intent. More critically, it includes a suspicious, shortened URL (`slanderchapter.com/LC6JHd`). This combination of unsolicited, inappropriate content and a suspicious link strongly suggests a phishing or malicious attempt, even if the primary goal is not traditional credential theft but rather malware delivery or scamming. The nature of the message itself is not a legitimate communication from a known service.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02077", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message exhibits several suspicious cues indicative of smishing. The message uses brand impersonation by referencing \"Home Depot,\" which is a common tactic in phishing. Furthermore, it includes a suspicious link (`http://marginaljo.shopping/v3E6qB`). The domain name is not the official Home Depot domain, and the structure of the link is generic and unusual, suggesting it leads to a malicious or deceptive landing page designed to trick the recipient into clicking. The lure of a \"Monthly GiveAway\" is a common social engineering tactic used to create a sense of opportunity and prompt immediate action.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02078", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It impersonates a legitimate delivery service (FedEx) to create a sense of urgency and prompt action. The inclusion of a tracking code adds a layer of perceived legitimacy. Most critically, it directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`c7dvr.info/FGdGtk12vilM`) rather than the official FedEx website, which is a classic tactic used in phishing attacks to steal personal or payment information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
