{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02094", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several classic signs of smishing. The primary suspicious cues are:\n1. **Urgency/Threat:** It creates a sense of urgency by stating the shipment \"could not be delivered\" due to an \"unpaid duty fee,\" pressuring the recipient to act quickly.\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The URL (`https://manage-acrwg.drr.ac/?confirm`) is highly suspicious. It does not appear to be the official, recognizable domain for UPS (which would typically be ups.com or a related official subdomain). The domain structure is convoluted and likely designed to mimic a legitimate service.\n3. **Request for Action/Payment:** It directs the user to click a link to resolve a financial issue (unpaid duty fee), which is a common tactic in phishing scams.\n4. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates a known brand, UPS, to gain trust.\n\nThe combination of urgency, a suspicious link, and a request related to payment/delivery makes this highly indicative of a phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02095", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The primary suspicious cues are the unsolicited notification of a large, unexpected deposit (\"Venmo $100 was Deposited\") which is a common lure in phishing attempts. Furthermore, the message includes a suspicious, non-official link (`http://lossfighting.com/5D2wSz`) that directs the user to an external, unrelated domain, likely to harvest credentials or personal information under the guise of \"claiming\" the money. The inclusion of a strict time limit (\"Within 24 Hours\") creates a sense of urgency, a classic social engineering tactic used in phishing.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02096", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency and Threat:** The message creates a sense of immediate crisis (\"Your Amazon Account has been locked,\" \"Please take action on your account within 48 hours to avoid permanent suspension\").\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL (`http://s953131863.onlinehome.us/authcust`) is highly suspicious. It does not use the official Amazon domain and appears to be a generic, potentially malicious domain designed to mimic a legitimate login page.\n3. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates a well-known brand (Amazon) to trick the recipient into clicking the link.\n4. **Request for Action/Credentials:** The message pressures the user to click a link to \"recover\" their account, which is the typical goal of phishing attacks to steal login credentials.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02097", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is very brief and lacks any typical indicators of smishing, such as urgent demands, suspicious links, requests for personal information, or brand impersonation. It appears to be a simple, informal statement or reminder about an exam date. The context is too minimal to definitively label it, but based on the content alone, it does not exhibit the characteristics of a phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02099", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message appears to be a notification from Snapchat regarding an invitation. The presence of a direct link to Snapchat (`https://t.snapchat.com/ruoK9x3T`) and an unsubscribe link (`w.snapchat.com/aiOioHdr`) suggests it is an automated notification from the platform itself, which is a common feature of legitimate app notifications. There are no immediate signs of high-pressure urgency, requests for sensitive personal information (like passwords or credit card numbers), or obvious brand impersonation outside of the expected Snapchat context. While unsolicited messages can sometimes be malicious, this specific format strongly resembles standard social media invitation notifications.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02100", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message is classified as ham because it appears to be a casual, conversational query regarding a contract date. There are no indicators of smishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. It reads like a normal follow-up or clarification between parties who likely have a pre-existing relationship or context regarding a contract.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
