{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02102", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The primary suspicious cues are the unexpected notification of a financial deduction, which creates a sense of urgency and potential alarm. Furthermore, the message directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`cesbc.us/`) to resolve the issue, which is a classic tactic used in phishing attacks to steal credentials or personal information. Legitimate banks typically use official app notifications or direct users to their verified website, not unsolicited links in SMS messages for urgent financial matters.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02103", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is classified as ham. The message is a casual, conversational exchange between two people (\"Thanks love. But am i doing torch or bold.\"). It contains no elements of phishing, such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02104", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message appears to be a casual, personal update between two individuals. It uses informal language (\"Dear,\" \"shall mail tonite,\" \"varunnathu edukkukayee raksha ollu\") and discusses personal plans and well-being (\"things are looking ok\"). There are no indicators of smishing, such as urgent demands, suspicious links, requests for financial information, or impersonation of a known entity.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02105", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several characteristics of smishing. The primary suspicious cues are the unsolicited nature of the request (\"Hey Niaz, help us answer a fast survey\"), the promise of an incentive (\"free watch in return\"), and the presence of a shortened, suspicious link (`lksr.link/9pvvbF`). Shortened links are often used in phishing attempts to obscure the true destination URL. The inclusion of \"Txt Stop to end\" is a common tactic used in unsolicited marketing or scam messages to provide a false sense of control or compliance.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02106", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several suspicious cues indicative of smishing. The message uses deliberate misspellings and unusual punctuation (e.g., \"paymen.t,\" \"la.st,\" \"pa.id,\" \"sur.prise,\" \"W3NGZ.COM\") which is a common tactic to bypass spam filters or appear less formal/official. The message claims a payment was made, which is a common lure, but immediately pivots to an unsolicited \"surprise present\" linked to a suspicious, non-standard domain (W3NGZ.COM). This combination of vague confirmation followed by an unsolicited link demanding interaction is highly characteristic of phishing attempts designed to trick the recipient into clicking a malicious link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02107", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The use of a generic, alarming subject line (\"Customer Alert-89e1-Contact our 24/7 Security-Department\") combined with an urgent notification (\"EBT-Card Temporarily Frozen!\") is a classic social engineering tactic designed to provoke immediate action. Furthermore, while it provides a phone number, the context of an unexpected security alert regarding a government benefit card (EBT) is a common vector for phishing attempts, often leading to requests for verification or login details if the user calls the provided number or clicks a hidden link (though no link is present here, the threat itself is the lure). The combination of urgency and a critical service notification makes it highly suspicious.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02108", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is a casual, conversational greeting that shares personal interests (football, basketball, outdoors). It does not contain any suspicious elements such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. This aligns with the characteristics of a legitimate, non-malicious message.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-02109", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is a simple, friendly birthday greeting. It contains no urgent requests, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The tone is entirely benign and conversational.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00014", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The sender, \"Hillary,\" is initiating an unsolicited social invitation, which is unusual for a legitimate contact unless there is prior context (which is absent). More critically, the message includes a suspicious, shortened, and non-standard URL (`http://hnkmrb.com/WIXTZAO`). Legitimate social invitations rarely require clicking an unknown, cryptic link to arrange a meeting. The combination of an unsolicited approach and a suspicious link strongly suggests an attempt to phish for information or lead the recipient to a malicious site.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00020", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several characteristics that suggest it is not a typical smishing attempt, but rather a personal request for financial assistance. There are no suspicious links, urgent demands for immediate action related to a service or account, or impersonation of a known brand. The tone is personal and conversational, discussing a family financial situation. While the request for help is a form of solicitation, it lacks the hallmarks of a malicious phishing attempt designed to steal credentials or money through a deceptive technical pretext.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00025", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message \"Ryder unsold.now gibbs.\" is extremely brief and lacks any clear context, recognizable branding, or typical phishing indicators like urgent demands, suspicious links, or requests for personal information. It appears to be a very truncated or garbled message, possibly a personal note, a very informal notification, or a typo. Without further context, it does not exhibit the hallmarks of a smishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00054", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several classic indicators of smishing. It impersonates a well-known service (Netflix) and creates a sense of urgency (\"placed on hold,\" \"as soon as possible\") to prompt immediate action. The core request is to update billing information, which is a common tactic used by phishers to steal sensitive financial data. While the message doesn't contain a visible link in the provided text, the context strongly suggests that a link would follow or be implied, leading the user to a fraudulent site.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00080", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The message uses a common delivery notification pretext, which is a frequent tactic in phishing. The request to \"update\" information is vague and immediately followed by a shortened, suspicious URL (`qrco.de/bdn5yo`). Shortened, non-official domain links are a major red flag, as they obscure the true destination of the link, likely leading to a credential harvesting or malware site. The generic salutation (\"Dear Sir\") is also impersonal, which is common in mass phishing attempts.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
