{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00117", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The message creates a sense of urgency (\"will return to sender if you fail to collect it\"), which is a common tactic to prompt immediate, unthinking action. Furthermore, it contains a shortened, suspicious link (`hqwr.link/77Taj8F`) that is not associated with a known, legitimate service, suggesting it is designed to lead to a phishing site. The vague nature of the \"Note Reward\" also lacks context, making it highly suspicious.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00122", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The opening is casual, but the core of the message directs the recipient to an external, suspicious link (\"ju11n.xyz/hXwQ9T2DpP\") under the guise of a \"controversial video.\" This tactic is often used to trick users into clicking malicious links, which can lead to malware installation or credential theft. The link itself uses a non-standard, potentially deceptive domain name.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00124", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses brand impersonation by referencing \"Home Depot,\" which is a common tactic in phishing to gain trust. Furthermore, it presents an unsolicited \"Gift Card Offer,\" which creates a lure or incentive. Most critically, it contains a suspicious link (`http://leaseill.associates/b42TrX`). The domain name is not the official Home Depot domain and appears randomly generated, suggesting it is designed to harvest credentials or personal information. The combination of brand impersonation, an enticing offer, and a suspicious, non-official link strongly points to a phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
