{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00762", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message contains several suspicious cues. It starts with a seemingly positive notification (\"Your bill is paid for March\"), which is a common tactic to build trust before introducing a suspicious element. The inclusion of a link to an unfamiliar, non-corporate domain (`unselfishdumplings.com`) that uses a short, randomized URL structure is highly characteristic of phishing attempts. The \"gift\" is a lure designed to entice the recipient to click the link, which likely leads to a malicious site designed to steal information or install malware.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00764", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The use of \"urgent notice\" creates a sense of pressure, a common tactic in phishing. More critically, the link provided, `a4nrv.info/YCdfXPIKIp`, is a suspicious, non-official domain that does not belong to USPS, suggesting an attempt to trick the recipient into clicking a malicious link. The message impersonates a legitimate service (USPS) to solicit action.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00778", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several suspicious cues indicative of smishing. The message uses a vague, attention-grabbing subject (\"Home Depot Study, Get Paid Today\") which is a common tactic in phishing to lure recipients. More critically, it contains a suspicious, shortened URL (`http://hireprevio.shopping/ybFO3G`). This URL does not appear to be an official Home Depot domain, suggesting it is a malicious link designed to harvest personal information or install malware. The promise of getting paid quickly is a classic lure used in scams.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00786", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message contains vague, moralizing language (\"honesty road,\" \"tough decisions\") and directs the recipient to take a specific action (\"Call the bank tomorrow\"). However, it lacks the typical hallmarks of smishing, such as urgent threats, suspicious links, or direct requests for sensitive personal information or payment. The tone is more suggestive or philosophical than overtly malicious or transactional, making it lean towards being non-malicious or simply odd/unrelated spam rather than targeted phishing.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00796", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. It impersonates a legitimate entity (United States Postal Service - USPS) to create a sense of urgency regarding a package delivery issue. The request is to interact with a suspicious link (`https://wsends.com/go/Tracking/R2H9`), which is not the official USPS domain, and to take action to \"reactivate\" the package, which is a common tactic to trick users into clicking malicious links or providing personal information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00802", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The message claims there is a \"new entry\" in the recipient's \"Car History,\" which is a common pretext used in phishing attempts to entice clicks. Crucially, it contains a shortened, suspicious link (`https://t.co/o5v07fefr1?fmuxtuprck yocmlxf`). Shortened links are frequently used by malicious actors to obscure the true destination of the URL. Furthermore, the message lacks any identifying information about who is sending it (e.g., a specific dealership or service name), making it generic and untrustworthy.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
