{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01210", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The primary suspicious cues are the unsolicited nature of the message, the vague claim of a \"credit score update\" (a common lure), and the presence of a suspicious, shortened, and non-official-looking URL (`5wac5ltscn.co/oaktfqv`). Legitimate financial institutions typically do not send unsolicited links via SMS to reveal sensitive personal information like a credit score; they usually direct users to log into their official, known website or app. The inclusion of an unsubscribe option is a common tactic used in phishing attempts to appear legitimate.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01211", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing or a similar type of scam. The primary red flags are:\n1. **Unsolicited Offer/Vagueness:** The message offers a \"job\" at a major company (Wipro) without any context, application process, or official communication channel.\n2. **Overly Promising Language:** The phrase \"you will get every thing in life in 2 or 3 years\" is an extreme, unrealistic promise often used in recruitment scams to lure victims.\n3. **Informal/Poor Grammar:** The use of \":)\" and the slightly awkward phrasing suggests a non-professional, potentially automated, or deceptive sender.\n\nWhile there is no explicit link or request for immediate payment in this specific snippet, the combination of an unsolicited, overly enthusiastic job offer from a large corporation via SMS is a classic tactic used in recruitment scams, which fall under the umbrella of phishing/smishing attempts designed to lead to further compromise.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01224", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses an urgent and enticing call to action (\"Get Paid! Only takes 2 minutes\"), which is a common tactic in phishing to prompt immediate action without critical thought. Furthermore, the link provided, `http://boardingma.shopping/fEXyQZ`, is highly suspicious. It uses a non-standard, generic domain (`.shopping`) and contains random characters, which is characteristic of malicious shorteners or phishing sites designed to mimic legitimate services. There is no context provided to suggest this is from a known, trusted entity.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
