{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01335", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. Specifically, it uses a generic notification format (\"You received a friend invite\") which is often used in phishing attempts. More critically, it directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-standard URL (`http://ryjlvn.com/P9nu967`) rather than a known, legitimate platform's domain. The request to \"Answer back here\" via a link is a common tactic to trick users into clicking and potentially entering credentials or personal information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01354", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several suspicious cues indicative of smishing. The message uses unusual formatting (e.g., \"Y.our,\" \"l.ast,\" \"r.eceived,\" \"l.oyalty re.ward fo.r\") which is often used to bypass spam filters or appear less formal/official. More critically, it contains a shortened, suspicious URL (\"61uxsyo.qmrWM.Com/zyd8\") that is likely designed to trick the recipient into clicking and entering personal or financial information under the guise of a \"loyalty reward.\" The combination of a billing notification followed immediately by an unsolicited link is a common tactic in phishing attacks.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
