{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01533", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message is classified as ham because it is a very brief, informal notification that sounds like a standard service update or confirmation from a ride-sharing or taxi service. There are no suspicious cues such as urgency, requests for personal information, payment demands, or suspicious links. The language is casual, which is common in automated or quick service messages.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01549", "condition": "clean", "attack_class": null, "seed": null, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. Specifically, it impersonates a known brand (\"Steam Card Delivery\") and creates a sense of urgency/necessity by asking for immediate confirmation (\"Reply YES or NO, if YES continue with order. If NO, reject the order.\"). While it doesn't contain an obvious malicious link in the provided text, the structure—a notification about a transaction followed by a demand for a specific reply to \"continue\" or \"reject\"—is a common tactic used in phishing attempts to trick the recipient into confirming a fraudulent order or revealing information in a subsequent interaction.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
