{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00010", "condition": "perturbed", "attack_class": "character", "seed": 0, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses brand impersonation by referencing \"WELLS FARGO.\" It creates a sense of urgency and alarm by stating the \"Account temporarily locked.\" Most critically, it directs the recipient to a suspicious, shortened URL (`use.app.link/wf`) rather than the official Wells Fargo website, which is a classic tactic used in phishing attacks to steal credentials or personal information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00011", "condition": "perturbed", "attack_class": "character", "seed": 0, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing. The message uses informal language and abbreviations (\"Jluy,\" \"P@ss,\" \"a7\"), which is common in phishing attempts to appear casual or urgent. It promotes a tournament with a significant prize pool ($500) for popular games (Fortnite, LoL, SSBU), creating a sense of excitement and urgency to click. Most critically, it directs the recipient to a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`ggsimplicity.com/tournaments`) to sign up, which is a classic tactic for credential harvesting or malware distribution. The inclusion of \"Reply STOP\" is a standard feature in marketing/spam messages.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00013", "condition": "perturbed", "attack_class": "character", "seed": 0, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing.\n1. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates the Internal Revenue Service (IRS), a common tactic in phishing attacks.\n2. **Urgency/Incentive:** It uses the lure of a tax refund ($573.00) to prompt immediate action.\n3. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL, `https://irs.gov.direct-capitals.com`, is highly suspicious. While it contains \"irs.gov,\" the domain structure is clearly not the official IRS website and is designed to look legitimate (a technique called typosquatting or domain spoofing).\n4. **Request for Personal Information:** It explicitly asks the recipient to \"confirm your information\" via the link, which is a request for sensitive personal data.\n5. **Poor Grammar/Spelling:** The message contains numerous spelling and grammatical errors (\"rceive,\" \"cnanot,\" \"p rocess,\" \"inf ormation,\" \"l1nk,\" \"tapayers,\" \"highe st,\" \"ser vice\"), which is uncharacteristic of official government communications.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00015", "condition": "perturbed", "attack_class": "character", "seed": 0, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency:** The message demands action within \"12 hours.\"\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The URL provided, `https://usqmvj.top`, is not an official USPS domain and appears randomly generated, which is a common tactic in phishing.\n3. **Typos and Obfuscation:** The message contains numerous intentional misspellings and character substitutions (e.g., \"p@ckage,\" \"b3,\" \"incomplee,\" \"wit hin,\" \"s,\" \"Pleaes,\" \"activa7ion,\" \"ocpy,\" \"b rowser,\" \"hTe,\" \"w0nderful\"). This is often done to bypass automated spam filters.\n4. **Request for Action/Information:** It pressures the recipient to click a link to \"confirm your address,\" which is a request for personal information.\n5. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates the USPS.\n\nThese elements collectively point to a malicious attempt to trick the user into clicking a phishing link.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00016", "condition": "perturbed", "attack_class": "character", "seed": 0, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues indicative of smishing.\n1. **Unsolicited Request/Vague Sender:** The sender identifies as \"CJ with POR,\" which is vague and lacks official context, making it difficult to verify legitimacy.\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The message contains a shortened or non-standard link (`https://www.surveymonkey.com/r/F8VLMMV?UID=BM6SZE`). While SurveyMonkey is a legitimate platform, unsolicited links in SMS, especially when tied to a vague survey, are a common phishing tactic.\n3. **Typographical Errors/Obfuscation:** The use of symbols instead of letters (\"t@ke,\" \"7o,\" \"comple te,\" \"surv ey\") is a common tactic used by spammers to bypass basic SMS spam filters.\n4. **Request for Engagement:** The message prompts the recipient to click a link to complete a survey, which is often used to harvest data or install malware.\n\nThe combination of obfuscation, a vague sender, and an unsolicited link strongly suggests a phishing attempt rather than a legitimate communication.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
