{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00079", "condition": "perturbed", "attack_class": "character", "seed": 0, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Misspellings and Typos:** The message is riddled with intentional or unintentional misspellings (\"b3 deliver3d,\" \"addre5s,\" \"Plewase,\" \"te,\" \"th3,\" \"brow5er,\" \"git\"), a common tactic used by phishers to bypass basic spam filters and appear less professional.\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The URL provided, `https://usps.tirckposts.com`, is not the official USPS domain (`usps.com`). The misspelling (\"tirckposts\" instead of \"truckposts\" or similar legitimate variations) is a classic example of domain spoofing or typosquatting.\n3. **Urgency/Action Required:** The message creates a sense of urgency by stating the package \"cannot be delivered\" due to incomplete information, pressuring the recipient to click the link immediately.\n4. **Request for Information/Action:** It explicitly asks the user to \"confirm your address in the link,\" which is a request for personal information.\n\nThe combination of poor grammar/spelling, a non-official domain, and a demand for immediate action strongly classifies this as a phishing attempt.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
