{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00150", "condition": "perturbed", "attack_class": "character", "seed": 0, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing.\n1. **Suspicious Content/Urgency:** It creates a sense of urgency by claiming an unauthorized transaction (\"Apple Macbook Pro on your Amazon account\") and demanding immediate action (\"If you do not authorize this paym3nt, please press 0ne 7o speak...\").\n2. **Obfuscation/Typos:** The message uses leetspeak and intentional misspellings (\"n3w,\" \"paym3nt,\" \"0ne 7o,\" \"aour,\" \"messa9e\") to evade automated spam filters, which is a common tactic in phishing.\n3. **Suspicious Links/Contact:** It directs the recipient to click a specific number/link (\"14699825000\") under the guise of listening to a \"full voice message,\" which is designed to trick the user into engaging with a malicious endpoint.\n4. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates a legitimate entity (Amazon) to gain trust.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00151", "condition": "perturbed", "attack_class": "character", "seed": 0, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message is classified as ham because it is a very informal, conversational text message between what appears to be acquaintances or friends. It contains no suspicious elements such as urgency, links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The language is casual and relates to a personal health or dietary note.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00152", "condition": "perturbed", "attack_class": "character", "seed": 0, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message exhibits characteristics of informal, casual conversation rather than a malicious attempt to trick the recipient. The language is highly colloquial, uses abbreviations (\"Hm m...\", \"Dunno lleh\", \"m@yb\", \"4\", \"goigng\", \"5mall\", \"ju5\", \"smth\", \"dta\"), and discusses a vague topic about a bag or something else to keep. There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment, which are hallmarks of smishing.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
