{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00171", "condition": "perturbed", "attack_class": "character", "seed": 0, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses urgent language (\"Alert,\" \"today\") and promises a large financial reward (\"up to 1.4 millions\"). It employs character substitution (e.g., \"@\" for \"a,\" \"c@n\" for \"can\") which is a common tactic to bypass spam filters. Most critically, it contains a suspicious, non-official-looking URL (`http://checkthis1.info/g5L6DJmM2F`) that directs the recipient to an external site, likely to harvest credentials or personal information. The mention of a \"Huge Comp@ny\" is vague and likely an attempt at brand impersonation without specifying a legitimate entity.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00172", "condition": "perturbed", "attack_class": "character", "seed": 0, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several characteristics of smishing. It uses brand impersonation (VONS), creates a sense of urgency/incentive (\"Finish signing up to receive 5 Rewards (worth $7)\"), and contains a shortened, suspicious link (`https://vons.onelink.me/MuHG/s37kcrr2`) that directs the user to complete an action, likely to harvest information or install malware. While it appears to be a marketing/sign-up message, the unsolicited nature combined with the suspicious link makes it highly indicative of a phishing attempt designed to trick the user into clicking.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
