{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00275", "condition": "perturbed", "attack_class": "character", "seed": 0, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several suspicious cues characteristic of smishing or social engineering, although it is framed as a personal emergency. The use of non-standard spelling and characters (\"H3y,\" \"ju5t,\" \"friend5,\" \"pl ease,\" \"0n,\" \"1ts,\" \"0485\") is a common tactic used in phishing attempts to bypass basic spam filters or to appear informal/desperate. The request to contact a new number on WhatsApp, especially when claiming a phone is broken, is a classic social engineering lure designed to move the conversation off the traceable SMS platform to a private channel where the scammer can more easily manipulate the victim. While it mimics a personal emergency (a benign context), the suspicious formatting and the immediate redirection to an external, unverified contact method strongly suggest an attempt to trick the recipient.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
