{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00906", "condition": "perturbed", "attack_class": "character", "seed": 0, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing/scamming behavior. The message contains numerous spelling and grammatical errors (\"so tahat,\" \"ar,\" \"th3,\" \"investm ent,\" \"gropu\"), which is a common tactic in phishing attempts to appear less polished or to bypass basic filters. It uses an unusual and suspicious link format (`whatsapp://chat/join/?code=...`) which is being used to direct the user to a specific chat group, often associated with investment scams. Furthermore, the message promises extremely high, unrealistic returns (\"1K-10K usd a day\") related to \"Bitcoin OF AMERICA\" and an \"investment plan,\" which is a classic hallmark of investment fraud or pump-and-dump schemes. The combination of urgency (implied by the invitation) and high-reward promises via a suspicious link strongly points to a malicious intent.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00907", "condition": "perturbed", "attack_class": "character", "seed": 0, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing.\n1. **Urgency/Threat:** The message uses alarming language (\"We noticed unusual activities on your account\") to create a sense of immediate panic, pressuring the recipient to act quickly without thinking.\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The URL provided, \"WellsfargoVerify.com/act,\" is highly suspicious. While it attempts to mimic the Wells Fargo brand, the domain name is not the official Wells Fargo domain (which would typically be wellsfargo.com). The use of \"Verify.com\" is a common tactic in phishing to appear legitimate.\n3. **Request for Action/Verification:** The message demands the user click a link to \"confirm the activities\" and \"make it secure,\" which is a classic phishing technique designed to steal login credentials or personal information.\n4. **Typographical Errors/Formatting:** The message contains unusual spacing (\"y0ur,\" \"acti vities\"), which is often a sign of automated or hastily constructed phishing attempts.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00909", "condition": "perturbed", "attack_class": "character", "seed": 0, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. The message uses an abbreviated, somewhat unprofessional format (\"FRM:\", \"MSG:flm-\"). It creates a sense of urgency and concern by stating the item \"will be returned to sender\" and that \"We have made several attempts to reach you.\" Most critically, it contains a suspicious link (`https://jf245-fedex.me/y.jDxio?37588372`). The domain name (`jf245-fedex.me`) is not the official FedEx domain, indicating brand impersonation designed to trick the recipient into clicking a malicious link, likely to steal personal or payment information.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00910", "condition": "perturbed", "attack_class": "character", "seed": 0, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is very brief and contains abbreviations (\"Mos tly\" likely means \"Mostly,\" \"lyk\" means \"like,\" \"footbl\" means \"football,\" \"crckt\" means \"cricket\"). It appears to be a casual, conversational message discussing sports interests. There are no indicators of urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00911", "condition": "perturbed", "attack_class": "character", "seed": 0, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing.\n1. **Urgency/Threat:** It creates a sense of immediate crisis (\"All funds are on hold due to multiple fraudulent direct deposits\").\n2. **Suspicious Links/Typosquatting:** The URL provided, `Https://www.UnionBank.Org_@nx.tn/EjmCx`, is highly suspicious. It uses non-standard characters (`_@`) and an unusual top-level domain (`.tn`), which is not the official UnionBank domain. The misspelling of \"Attention\" (`ATTENTI0N`) and other words (e.g., \"direact,\" \"wil1\") are common tactics used by phishers to bypass basic filters.\n3. **Request for Action/Personal Info:** It directs the user to click a link to \"remove hold,\" which is a classic tactic to trick users into entering login credentials or personal information on a fake site.\n4. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates a known financial institution (UnionBank).\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00912", "condition": "perturbed", "attack_class": "character", "seed": 0, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is a very short, informal text message that appears to be a personal communication regarding a meeting time (\"I'll be there before 4\"). There are no suspicious cues such as urgency, suspicious links, brand impersonation, or requests for personal information or payment. The language is conversational and benign.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-00913", "condition": "perturbed", "attack_class": "character", "seed": 0, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of a scam, classifying it as smishing (or in this case, a romance/dating scam delivered via SMS).\n\nEvidence:\n1. **Suspicious Content/Tone:** The message is unsolicited, highly suggestive, and attempts to lure the recipient into a relationship or interaction based on a fabricated premise (a wealthy friend looking for a husband). This is a classic setup for romance scams.\n2. **Typographical Errors/Poor Grammar:** The message is riddled with numerous spelling and grammatical errors (\"n1ce,\" \"wit h,\" \"financ ial,\" \"m@rry,\" \"yars,\" \"ocld,\" \"txhe,\" \"tdhe,\" \"Wi5h\"). Scammers often use poor grammar to appear less professional or to bypass basic spam filters.\n3. **Call to Action with a Link:** It directs the recipient to click a link (`https://wa.me/13213360655`). While this specific link is a WhatsApp link (not a typical credential-harvesting phishing site), the intent is to move the conversation off the SMS platform into a private chat where the scammer can build trust and eventually request money or personal information.\n\nThe combination of unsolicited, highly personal/romantic solicitation, poor quality writing, and a directive to click a link strongly points to a malicious or deceptive intent.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
