{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01340", "condition": "perturbed", "attack_class": "character", "seed": 0, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message appears to be a promotional or informational SMS from a telecommunications provider (implied by mentions of \"Amigo Sin L1mite,\" \"Telcel,\" and roaming/data rates). It provides details about international roaming plans and a technical advisory regarding VOLTE compatibility for US travel.\n\nEvidence for classification:\n1. **Brand Impersonation/Context:** It references specific telecommunication services and providers (Telcel, Amigo Sin L1mite), suggesting it originates from a legitimate service provider.\n2. **Content:** The content is informational (pricing, technical requirements) rather than an immediate demand for sensitive data or payment under duress.\n3. **Suspicious Cues:** While it contains a shortened link (`bit.ly/3DUDsJi`), this is common in legitimate marketing/informational SMS campaigns. The message itself is detailed and contextually relevant to a mobile service user. There is no overt sense of extreme urgency or immediate threat typical of phishing.\n\nDespite the presence of a link, the overall tone and content strongly suggest a legitimate service update or marketing communication from the carrier, rather than a deceptive attempt to steal credentials.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01341", "condition": "perturbed", "attack_class": "character", "seed": 0, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message appears to be a casual inquiry about a property (\"1149 aHrwich Dr\") and proposes a discussion about a \"lump sum deal.\" The language is informal (\"Lmk!\", \"Aman da\"). There are no immediate, high-risk indicators typically associated with smishing, such as urgent threats, requests for login credentials, or suspicious external links. The slight misspelling/leetspeak (\"aHrwich,\" \"cal1\") is minor and could be due to casual texting rather than a malicious attempt. It reads more like a legitimate, albeit informal, communication between parties interested in a real estate transaction.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01342", "condition": "perturbed", "attack_class": "character", "seed": 0, "true_label": "ham", "pred": "ham", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS message is very short and informal. It contains no links, no requests for personal information or payment, and does not exhibit any signs of urgency or brand impersonation typically associated with smishing attacks. It appears to be a casual, personal message.\n\nFINAL: ham"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01343", "condition": "perturbed", "attack_class": "character", "seed": 0, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The SMS exhibits several strong indicators of smishing. Key suspicious cues include:\n1. **Urgency/Threat:** The message claims the card has been \"Temporarily Suspended Due to a Un recognized Transaction Attempt,\" creating immediate panic to prompt action.\n2. **Suspicious Link:** The URL provided (`http://22-sc.com/ouBfl`) is highly suspicious. It does not resemble the official domain of Bank of America and uses unusual characters and structure, typical of phishing sites.\n3. **Impersonation/Formatting:** While it mentions \"Bank Of America,\" the message uses poor grammar (\"Plea se Review Rec3nt Transactions a7\") and unusual character substitutions (e.g., 'C@rd', 'Rec3nt'), which are common tactics used by scammers to bypass basic filters while mimicking a legitimate source.\n4. **Request for Action:** It directs the user to click a link to \"Review Recent Transactions,\" which is the primary goal of smishing—to trick the user into entering credentials or personal data on a fake site.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
{"model": "google/gemma-4-E4B-it", "template": "t3_neutral_explanation", "id": "msg-01344", "condition": "perturbed", "attack_class": "character", "seed": 0, "true_label": "smishing", "pred": "smishing", "parse_mode": "strict", "raw": "The message exhibits several strong indicators of smishing.\n1. **Brand Impersonation:** It impersonates the Internal Revenue Service (IRS), a common tactic in phishing attacks.\n2. **Urgency/Incentive:** It uses the lure of a \"tax refund\" to entice the recipient into acting quickly.\n3. **Suspicious Link:** The provided URL, `https://irs.gov.direct-paying.com`, is highly suspicious. While it contains \"irs.gov,\" the addition of \"direct-paying.com\" makes it a clear attempt to mimic an official domain while routing the user to a malicious or unofficial site.\n4. **Request for Personal Information:** The message explicitly asks the user to \"confirm your information,\" which is the primary goal of phishing scams.\n5. **Poor Grammar/Typos:** The message contains numerous spelling and grammatical errors (\"jcannot,\" \"y0ur,\" \"7he,\" \"mis sion,\" \"hel p,\" \"7axpayers,\" \"obli gations,\" \"h1ghest qual1ty\"), which is characteristic of hastily constructed scam messages.\n\nFINAL: smishing"}
